/* Built by tools/build-split.py from data.js — do not edit. e2, SDLs 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 29 */
(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2.94574de12c.js"]={"21":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Viewed from the front, how far does the primary intestinal loop normally rotate in total?","choices":{"A":"About 90 degrees clockwise","B":"About 180 degrees clockwise","C":"About 90 degrees counterclockwise","D":"About 270 degrees counterclockwise","E":"About 360 degrees counterclockwise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The loop rotates about 270 degrees counterclockwise: about 90 degrees during herniation and the remaining 180 degrees during return to the abdomen. Ninety degrees counterclockwise is only the herniation phase, and clockwise rotation is abnormal. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise during the 4th to 6th weeks, so the left vagus comes to lie anteriorly (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the jejunum and ileum coil during rotation, whereas the large intestine lengthens without coiling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms the axis of rotation of the primary intestinal loop?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Vitelline duct and yolk stalk","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Inferior mesenteric artery","E":"Left umbilical vein remnant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The superior mesenteric artery forms the axis about which the primary intestinal loop rotates. The celiac trunk and inferior mesenteric artery supply the foregut and hindgut, and the vitelline duct connects the loop apex to the yolk sac. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic limb of the primary loop forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, and part of the ileum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dorsal mesentery twists around the origin of this artery as the caudal limb moves to the right (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do the herniated intestinal loops begin to return to the abdominal cavity?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Sixth week","C":"Eighth week","D":"Sixteenth week","E":"Tenth week"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Herniated loops begin to return during the 10th week, aided by regression of the mesonephros, slower liver growth, and abdominal cavity expansion. Herniation begins in the 6th week, and the return is finished by about the 12th week. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal jejunum returns first and settles on the left side (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud returns last and then descends into the right iliac fossa (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Which covering surrounds the herniated viscera in an omphalocele?","choices":{"A":"Skin","B":"Dura","C":"Amnion","D":"Chorion","E":"Pleura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In omphalocele, the viscera herniate through an enlarged umbilical ring and are covered by a membrane of amnion and peritoneum separated by Wharton's jelly, which gives the sac a smooth, spherical look on ultrasound. Skin covers an umbilical hernia, and gastroschisis has no covering membrane. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the omphalocele sac as peritoneum plus amnion, light gray and shiny (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele occurs in about 2.5 per 10,000 births with about 25% mortality (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do midgut loops normally enter the umbilical cord as physiologic herniation?","choices":{"A":"Third week","B":"Fourth week","C":"Twelfth week","D":"Sixth week","E":"Twentieth week"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rapid elongation of the loop and growth of the liver make the abdomen temporarily too small, so loops enter the extraembryonic cavity of the cord during the 6th week. They return beginning in the 10th week. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: coiling of the small intestinal loops and formation of the cecum occur while the gut is herniated (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: physiologic herniation persists until about the 10th week, so bowel in the cord base before then is normal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the abdominal wall defect of gastroschisis usually located?","choices":{"A":"Through the enlarged umbilical ring itself","B":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the right","C":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the left side","D":"In the upper epigastric midline above it","E":"Just above the pubic symphysis in midline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastroschisis occurs lateral to the umbilicus, usually on the right, from abnormal body wall closure around the connecting stalk. Herniation through the umbilical ring itself is omphalocele. A suprapubic defect suggests bladder exstrophy. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is a lateral fold closure defect, while bladder exstrophy is a caudal fold defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis is increasing in frequency, especially in babies of mothers under 20 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Meckel diverticulum results from persistence of which embryonic structure?","choices":{"A":"Urachal remnant","B":"Allantoic stalk","C":"Vitelline duct","D":"Cloacal membrane","E":"Umbilical vein"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A small persisting portion of the vitelline duct forms an outpocketing of the ileum, Meckel diverticulum. A persisting urachus comes from the allantois and drains urine. The cloacal membrane and umbilical vein are not related to the ileum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: the diverticulum usually causes no symptoms in adults (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Along which border of the ileum does Meckel diverticulum arise?","choices":{"A":"Antimesenteric border","B":"Mesenteric border","C":"Duodenojejunal border","D":"Retroperitoneal border","E":"Ileocecal valve border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meckel diverticulum lies on the antimesenteric border of the ileum, about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve. It does not arise from the mesenteric border, the duodenojejunal flexure, or the ileocecal valve itself. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rule of 2s lists 2 feet from the ileocecal valve and 2 inches long (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum is twice as likely in males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"What anomaly results when the omphalomesenteric duct stays patent along its entire length?","choices":{"A":"Vitelline cyst","B":"Urachal sinus tract","C":"Duplication cyst","D":"Omphalocele sac","E":"Vitelline fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A duct that stays patent throughout forms a vitelline, or umbilical, fistula connecting the ileal lumen with the umbilicus, so feces may drain at the umbilicus. A vitelline cyst forms when only the middle part persists between two fibrous cords. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: umbilical drainage of feces points to a patent omphalomesenteric duct, whereas urine drainage points to a patent urachus (Langman Ch. 16). COMLEX: the vitelline duct connects the apex of the primary loop with the yolk sac (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"In which intestinal segment do most atresias and stenoses occur?","choices":{"A":"Jejunum","B":"Distal ileum","C":"Appendix","D":"Duodenum","E":"Colon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Most atresias and stenoses occur in the duodenum, the fewest occur in the colon, and the jejunum and ileum have equal numbers (about 1 in 1500 births). The appendix is not a usual atresia site. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia is associated with Down syndrome and shows a double bubble on x-ray (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: atresias account for 95% of these defects and stenoses for only 5% (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism most likely causes atresia of the upper duodenum, just distal to the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Lack of recanalization","B":"Vascular accident","C":"Midgut volvulus","D":"Amniotic fluid injury","E":"Persistent vitelline cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Upper duodenal atresias are probably due to failure of the solid duodenal lumen to recanalize. Distal to the duodenum, atresias have been attributed to vascular accidents, which can follow volvulus, malrotation, or abdominal wall defects. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting and distension within the first 1 to 2 days of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: the duodenal lumen is temporarily obliterated by epithelial proliferation before recanalizing (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Misexpression of which gene families supports a non-vascular, differentiation-based cause of intestinal atresia?","choices":{"A":"RET and GDNF family receptor genes","B":"HOX genes and FGF family genes","C":"SHH and patched receptor genes","D":"PAX and SOX transcription factor genes","E":"WNT and BMP signaling pathway genes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Misexpression of HOX genes and of FGF family ligands and receptors disrupts epithelial-mesenchymal signaling and causes gut atresias, showing that faulty gut differentiation, not only vascular accidents, can produce these defects. RET mutations cause aganglionic megacolon. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RET mutations impair neural crest migration and cause Hirschsprung disease (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: in 50% of atresias a region of bowel is lost, and in 20% a fibrous cord remains (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which germ layer forms the epithelium lining the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Ectoderm","B":"Neural crest","C":"Somatic layer","D":"Paraxial layer","E":"Endoderm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The epithelium of the digestive system and the parenchyma of its derivatives originate in endoderm. Connective tissue, muscle, and peritoneal components come from mesoderm. Ectoderm forms only the caudal anal canal. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudal third of the anal canal is ectodermal and is supplied by the inferior rectal artery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: gut differentiation depends on reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal interactions (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which embryonic tissue forms the smooth muscle of the small intestinal wall?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm","B":"Gut tube endoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Paraxial mesoderm","E":"Somatic body wall mesoderm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscular and connective tissue components of the gut come from mesoderm, specifically the splanchnic (visceral) layer surrounding the endodermal tube. Endoderm forms the epithelium. Somatic and paraxial mesoderm form body wall and somite derivatives. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endodermal sonic hedgehog induces HOX gene expression in the surrounding gut mesoderm (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: visceral peritoneum covers the gut while parietal peritoneum lines the body wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure is the most distal derivative of the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Distal third of the transverse colon and flexure","B":"Proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon","C":"Descending colon at the splenic flexure region","D":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve junction","E":"Upper anal canal above the pectinate line"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon with the distal third. The distal third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, and the upper anal canal are hindgut. The terminal ileum is proximal to the end. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut receives vagal parasympathetic innervation and lies at the L1 vertebral level of its artery (First Aid Gastrointestinal, blood supply and innervation). COMLEX: the caudal limb of the primary loop forms the lower ileum, cecum, appendix, and ascending colon (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-day-old boy has sudden bilious vomiting. An upper GI series shows the small intestinal loops lying in the right abdomen, and a contrast enema places the cecum and colon in the left abdomen. The ileum enters the cecum from the right. Which developmental error best explains this anatomy?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of the primary loop","B":"Complete rotation with failed colonic fixation","C":"Failure of the loops to leave the abdomen","D":"Persistent attachment of the omphalomesenteric duct","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When rotation amounts to only 90 degrees, the colon and cecum return first and settle on the left, and later loops move to the right, giving a left-sided colon. Clockwise (reversed) rotation places the transverse colon behind the duodenum. Failed fixation leaves a mobile colon in its normal place. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: malrotation leaves the small bowel clumped on the right and the colon on the left, with fibrous Ladd bands (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malrotation). COMLEX: malrotation predisposes to volvulus with compromised blood supply (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl with known intestinal malrotation abruptly develops bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and bloody stools. Heart rate is 190/min, and capillary refill is 4 seconds. Which complication most likely threatens her bowel?","choices":{"A":"Telescoping of ileum into the cecum","B":"Twisting that compromises blood flow","C":"Spasm of an aganglionic colonic segment","D":"Ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa","E":"Plugging of the ileum by thick meconium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Malrotation may result in twisting of the intestine (volvulus) and a compromised blood supply, which explains bloody stools and shock. Intussusception, aganglionosis, Meckel ulceration, and meconium ileus cause obstruction or bleeding by other mechanisms and are not the typical malrotation emergency. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: midgut volvulus is more common in infants, whereas sigmoid volvulus is more common in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: in gastroschisis, volvulus with compromised blood supply may kill large regions of bowel (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman undergoes CT for flank pain. The radiologist notes that her transverse colon passes posterior to the duodenum and lies behind the superior mesenteric artery. Which embryologic event best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","B":"Counterclockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","C":"Failed posterior attachment of the ascending colon","D":"Arrested descent of the cecal bud from the liver","E":"Return of the colon before the proximal jejunum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reversed rotation occurs when the primary loop rotates 90 degrees clockwise, so the transverse colon passes behind the duodenum and the superior mesenteric artery. Counterclockwise rotation of only 90 degrees produces a left-sided colon. Failed attachment produces a mobile colon. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after normal rotation, the transverse colon passes in front of the duodenum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud lies temporarily in the right upper quadrant below the liver before descending (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a routine ultrasound at 20 weeks of gestation. It shows a smooth, membrane-bound midline sac containing bowel and liver at the cord insertion. Which associated finding should next be sought?","choices":{"A":"Renal agenesis","B":"Cardiac anomaly","C":"Hip dysplasia","D":"Cleft palate","E":"Clubfoot deformity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is omphalocele, which is associated with cardiac anomalies in about 50%, neural tube defects in about 40%, and chromosomal abnormalities in about 15% of live-born infants. The other anomalies are not the classic associations. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: omphalocele is associated with trisomies 13 and 18 and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele arises when bowel fails to return from physiologic herniation during weeks 6 to 10 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn boy is delivered to an 18-year-old mother. Thickened, uncovered bowel loops protrude through a small defect just to the right of a normal umbilical cord. No other anomalies are found. Which developmental error most likely caused this defect?","choices":{"A":"Failure of loops to return from the umbilical cord","B":"Persistence of a patent omphalomesenteric duct","C":"Delayed closure of the umbilical ring after birth","D":"Abnormal closure of the body wall around the stalk","E":"Failed recanalization of the solid gut lumen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastroschisis is most likely due to abnormal closure of the body wall around the connecting stalk. The bowel is exposed to amniotic fluid and may be damaged. Failed return of herniated loops causes omphalocele, and delayed umbilical ring closure causes an umbilical hernia covered by skin. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is not commonly associated with chromosomal abnormalities and has a good prognosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis occurs in about 1 per 10,000 births (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman has an early ultrasound at 9 weeks of gestation. Several bowel loops are seen within the base of the umbilical cord. The fetal heart, spine, and limbs appear normal. Which interpretation is most appropriate for this normal-appearing fetus?","choices":{"A":"Omphalocele with amnion covering","B":"Gastroschisis with uncovered loops","C":"Physiologic midgut herniation","D":"Congenital umbilical hernia","E":"Vitelline cyst at the umbilicus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Midgut loops normally herniate into the cord from the 6th week and begin returning in the 10th week, so bowel in the cord at 9 weeks is expected. Omphalocele and gastroschisis are diagnosed later in gestation. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a congenital umbilical hernia is covered by skin and often closes spontaneously (First Aid Gastrointestinal, congenital umbilical hernia). COMLEX: herniation occurs because liver growth makes the abdomen temporarily too small (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has painless passage of a large amount of maroon blood per rectum. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL (reference range: 10.5–13.5 g/dL). A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan shows a focus of uptake in the right lower quadrant. What most likely caused his bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Acid secretion by ectopic gastric mucosa","B":"Ischemia from an ileocolic intussusception","C":"Angiodysplasia within the cecal mucosal wall","D":"Hyperplasia of the ileal lymphoid follicles","E":"Erosion by a swallowed coin or button battery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The scan detects heterotopic gastric mucosa in an ileal diverticulum. Acid from this mucosa ulcerates adjacent ileal mucosa, causing bleeding or perforation. Intussusception causes pain, angiodysplasia is a disease of older adults, and lymphoid hyperplasia does not take up pertechnetate. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pertechnetate scan is called a Meckel scan because it detects heterotopic gastric mucosa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: heterotopic pancreatic tissue in the diverticulum can also cause ulceration (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has colicky pain, bilious vomiting, and distension. At surgery, a cyst lies between the ileum and the umbilicus, attached at both ends by fibrous cords, and a loop of ileum is wrapped around one cord. Which mechanism caused her obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal ulceration with free perforation","B":"Volvulus of loops on the vitelline ligaments","C":"Telescoping of the ileum into the ascending colon","D":"Aganglionic spasm of a distal colonic segment","E":"Inspissated meconium filling the ileal lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When both ends of the duct become fibrous cords and the middle forms a vitelline cyst, the cords cross the peritoneal cavity, so loops may twist around them, causing obstruction, strangulation, or volvulus. Ulceration is the bleeding mechanism of ectopic mucosa. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: a vitelline cyst is also called an enterocystoma (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has right lower quadrant pain. At laparoscopy, the appendix is normal, but an inflamed 5-cm outpouching arises from the ileum about 50 cm from the ileocecal valve. Pathology shows mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis propria in its wall. How is this outpouching best classified?","choices":{"A":"Duplication cyst","B":"False diverticulum","C":"Pulsion pseudodiverticulum","D":"Pancreatic rest","E":"True diverticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A persistent vitelline duct remnant contains all layers of the bowel wall, so it is a true diverticulum. False diverticula contain only mucosa and submucosa. Duplication cysts are separate tubular or cystic structures, most often near the ileum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Meckel diverticulum). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum commonly presents in the first 2 years of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: about 2% of people have a Meckel diverticulum (lecture notes; Langman gives 2% to 4%).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn girl has bilious vomiting and distension on day 1. Her mother used cocaine during pregnancy. Abdominal x-ray shows several dilated proximal loops and a gasless colon. At surgery, the proximal jejunum ends blindly, separated from distal bowel by a V-shaped mesenteric gap. Which mechanism best explains this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Ischemic necrosis of a fetal intestinal segment","B":"Failure of solid-cord recanalization of the lumen","C":"Traction by a persistent vitelline fibrous cord","D":"Exposure of loops to amniotic fluid through a defect","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees of turning"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Atresias distal to the duodenum have been attributed to vascular accidents that compromise blood flow and cause necrosis and loss of a bowel segment. Vasoconstrictive drugs raise this risk. Failure of recanalization is the mechanism for upper duodenal atresia. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35); First Aid Gastrointestinal (intestinal atresia). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal and ileal atresia are associated with cystic fibrosis and gastroschisis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: maternal tobacco smoking is a risk factor for jejunal and ileal atresia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A preterm newborn with low birth weight has bilious vomiting. At surgery, the proximal jejunum ends blindly, and the short distal small intestine is coiled around a thin mesenteric remnant. Which defect is present?","choices":{"A":"Membranous duodenal web","B":"Annular pancreas","C":"Apple peel atresia","D":"Multiple segmental atresias","E":"Midgut duplication cyst"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Apple peel atresia accounts for about 10% of atresias. It occurs in the proximal jejunum, and the shortened distal intestine is coiled around a remnant of the mesentery. Membranous webs and annular pancreas obstruct the duodenum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: effects of an atresia depend on how much bowel is damaged and where it lies (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a thin diaphragm separating larger and smaller pieces of bowel accounts for about 20% of atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn boy has several separate ileal atresias found at surgery. The mesenteric vessels are intact, there was no malrotation or volvulus, and an older sister had a similar defect. Which mechanism most likely explains his atresias?","choices":{"A":"Intrauterine volvulus with segment infarction","B":"Failed recanalization of the solid lumen","C":"Maternal vasoconstrictive drug exposure","D":"Faulty gene-directed gut differentiation","E":"Amniotic injury through an abdominal wall defect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"New evidence suggests that problems with gut differentiation, including misexpression of developmental genes and growth factor receptors, can cause atresias without a vascular accident. Intact vessels and no volvulus argue against ischemia, and recanalization failure applies to the upper duodenum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stenoses and multiple atresias each account for about 5% of these defects (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about 33% of intestinal duplications are associated with other defects such as atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain. CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the superior mesenteric artery just beyond its origin. Which bowel segment is most likely to be spared from ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Proximal jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Descending colon","D":"Cecum and appendix","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The descending colon is a hindgut derivative supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. The jejunum, ileum, cecum, and ascending colon are midgut derivatives supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the hindgut extends from the distal third of the transverse colon to the anal canal above the pectinate line (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the left third of the transverse colon is a hindgut derivative (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has severe celiac trunk stenosis, but her distal duodenum stays well perfused. Which landmark marks the embryologic boundary between foregut and midgut territory in her duodenum?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric sphincter","B":"Minor duodenal papilla","C":"Duodenojejunal flexure","D":"First-part duodenal bulb","E":"Major duodenal papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The foregut includes the duodenum to the level of the liver and ventral pancreatic buds (major papilla), and the midgut begins just distal to the entrance of the bile duct. The duodenum caudal to the major papilla is therefore midgut. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the foregut extends from the esophagus to the duodenum at the level of the ampulla of Vater (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the liver, pancreas, and biliary apparatus grow out of the upper duodenal endoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn with holoprosencephaly has a loss-of-function mutation in a secreted signaling gene, and imaging shows gut malformations as well. In the developing gut, which signal from the endoderm patterns the surrounding mesoderm along the craniocaudal axis?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"Bone morphogenetic protein 4","D":"Sonic hedgehog","E":"Fibroblast growth factor 10"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gut endoderm secretes sonic hedgehog, which induces HOX genes in the surrounding mesoderm that then regulate the craniocaudal organization of the gut. A retinoic acid gradient specifies gut regions but is not the endodermal signal to mesoderm. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HOX genes in gut mesoderm, induced by endodermal signaling, regulate craniocaudal organization of the gut and its derivatives (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: an RA gradient causes region-specific transcription factors along the gut tube (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman is at 11 weeks of gestation, and her fetus is undergoing normal midgut return. Where does the cecal bud lie just after it reenters the abdomen?","choices":{"A":"Right upper quadrant","B":"Left upper quadrant","C":"Right lower quadrant","D":"Left lower quadrant","E":"Pelvic cavity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cecal bud is the last part to reenter and lies temporarily in the right upper quadrant below the right lobe of the liver. It then descends into the right iliac fossa. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops, Fig. 15.27). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in about 50% of people the appendix is retrocecal or retrocolic because it develops during colonic descent (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the appendix forms from the distal end of the cecal bud (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn has a midline membrane-covered umbilical sac, macroglossia, and hypoglycemia. Which syndrome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Down syndrome","B":"Turner syndrome","C":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","D":"DiGeorge syndrome","E":"Marfan syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Omphalocele is associated with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome and trisomies 13 and 18. Macroglossia and neonatal hypoglycemia point to Beckwith-Wiedemann. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (ventral wall defects); Langman Ch. 15 (Body wall defects). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 15% of live-born infants with omphalocele have chromosomal abnormalities (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: omphalocele reflects failed return of physiologically herniated loops, whereas gastroschisis reflects abnormal body wall closure around the connecting stalk (lecture notes; Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man asks how common his incidentally found ileal diverticulum is. What fraction of people have one?","choices":{"A":"About 0.1%","B":"About 10%","C":"About 25%","D":"About 40%","E":"About 2%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 2% of people have a persistent proximal vitelline duct forming an ileal diverticulum, matching First Aid's rule of 2s; Langman gives a range of 2% to 4%. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum may contain two types of ectopic epithelium, gastric and pancreatic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum can present with painless hematochezia or melena (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has bilious vomiting from jejunal atresia. Which finding is expected on her abdominal x-ray?","choices":{"A":"Triple bubble sign","B":"Double bubble sign","C":"String sign","D":"Bird beak sign","E":"Apple core lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jejunal and ileal atresia may show a triple bubble (dilated stomach, duodenum, and proximal jejunum) with a gasless colon. A double bubble suggests duodenal atresia. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (intestinal atresia); Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in jejunal and ileal atresia, ischemic necrosis leads to segmental resorption and discontinuous bowel (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: a narrowing with a thin diaphragm is one of the common atresia patterns (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn has gut segments with abnormal regional identity. Which signal specifies gut regions along the craniocaudal axis before epithelial-mesenchymal interactions?","choices":{"A":"Sonic hedgehog gradient","B":"Retinoic acid gradient","C":"BMP4 gradient","D":"FGF10 gradient","E":"Indian hedgehog gradient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Langman states that regions of the gut tube such as the esophagus, stomach, and duodenum are specified by a retinoic acid gradient that induces region-specific transcription factors. Sonic hedgehog, BMP4, FGF10, and Indian hedgehog act later in reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal signaling rather than setting craniocaudal regional identity. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Summary). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: differentiation of gut derivatives depends on reciprocal endoderm-mesoderm interactions (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the gut tube extends from the oropharyngeal membrane to the cloacal membrane (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has recurrent right-sided abdominal pain. CT shows small bowel loops trapped behind a poorly attached ascending colon. Which defect is present?","choices":{"A":"Paraduodenal hernia","B":"Umbilical hernia","C":"Hiatal hernia","D":"Retrocolic hernia","E":"Inguinal hernia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Incomplete attachment of the ascending colon to the posterior body wall may leave retrocolic pockets, and small intestine trapped behind the ascending colon forms a retrocolic hernia. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a mobile ascending colon that fails to attach may twist, causing volvulus of the cecum and colon (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Toldt fascia lies between peritoneal layers where colon attaches to the posterior wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"}],"22":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery supplies the cecum and ascending colon?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Splenic artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cecum and ascending colon are midgut derivatives and are supplied by the superior mesenteric artery through its ileocolic and right colic branches. The celiac trunk and splenic artery supply foregut structures, and the internal iliac and median sacral arteries supply pelvic structures rather than midgut colon. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut ends where the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon meet the distal third (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic branches serve the colonic part of this territory (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Where along the transverse colon does midgut territory end and hindgut territory begin?","choices":{"A":"Junction of its proximal two-thirds and distal third","B":"Junction of its proximal third and distal two-thirds","C":"Midpoint between the two colic flexures","D":"Hepatic flexure at the right colic angle","E":"Splenic flexure at the left colic angle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The midgut extends to the junction of the proximal two-thirds and distal third of the transverse colon; the distal third belongs to the hindgut. The proximal-third split reverses the proportions, the midpoint is not the boundary, the hepatic flexure lies well within midgut territory, and the boundary lies just before the splenic flexure rather than at it. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: this boundary is why the splenic flexure region is a watershed between superior and inferior mesenteric supply (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI blood supply). COMLEX: hindgut derivatives continue caudally to the pectinate line of the anal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following large intestine structures develops from the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Vermiform appendix","C":"Descending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Upper anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut forms the cecum, vermiform appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The sigmoid colon, descending colon, rectum, and upper anal canal all come from the hindgut and receive inferior mesenteric supply. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix forms from the distal end of the cecal bud (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about half of appendices lie retrocecal or retrocolic because the appendix grows during colonic descent (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which connective tissue layer anchors the ascending and descending colon to the posterior abdominal wall?","choices":{"A":"Scarpa fascia","B":"Camper fascia","C":"Toldt fascia","D":"Denonvilliers fascia","E":"Transversalis fascia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the ascending and descending colon settle against the posterior wall, their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum through Toldt fascia. Scarpa and Camper fascia are layers of the anterior abdominal subcutaneous tissue, Denonvilliers fascia lies between rectum and prostate, and transversalis fascia lines the inner abdominal wall. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgeons mobilize the colon along the white line of Toldt during colectomy (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the mesentery of the sigmoid colon persists, so this segment stays intraperitoneal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Once their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum, the ascending and descending colon are classified as which type of structure?","choices":{"A":"Primarily retroperitoneal","B":"Intraperitoneal on a mesentery","C":"Infraperitoneal in the pelvis","D":"Extraperitoneal in the wall","E":"Secondarily retroperitoneal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The colon first hangs on a dorsal mesentery and becomes secondarily retroperitoneal after fusion with the posterior wall. Primary retroperitoneal organs, such as the kidneys, develop without a mesentery; intraperitoneal describes segments that keep a mesentery, such as the transverse and sigmoid colon; infraperitoneal and extraperitoneal describe pelvic or body wall positions. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum except its first part and the pancreas except its tail are also secondarily retroperitoneal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, retroperitoneal structures). COMLEX: the transverse mesocolon fuses with the greater omentum rather than the posterior wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"After midgut rotation, which process carries the cecum from the right upper quadrant to the right lower quadrant?","choices":{"A":"Rotation of the stomach","B":"Elongation of the colon","C":"Enlargement of the liver","D":"Regression of the mesonephros","E":"Fusion of the mesocolon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cecal bud lies first in the right upper quadrant below the liver, and lengthening of the colon carries the cecum and appendix caudally into the right iliac fossa. Gastric rotation shapes the foregut, liver growth and mesonephric regression influence the earlier return of herniated loops, and mesocolic fusion fixes the colon after it is in place. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cecal bud is the last part of the herniated loop to reenter the abdomen (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a cecum that stays high produces a subhepatic appendix with atypical right upper quadrant pain (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms between the anal and urogenital openings after the cloaca is divided?","choices":{"A":"Genital tubercle","B":"Urogenital fold","C":"Anal column","D":"Perineal body","E":"Labioscrotal swelling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When the cloacal membrane ruptures, the tip of the dividing mesodermal wedge remains between the anal and urogenital openings as the perineal body (Langman-only detail; not in SDL 22 lecture notes). The genital tubercle, urogenital folds, and labioscrotal swellings are external genital primordia, and anal columns are mucosal folds of the upper anal canal. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; tip of urorectal septum → perineal body, Figs. 15.36–15.37); SDL 22 lecture notes (urorectal septum context). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the perineal body anchors the perineal muscles and can be torn during vaginal delivery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the allantois enters the anterior cloaca and the hindgut enters the posterior cloaca (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"At the end of which week does the anal membrane (posterior cloacal membrane) break down?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Seventh week","C":"Fifth week","D":"Tenth week","E":"Twelfth week"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The anal membrane (the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane) degenerates at the end of the seventh week, opening communication between the anal pit and the hindgut. The fourth and fifth weeks are too early because the cloaca is still being partitioned, and the tenth and twelfth weeks correspond to return of the herniated midgut. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (imperforate anus / anal membrane); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectourethral and rectovaginal fistulas occur in about 1 in 5,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: these defects likely reflect misexpression of genes during epithelial–mesenchymal signaling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"If the urorectal septum fails to extend far enough caudally, how is the hindgut opening displaced?","choices":{"A":"Posterior shift of the hindgut opening","B":"Lateral shift of the hindgut opening","C":"Cranial shift of the hindgut opening","D":"Duplication of the hindgut opening","E":"Anterior shift of the hindgut opening"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A small cloaca or a urorectal septum that stops short causes the hindgut opening to shift anteriorly, so it can empty into the urethra, vagina, or perineum. Posterior, lateral, and cranial displacement are not described, and duplication is a separate anomaly unrelated to septal growth. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an anterior opening explains meconium in the urine of a boy with a rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: some variants end in rectal atresia or a fibrous cord to the perineum (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which epithelium lines the anal canal just below the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","B":"Transitional epithelium","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the ectodermal mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium that continues to the perianal skin. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airways, transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract, simple cuboidal epithelium lines ducts and tubules, and stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger gland ducts. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line are typically squamous cell carcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: an anal transitional zone lies at the junction of the two epithelial types (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of which vessel?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric artery","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Internal pudendal artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Left colic artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superior rectal artery continues the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, and supplies the anal canal above the pectinate line. The internal iliac and internal pudendal arteries supply the region below the line, the median sacral artery is a small midline branch of the aorta, and the left colic artery is a separate, more proximal branch. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids above the line drain to the superior rectal vein and portal system (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: sigmoidal branches arise from the same parent vessel between the left colic and terminal branch (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal landmark lies immediately above the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Anal verge","B":"Perianal skin","C":"Intersphincteric groove","D":"Anal columns","E":"Levator ani"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pectinate line, which marks the junction of endodermal and ectodermal parts of the anal canal, lies just below the anal columns. The anal verge and perianal skin lie below it at the outer margin, the intersphincteric groove lies between the sphincters below the line, and the levator ani is pelvic floor muscle rather than a mucosal landmark. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral pain fibers serve the canal above the line, and somatic fibers serve it below (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the cranial two-thirds of the anal canal come from the primitive anorectal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Parasympathetic ganglion cells of the enteric nervous system derive from which embryonic cells?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm cells","B":"Paraxial mesoderm cells","C":"Surface ectoderm cells","D":"Neural tube cells","E":"Neural crest cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enteric parasympathetic ganglion cells come from neural crest cells that migrate from the neural folds into the bowel wall. Splanchnic mesoderm forms the gut muscle and connective tissue, paraxial mesoderm forms somites, surface ectoderm forms epidermis, and the neural tube forms the central nervous system. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neural crest derivatives also include adrenal medulla, melanocytes, and Schwann cells (First Aid Embryology, germ layer derivatives). COMLEX: the endothelin-3 and EDNRB pathway also supports these migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"The RET gene that is mutated in congenital megacolon encodes which type of protein?","choices":{"A":"Ligand-gated ion channel","B":"G protein-coupled receptor","C":"Nuclear hormone receptor","D":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","E":"Serine threonine kinase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"RET encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase required for proliferation, survival, and directed migration of enteric neural crest precursors. Ligand-gated channels, G protein-coupled receptors, nuclear hormone receptors, and serine threonine kinases are other signaling classes not encoded by RET. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gain-of-function RET mutations cause MEN 2A and 2B, the opposite of the loss of function in megacolon (First Aid Endocrinology, MEN syndromes). COMLEX: RET is discussed further with neural crest development in Langman Ch. 18.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Which bowel segment is involved in nearly every case of aganglionic megacolon?","choices":{"A":"Rectum","B":"Cecum","C":"Appendix","D":"Jejunum","E":"Ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aganglionosis begins distally and extends proximally for a variable distance, so the rectum is involved in nearly all cases. The cecum and appendix are affected only in long-segment or total colonic disease, and the jejunum and ileum only in rare extended forms. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: suction rectal biopsy is the diagnostic test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: in about 80% the defect extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid, and in about 3% the whole colon is affected (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen. His pulse is 112/min and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum lactate is 4.8 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the first anterior branch of the aorta below the celiac trunk, just past its origin. Which colonic segment is at greatest risk of infarction?","choices":{"A":"Descending colon","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Distal transverse colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The occluded vessel is the superior mesenteric artery, which supplies midgut colon: cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum, and distal third of the transverse colon are hindgut and receive inferior mesenteric supply. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute mesenteric ischemia classically follows embolism from atrial fibrillation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: pain out of proportion to examination is the early bedside clue (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman undergoes open aortic aneurysm repair during which the vessel arising from the anterior aorta at the level of L3 is ligated. Two days later she has left lower quadrant pain and bloody diarrhea. Her pulse is 98/min and blood pressure is 128/74 mm Hg. Colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa from just beyond the left colic flexure to the rectosigmoid. Which other structure normally depends on the same arterial territory?","choices":{"A":"Lower third of the anal canal","B":"Cecum and vermiform appendix","C":"Proximal two-thirds of transverse colon","D":"Upper two-thirds of the anal canal","E":"Terminal ileum and ileocecal valve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ligated vessel is the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, which supplies the distal transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, and upper two-thirds of the anal canal. The lower third of the anal canal is ectodermal and supplied by inferior rectal branches, while the cecum, appendix, proximal transverse colon, and terminal ileum are midgut. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia after aortic surgery reflects loss of inferior mesenteric inflow (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: the left colic, sigmoidal, and superior rectal arteries are the named branches of this vessel (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man is admitted with septic shock, and his blood pressure falls to 78/42 mm Hg for several hours. Two days later he has crampy left upper quadrant pain and passes maroon stool. His pulse is 104/min. Serum lactate is 3.1 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Colonoscopy shows segmental ischemia centered near the left colic flexure. The ischemic region lies at the watershed between the terminal branches of which arteries?","choices":{"A":"Ileocolic and right colic arteries","B":"Middle colic and left colic arteries","C":"Right colic and middle colic arteries","D":"Left colic and sigmoidal arteries","E":"Sigmoidal and superior rectal arteries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The left colic flexure region lies near the midgut–hindgut boundary, where terminal middle colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery meet left colic branches of the inferior mesenteric artery. The ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic pairs lie within midgut territory, and the left colic, sigmoidal, and superior rectal pairs lie within hindgut territory. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction are the classic watershed sites for colonic ischemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: hypotension rather than occlusion is the usual cause of this pattern (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has 12 hours of colicky abdominal pain, distension, and vomiting. She has had two similar milder episodes. Her pulse is 108/min and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. CT shows a dilated cecum displaced into the left upper quadrant with a whirl of mesenteric vessels at its base. Which anatomic feature of her colon most likely allowed this twist?","choices":{"A":"Elongated mesentery of the sigmoid colon","B":"Narrowed lumen at the ileocecal valve","C":"Persistent free mesentery of the ascending colon","D":"Absent ganglion cells in the rectum","E":"Thickened teniae coli of the cecum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Failure of the ascending colon to fuse with the posterior wall leaves a free mesentery, so the mobile cecum and colon can twist around their vascular pedicle and obstruct. An elongated sigmoid mesentery predisposes to sigmoid twisting rather than a displaced cecum, a narrow ileocecal valve does not cause rotation, rectal aganglionosis causes functional obstruction, and teniae do not create mobility. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: volvulus of the cecum and colon can progress to ischemic gangrene (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: volvulus is one of the four major causes of intestinal obstruction, most often in the sigmoid colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — intestinal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with no prior abdominal surgery has intermittent cramping pain and bilious vomiting for 2 days. His pulse is 96/min and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. CT shows a cluster of dilated small bowel loops lying behind the right colon within an abnormal peritoneal recess. Which developmental error most likely produced this recess?","choices":{"A":"Incomplete fusion of the ascending mesocolon","B":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus","C":"Failed return of the herniated midgut","D":"Reversed rotation of the midgut loop","E":"Arrested descent of the sigmoid colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incomplete or irregular fusion of the ascending mesocolon with the posterior wall leaves retrocolic pockets in which small intestine can become trapped and obstruct. A vitelline duct remnant causes a diverticulum or fistula, failed return of the midgut produces an umbilical defect, reversed rotation places the duodenum anterior to the transverse colon, and the sigmoid does not descend in the same way. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hernias and adhesions are leading causes of small bowel obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: internal hernias can occur without surgical history when mesenteric fusion is abnormal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has 18 hours of fever, anorexia, and right upper quadrant pain. His temperature is 38.4°C (101.1°F) and pulse is 110/min. Leukocyte count is 15,800/mm3 (reference range: 4,500–11,000/mm3). Ultrasonography shows a normal gallbladder and an inflamed appendix lying beneath the right lobe of the liver at the end of a high-riding colon. Which developmental process most likely failed?","choices":{"A":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut","B":"Caudal descent of the cecal bud","C":"Fusion of the descending mesocolon","D":"Recanalization of the appendiceal lumen","E":"Return of loops from the umbilical cord"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After rotation the cecum lies in the right upper quadrant and normally descends into the right iliac fossa; when it fails to descend, the cecum and appendix stay subhepatic and appendicitis presents with right upper quadrant pain. Failed rotation places the colon on the left, descending mesocolic fusion affects the left side, the appendix does not undergo recanalization, and failed return causes an umbilical defect. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: periumbilical pain migrating to McBurney point is the classic presentation when the appendix is in its usual position (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: atypical appendix positions delay diagnosis and raise perforation risk (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — appendicitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has a flat perineum with no anal opening. His vital signs are normal. Over the next day, greenish particulate material appears in his urine. Imaging confirms a rectourinary fistula. Which embryologic error most likely caused this malformation?","choices":{"A":"Arrested caudal growth of the urorectal septum","B":"Failed proliferation of proctodeal ectoderm","C":"Failed recanalization of the rectal lumen","D":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum","E":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the cloaca is too small or the urorectal septum does not extend far enough caudally, the hindgut opening shifts anteriorly into the urethra, producing a rectourethral fistula. Proctodeal ectoderm forms the anal pit, the rectum is not described as recanalizing, neural crest loss causes megacolon without a fistula, and a vitelline duct remnant opens at the umbilicus. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: meconium in the urine of a newborn boy signals a rectourinary tract communication (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: anorectal malformations often occur with vertebral, cardiac, renal, and limb defects in VACTERL association (First Aid Genetics, VACTERL).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has no anal opening in its normal position. On examination, meconium is seen passing from the vaginal introitus. Her vital signs are normal and the abdomen is mildly distended. Abnormal partitioning between which two embryonic compartments most likely caused this finding?","choices":{"A":"Allantoic duct and yolk sac","B":"Midgut and hindgut loops","C":"Urogenital sinus and anorectal canal","D":"Mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts","E":"Bladder and the urachus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The urorectal septum normally divides the cloaca into the urogenital sinus anteriorly and the primitive anorectal canal posteriorly; incomplete separation leaves a rectovaginal fistula. The allantois and yolk sac are extraembryonic, the midgut–hindgut junction is in the transverse colon, the genital ducts form internal reproductive organs, and a urachal defect drains urine at the umbilicus. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectovaginal fistula is the female counterpart of the rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cloaca is endoderm-lined and bounded ventrally by surface ectoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has not passed meconium, and his abdomen is distended. A normally located anal dimple is present, and a thin bluish bulging membrane is seen at its center. No meconium appears in the urine or on the perineum, and imaging shows no fistula. The persistent tissue separates the hindgut from which structure?","choices":{"A":"Allantois","B":"Urachus","C":"Phallus","D":"Bladder","E":"Anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Imperforate anus occurs when the anal membrane fails to break down, so the hindgut stays closed off from the anal pit, the ectodermal depression of the proctodeum. The allantois and its remnant, the urachus, run from the bladder toward the umbilicus, the phallus forms from the genital tubercle, and the bladder forms from the urogenital sinus anterior to the septum. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an isolated membrane without a fistula distinguishes this lesion from septal defects (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: the anal membrane is the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — intestinal atresia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a painful, bleeding perianal lesion for 3 months. He has a history of HIV infection. His vital signs are normal. Anoscopy shows an ulcerated mass entirely below the pectinate line, and biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. Which artery is the main arterial supply to the region of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectal artery","B":"Middle colic artery","C":"Sigmoidal artery","D":"Inferior rectal artery","E":"Median sacral artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the anal canal is ectodermal and supplied by the inferior rectal arteries from the internal pudendal arteries. The superior rectal artery supplies the endodermal canal above the line, and the middle colic, sigmoidal, and median sacral arteries supply other territories. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line drain to the superficial inguinal nodes (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: HIV and HPV infection raise the risk of anal squamous cancer (general knowledge; no First Aid anal-cancer entry verified).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has painless bright red blood coating her stool for 2 weeks. Her vital signs are normal. Hemoglobin is 13.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Anoscopy shows engorged vascular cushions arising above the pectinate line. Biopsy of the mucosa overlying these cushions would most likely show which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","B":"Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells","C":"Transitional epithelium with umbrella cells","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium with ducts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Above the pectinate line the mucosa is endodermal and lined by simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells and intestinal glands. Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the airways, transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract, simple cuboidal epithelium lines tubules, and stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger gland ducts. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids are painless because the region above the line has visceral innervation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hemorrhoids). COMLEX: tumors above the line are typically adenocarcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — hemorrhoids","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has sharp anal pain with each bowel movement and streaks of blood on the toilet paper for 3 weeks. His vital signs are normal. Examination shows a posterior midline tear of the anoderm extending from the outer margin to just below the pectinate line. The epithelium at the site of this tear derives from which embryonic tissue?","choices":{"A":"Endoderm of the hindgut","B":"Mesoderm of the urorectal septum","C":"Endoderm of the allantois","D":"Ectoderm of the neural tube","E":"Ectoderm of the anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lower third of the anal canal, below the pectinate line, derives from surface ectoderm of the proctodeum, the anal pit, which is why it is squamous and somatically sensitive. Hindgut endoderm forms the canal above the line, the septal mesoderm forms the perineal body, allantoic endoderm relates to the bladder, and neural tube ectoderm forms the central nervous system. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anal fissures are painful because they lie below the line in somatically innervated tissue (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anal fissure). COMLEX: most fissures occur in the posterior midline (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium since birth and has progressive abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. His temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F) and pulse is 150/min. Rectal examination produces an explosive release of stool. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectosigmoid with a markedly dilated colon proximal to it. Which mechanism best explains the dilation of the proximal colon?","choices":{"A":"Tonic contraction of the distal aganglionic segment","B":"Absent ganglia in the dilated proximal segment","C":"Mechanical obstruction by inspissated meconium","D":"Excess ganglion cells in the distal segment","E":"Fibrotic stricture from ischemic injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lost enteric ganglion cells normally signal relaxation, so the distal aganglionic segment stays hypercontracted, peristalsis fails to pass, and content backs up into the normally innervated proximal colon, which dilates. The dilated segment has ganglia, inspissated meconium causes meconium ileus, excess ganglia do not cause this picture, and ischemic strictures are acquired. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the narrow segment is the diseased one, and the dilated bowel above it is normal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: Hirschsprung disease is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — defecation reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-week-old girl has chronic constipation and abdominal distension, and her older brother required colonic surgery in infancy. Her vital signs are normal. Genetic testing shows a heterozygous loss-of-function variant in RET. Which developmental process was most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of the colonic lumen","B":"Partitioning of the cloaca","C":"Differentiation of colonic smooth muscle","D":"Rotation of the primary intestinal loop","E":"Craniocaudal migration of enteric precursors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Loss of RET signaling arrests the proximal-to-distal migration of neural crest precursors along the bowel or causes their premature death, leaving a distal aganglionic segment. Recanalization defects cause atresia or stenosis, cloacal partitioning defects cause fistulas, smooth muscle differentiation is a mesodermal process, and rotation defects cause malrotation. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most distal bowel is affected because it is reached last by migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: in about 80% of cases the aganglionic segment extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid (Langman Ch. 15); EDNRB–EDN3 also supports enteric crest migration (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-day-old boy has delayed passage of meconium and abdominal distension. His vital signs are normal. A suction biopsy is taken from the rectum 2 cm above the pectinate line. Absence of ganglion cells in which layers of the bowel wall would confirm the suspected diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal crypts and lamina propria","B":"Muscularis mucosae and serosa","C":"Serosa and mesenteric fat","D":"Submucosal and myenteric plexuses","E":"Celiac and mesenteric ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Neural crest cells form the enteric ganglia of the submucosal and myenteric plexuses, and their absence in a rectal biopsy confirms aganglionic megacolon. The mucosa, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, serosa, and mesenteric fat do not house enteric ganglia, and the celiac and mesenteric ganglia are prevertebral sympathetic ganglia outside the wall. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosed by absence of ganglion cells on rectal suction biopsy; RET mutation in the REcTum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: biopsies are taken ≥2 cm above the pectinate line to avoid the normal hypoganglionic zone (general knowledge).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — GI wall layers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has ischemia limited to the sigmoid colon. Which arteries most directly supply this segment?","choices":{"A":"Middle colic arteries","B":"Right colic arteries","C":"Ileocolic arteries","D":"Sigmoidal arteries","E":"Inferior rectal arteries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The sigmoid colon is hindgut and is supplied by the sigmoidal branches of the inferior mesenteric artery. The middle colic, right colic, and ileocolic arteries supply midgut colon, and the inferior rectal arteries supply the anal canal below the pectinate line. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rectosigmoid junction is a watershed site in colonic ischemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: the left colic artery supplies the descending colon (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — colonic ischemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman is found to have an abnormally mobile cecum and ascending colon on imaging. Which acute complication is she predisposed to?","choices":{"A":"Cecal volvulus","B":"Sigmoid volvulus","C":"Intussusception","D":"Diverticulitis","E":"Angiodysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An ascending colon that keeps a free mesentery is mobile and can twist around its vascular pedicle, causing volvulus of the cecum and colon. Sigmoid volvulus relates to a long sigmoid mesentery, intussusception involves telescoping bowel, diverticulitis follows acquired outpouchings, and angiodysplasia is a vascular malformation. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: untreated volvulus can progress to ischemic gangrene (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: cecal twisting appears on imaging as a dilated cecum displaced from the right lower quadrant (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — volvulus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has no anal opening and meconium in his urine. Into which structure does the abnormal tract most likely open?","choices":{"A":"Umbilicus","B":"Ureter","C":"Seminal vesicle","D":"Sacral skin","E":"Urethra"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A urorectal septum that stops short shifts the hindgut opening anteriorly, and in boys it commonly opens into the urethra as a rectourethral fistula. The umbilicus relates to vitelline or urachal remnants, and the ureter, seminal vesicle, and sacral skin are not typical sites. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the female counterpart opens into the vagina (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: these fistulas occur in about 1 in 5,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has an adenocarcinoma of the anal canal just above the pectinate line. Which artery mainly supplies this site?","choices":{"A":"Inferior rectal artery","B":"Superior rectal artery","C":"Middle colic artery","D":"Ileocolic artery","E":"Obturator artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The canal above the pectinate line is endodermal hindgut and is supplied by the superior rectal artery, the continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery. The inferior rectal artery supplies the canal below the line, the middle colic and ileocolic arteries supply midgut colon, and the obturator artery supplies the thigh. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors above the line drain to internal iliac nodes (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: venous drainage above the line reaches the portal system (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn with aganglionic megacolon has normal RET. Which other receptor pathway supports enteric neural crest migration?","choices":{"A":"Wnt and Frizzled","B":"Sonic hedgehog and Patched","C":"Endothelin 3 and EDNRB","D":"Notch and Delta","E":"BMP and SMAD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Besides RET, the endothelin-3 and EDNRB pathway supports proliferation and migration of enteric neural crest precursors, and defects in it also cause aganglionosis. Wnt, hedgehog, Notch, and BMP pathways pattern other parts of the gut and embryo. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteric ganglia lie in the submucosal and myenteric plexuses (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: aganglionosis begins distally and extends proximally (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a polyp in the descending colon. Its epithelium arose from which embryonic tissue?","choices":{"A":"Midgut endoderm","B":"Splanchnic mesoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Neural crest","E":"Hindgut endoderm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The descending colon is a hindgut derivative, and its epithelium comes from hindgut endoderm. Midgut endoderm lines the cecum through proximal transverse colon, splanchnic mesoderm forms muscle and connective tissue, surface ectoderm forms the lower anal canal, and neural crest forms enteric ganglia. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut epithelium and glandular parenchyma are endodermal throughout the tract (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: hindgut endoderm also lines the bladder and urethra (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — origins of gut components","isHighYield":true}],"23":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"In which part of the small intestine are goblet cells most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum","B":"Distal duodenum","C":"Proximal jejunum","D":"Terminal ileum","E":"Middle jejunum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Goblet cells increase in number from the duodenum to the terminal ileum. The duodenum and jejunum have fewer of them. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: on routine H&E, the mucinogen-filled apex of a goblet cell looks empty because the mucinogen is lost during preparation (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"What portion of the small intestine does the jejunum make up?","choices":{"A":"Lower three-fifths of the small intestine","B":"Middle one-fifth of the small intestine","C":"Upper two-fifths of the small intestine","D":"Upper one-tenth of the small intestine","E":"Lower one-fifth of the small intestine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The jejunum is about 2.5 m long and makes up the upper two-fifths of the small intestine. The ileum is the lower three-fifths, and the duodenum is about 25 cm long. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the small intestine is the longest part of the digestive tract, at more than 6 m (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the ileum ends at the ileocecal junction, where the distal ileum joins the cecum (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are plicae circulares most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum next to the pylorus","B":"Middle and distal ileum segments","C":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve","D":"Cecum and proximal ascending colon","E":"Distal duodenum and proximal jejunum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Plicae circulares are most numerous in the distal duodenum and the beginning of the jejunum. They become smaller and fewer by the middle of the ileum; the lecture notes likewise describe the jejunum as having the tallest, most numerous plicae and the distal ileum as markedly reduced. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on oral contrast, the jejunum has a feathered look because its plicae circulares are taller and more numerous than the ileum's (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: these circular folds are permanent ridges that extend one-half to two-thirds of the way around the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides the major amplification of the small intestinal luminal surface?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares folds","B":"Mucosal intestinal villi","C":"Crypts of Lieberkühn","D":"Enterocyte microvilli","E":"Lateral cell plications"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Microvilli of the enterocytes provide the largest amplification of the luminal surface (20- to 600-fold), with several thousand per cell. Plicae and villi amplify it less, and lateral plications enlarge the lateral membrane. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a malabsorption workup, steatorrhea can be screened for with a Sudan stain for fecal fat (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: under the light microscope, the closely packed microvilli form a striated border on the apex of the enterocyte (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"By roughly how much do microvilli increase the apical surface area of an enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Up to about 600-fold","B":"Up to about 6-fold","C":"Up to about 60-fold","D":"Up to about 6,000-fold","E":"Up to about 60,000-fold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Microvilli increase the apical surface area of an enterocyte by as much as 600 times. The other values are one or more orders of magnitude off. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: villi project 0.5–1.5 mm into the lumen and cover the whole small intestine, which gives it a velvety look (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: plicae circulares first appear about 5–6 cm beyond the pylorus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein anchors the actin core at the tip of each microvillus?","choices":{"A":"Myosin I","B":"Spectrin","C":"Villin","D":"Dystrophin","E":"Titin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The actin microfilaments of the microvillus core are anchored to villin at the tip. Myosin I attaches them to the plasma membrane along the sides. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tight junctions in the ileum are relatively impermeable, so solutes must be moved across by active transport (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: elongated mitochondria cluster in the apical enterocyte cytoplasm, between the terminal web and the nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibacterial products are found in Paneth cell secretory vesicles?","choices":{"A":"Lysozyme and α-defensins","B":"Mucinogen and trefoil factors","C":"Pepsinogen and gastric lipase","D":"Secretin and cholecystokinin","E":"Intrinsic factor and gastrin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Paneth cell vesicles contain lysozyme, α-defensins, other glycoproteins, an arginine-rich protein, and zinc. Mucinogen comes from goblet cells, and the hormones come from enteroendocrine cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lysozyme digests the cell walls of certain groups of bacteria (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Paneth cells may occasionally be found in small numbers in the normal colon, and their number may rise in some diseases (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the basal portion of a goblet cell intensely basophilic?","choices":{"A":"Abundant smooth ER with lipid droplets","B":"Dense packing of acidic mucinogen granules","C":"Stacked apical mitochondria with actin","D":"Large glycogen stores with many lysosomes","E":"Heterochromatic nucleus with extensive rER"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The narrow basal stem holds a heterochromatic nucleus, extensive rER, and free ribosomes, which make it intensely basophilic. The mucinogen granules sit at the apex. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a large Golgi apparatus forms a wide cup around newly formed mucinogen granules above the goblet cell nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: microvilli are more obvious on immature goblet cells in the deep half of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What activates the apical receptors of open-type intestinal enteroendocrine cells?","choices":{"A":"Stretch of the muscularis","B":"Taste receptor stimulation","C":"Vagal acetylcholine release","D":"Circulating thyroid hormone","E":"Lamina propria histamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Open enteroendocrine cells have taste receptors on their apical membrane, and activating them triggers a G-protein cascade and peptide release. Closed cells lie mainly in the lower gland. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP released in the small intestine stimulates insulin release from the pancreas (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: somatostatin and histamine from enteroendocrine cells act as paracrine hormones that do not circulate (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the small intestinal stem cell niche located?","choices":{"A":"Tips of the intestinal villi","B":"Middle third of each villus surface","C":"Within the muscularis mucosae layer","D":"Lower half of the intestinal gland","E":"Within the duodenal submucosal glands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the base of the intestinal gland, and the niche is restricted to the lower half of the gland. Cells then migrate upward onto the villus and are shed at the tip. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in celiac disease, histology shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: the niche is also called the zone of cell replication (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the renewal time for absorptive and goblet cells in the human small intestine?","choices":{"A":"4–6 days","B":"4–6 hours","C":"4–6 weeks","D":"4–6 months","E":"1–2 years"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autoradiographic studies show that absorptive and goblet cells in the human small intestine are renewed every 4–6 days. Paneth cells live much longer, about 4 weeks. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aging epithelial cells undergo apoptosis at the villus tip and slough into the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: contraction of the terminal web may help close the gaps left when aging cells exfoliate (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Increased expression of which transcription factor commits crypt progenitor cells to the secretory lineage?","choices":{"A":"Notch1","B":"Math1","C":"Cdx2","D":"Pdx1","E":"Ptf1a"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cells committed to the secretory lineage (goblet, enteroendocrine, and Paneth cells) have increased Math1 expression. When Math1 is inhibited, cells follow the default pathway and become enterocytes. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteroendocrine cells appear to divide only once before differentiating (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: enteroendocrine cells migrate with absorptive and goblet cells, but more slowly (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are Peyer patches preferentially located in the ileal wall?","choices":{"A":"Along the mesenteric border itself","B":"Within the inner muscularis externa","C":"Opposite the mesenteric attachment","D":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium","E":"Within the myenteric nerve plexus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Peyer patches are large and numerous in the ileum and lie preferentially on the side of the intestine opposite the mesenteric attachment. They sit in the lamina propria and often extend into the submucosa. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches are unencapsulated lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa of the ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: in gross specimens, these aggregated nodules look like clusters of white specks (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"From what is the secretory component of secretory IgA derived?","choices":{"A":"Plasma cell J chain peptide","B":"Cleaved polymeric Ig receptor","C":"Goblet cell mucin glycoprotein","D":"Paneth cell defensin precursor","E":"Hepatocyte bile acid carrier"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After the receptor-dIgA complex reaches the apical surface, the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor is cleaved. Its bound extracellular portion becomes the secretory component. The J chain comes from the plasma cell and links the IgA dimer. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the polymeric Ig receptor carries dIgA across the enterocyte by transcytosis; only at the apical surface is it cleaved, and the retained fragment is the secretory component (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the receptor is a 75-kDa transmembrane glycoprotein synthesized by enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody do most plasma cells in the intestinal lamina propria secrete?","choices":{"A":"Monomeric IgG","B":"Pentameric IgM","C":"Monomeric IgE","D":"Monomeric IgD","E":"Dimeric IgA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Most lamina propria plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA rather than IgG. Some make pentameric IgM or IgE, and IgM can use a similar transcytotic route. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B cells stimulated in Peyer patch germinal centers become IgA-secreting plasma cells that settle in the lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: some IgE binds lamina propria mast cells and sensitizes them to luminal antigens (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with dyspepsia has an endoscopic biopsy taken just distal to the pylorus. It shows branched tubular submucosal glands whose secretion is highly alkaline. Besides protecting the mucosa, what does this secretion do?","choices":{"A":"Secretes lysozyme and defensins into the crypt lumens","B":"Brings chyme near the pancreatic enzyme pH optimum","C":"Absorbs conjugated bile acids from the passing chyme","D":"Produces intrinsic factor needed for vitamin B12 uptake","E":"Concentrates dimeric IgA for transport to the lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Brunner gland secretion contains bicarbonate and neutral and alkaline glycoproteins. It neutralizes acid chyme and brings the intestinal contents close to the optimal pH for activating pancreatic enzymes. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Submucosa, Fig. 17.25; Serosa). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bicarbonate from Brunner glands neutralizes acid, and pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion is stimulated by secretin (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI secretory products). COMLEX: the duct of each Brunner gland opens into the lumen of an intestinal crypt (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a small bowel resection for trauma. The specimen shows tall villi and tall, closely spaced circular folds. It has no submucosal glands and no large lymphoid aggregates. Which segment was removed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Ileum","C":"Jejunum","D":"Appendix","E":"Rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prominent, numerous plicae and tall villi without Brunner glands or Peyer patches identify the jejunum. Submucosal glands mark the duodenum, Peyer patches mark the ileum, and the colon lacks villi. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileum contains Peyer patches, and its plicae circulares are limited to its proximal part (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the jejunum gradually changes its morphology as it becomes the ileum, with no sharp boundary (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has a stab wound that injures the retroperitoneal part of his duodenum. Which layer forms the outer covering of this segment?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mesothelium","C":"Submucosa","D":"Muscularis","E":"Adventitia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parts of the duodenum in the retroperitoneum contact the posterior abdominal wall and have an adventitia. The intraperitoneal small intestine is covered by serosa, a mesothelium with underlying connective tissue. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Submucosa, Fig. 17.25; Serosa). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum is about 25 cm long and is the first, shortest, and widest part of the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the myenteric plexus between the two muscularis externa layers contains postsynaptic parasympathetic neuron cell bodies (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl23_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has chronic diarrhea and iron-deficiency anemia. Duodenal biopsy shows a flat mucosa with crypt hyperplasia and intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which surface-amplifying structure is lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae","B":"Villi","C":"Crypts","D":"Teniae","E":"Rugae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Villi are finger- or leaf-like mucosal projections with a lamina propria core, and celiac disease flattens them. Plicae have a submucosal core and persist. Microvilli are cell-surface structures. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); First Aid Gastrointestinal (celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and/or proximal jejunum and is linked to HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac serology includes IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has edema and fatty stools. Biopsy shows dilated central lymphatic capillaries in his villi. Why do absorbed lipoprotein particles normally enter these vessels and not the villus blood capillaries?","choices":{"A":"Villus blood capillaries lack endothelial fenestrations","B":"Lymphatic capillaries actively pump in triglyceride","C":"Portal venous blood rapidly degrades fat particles","D":"Lipoproteins cross the tight junctions into lymph","E":"Particles are too large for the blood capillaries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sugars, amino acids, and other solutes diffuse into the fenestrated capillaries under the epithelium. Lipoprotein particles are too large to enter blood vessels, so they enter the lacteal. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an enterocyte packages resynthesized lipid as chylomicrons and discharges it into the intercellular space (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: smooth muscle from the muscularis mucosae runs alongside each lacteal and may squeeze lymph out of it (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man in a nutrition study has a jejunal biopsy during active absorption. Electron microscopy shows widened spaces between the lateral surfaces of adjacent enterocytes. What structural change explains this?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of the apical tight junction protein strands","B":"Loss of the underlying enterocyte basal lamina sheet","C":"Separation of interdigitating cell-surface folds","D":"Shedding of aged enterocytes at the villus tips","E":"Fusion of mucinogen granules at the cell apex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enterocytes have flattened lateral processes (plicae) that interdigitate with those of neighboring cells. During active absorption these separate, enlarging the intercellular compartment and driving flow into the lamina propria. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the duodenum and jejunum, hypotonic contents allow water and small solutes to cross leaky tight junctions by solvent drag (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: contraction of the terminal web spreads the microvilli apart and exposes more surface area (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with chronic diarrhea has a jejunal biopsy. At the base of the intestinal glands, H&E shows cells with basophilic basal cytoplasm and large, intensely eosinophilic, refractile apical vesicles. What probably causes the eosinophilia?","choices":{"A":"Heme-rich cytochromes","B":"Glycogen granule stores","C":"Neutral mucin polymer","D":"Arginine-rich protein","E":"Iron-ferritin deposits"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These are Paneth cells. An arginine-rich protein in their secretory vesicles is probably responsible for the intense acidophilia. Mucin is lost on routine sections, and glycogen and ferritin do not explain the staining. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: zinc is also stored in these refractile apical vesicles (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has an ileal biopsy for chronic diarrhea. Routine H&E shows many epithelial cells with pale, empty-looking apical cups and narrow basal stems. Why does the apex of these cells look empty?","choices":{"A":"Lipid droplets dissolve in alcohol","B":"Mucinogen is lost during preparation","C":"Glycogen leaches out during fixation","D":"Apical vacuoles are filled with water","E":"Dying cells lose their organelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Goblet cell mucinogen is water soluble and is lost during routine H&E preparation, so the mucinogen-filled apex looks empty. TEM shows a large mass of mucinogen granules there. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Barrett esophagus, the squamous epithelium is replaced by nonciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: the apical mucinogen mass in a goblet cell pushes on and distorts the shape of neighboring cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl23_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has a well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor in her duodenum. From which epithelial cell type does this tumor most likely arise?","choices":{"A":"Enteroendocrine cells","B":"Absorptive enterocytes","C":"Intermediate goblet cells","D":"Microfold epithelial cells","E":"Myoepithelial basket cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteroendocrine cells make paracrine and endocrine peptides, including CCK, secretin, GIP, and motilin, and are the epithelial source of neuroendocrine tumors. Enterocytes absorb, and M cells transport antigen. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin from the small intestine initiates gastric and intestinal motility (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: VIP, bombesin, and enkephalins are neurocrine peptides secreted by submucosal and myenteric nerve cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has watery diarrhea 5 days after starting cytotoxic chemotherapy. Biopsy shows short, denuded villi. Why is the small intestinal epithelium damaged so quickly?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells migrating upward onto the villi","B":"Slow turnover of the mature villus enterocyte layer","C":"Continuous replacement from dividing crypt cells","D":"Stem cells concentrated near the outer villus tips","E":"Mature enterocytes dividing on the villus tips"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Small intestinal absorptive and goblet cells are replaced every 4–6 days from a highly proliferative niche in the lower crypt. When chemotherapy blocks division, losses at the villus tip are not replaced. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells in the niche usually undergo one or two divisions before committing to absorptive or goblet fates (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: cells shed at the villus tip are replaced by cells migrating upward from the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man in a research study has radiolabeled crypt cells. Days later, most labeled cells are on the villi. One labeled population has migrated downward to the gland base, lives about 4 weeks, and no longer divides. Which cells are these?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells","B":"Goblet cells","C":"Enterocytes","D":"Stem cells","E":"Plasma cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Paneth cells come from the crypt stem cells, migrate downward to the bottom of the gland, live about 4 weeks, and no longer divide once recognizable. Absorptive and goblet cells migrate upward. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dead Paneth cells are replaced by differentiation of a nearby committed cell in the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: stem cells in the crypt base give rise to every mature cell type of the intestinal epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has a genetic variant that blocks the transcription factor driving secretory fate in his intestinal stem cell niche. Which cells would differentiating crypt cells mainly become?","choices":{"A":"Mucus-secreting goblet cells","B":"Antimicrobial Paneth cells","C":"Hormone-secreting endocrine cells","D":"Absorptive enterocytes","E":"Antigen-sampling microfold cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Inhibiting the secretory transcription factor sends cells down the default pathway into absorptive enterocytes. Goblet, Paneth, and enteroendocrine cells form the secretory lineage. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells committed to the absorptive fate lose their secretory granules and gather mitochondria, rER, and ribosomes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: intermediate cells committed to the goblet fate develop a small rounded cluster of granules under the apical membrane (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man develops typhoid fever after travel. The bacteria crossed his ileal epithelium where it covers large lymphatic nodules. Which route did they most likely use?","choices":{"A":"Microfold cell transcytosis","B":"Paracellular tight junctions","C":"Goblet cell mucus exocytosis","D":"Paneth cell phagocytosis","E":"Lacteal lymphatic uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M cells over Peyer patches bind macromolecules and Gram-negative bacteria to GP2 receptors. They endocytose them and release them into a basolateral pocket holding immune cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23); Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches contain microfold cells that sample antigens and present them to immune cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: M cells have microfolds and a thin glycocalyx on their apical surface, not microvilli (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has recurrent giardiasis. Serum IgA is undetectable (reference range 70–400 mg/dL), and IgG and IgM are normal. Which mucosal defense is mainly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Lysis of luminal bacteria by Paneth cell enzymes","B":"Sensitization of the lamina propria mast cells","C":"Opsonization of invading bacteria for neutrophils","D":"Blocking of microbial adhesion to the epithelium","E":"Activation of the classical complement pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Luminal secretory IgA binds antigens, toxins, and microbes. It blocks their attachment and invasion by inhibiting motility, causing aggregation, or masking adhesion sites. Mast cell sensitization uses IgE, and opsonization is mainly an IgG role. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretory IgA binds an HIV envelope glycoprotein and prevents viral attachment and internalization (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the GALT works as an immunologic barrier along the whole length of the gastrointestinal tract (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has recurrent enteric infections. Her lamina propria plasma cells secrete normal J-chain-linked IgA dimers, but her intestinal secretions lack IgA. Which step most likely fails?","choices":{"A":"Class switching of mucosal B cells to the IgA isotype","B":"Linking of IgA monomers together by the J chain","C":"Homing of IgA plasma cells to the lamina propria","D":"Antigen uptake by the Peyer patch dendritic cells","E":"Receptor-mediated transcytosis across enterocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dimeric IgA binds a receptor on the basal enterocyte membrane and is endocytosed. It is carried across the cell to the apical surface and released into the lumen. Normal dimers with absent luminal IgA point to failed epithelial transport. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgM molecules use a similar receptor-mediated transcytosis pathway to reach the mucosal surface (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: dendritic cells and lymphocytes in the M-cell recess sample luminal antigen and can stimulate specific antibody production (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man is having a pancreaticoduodenectomy. How long is the first, shortest, and widest part of his small intestine?","choices":{"A":"About 5 cm","B":"About 2.5 m","C":"About 3.5 m","D":"About 25 cm","E":"About 60 cm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The duodenum is about 25 cm long. The jejunum is about 2.5 m and the ileum about 3.5 m long. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum ends at the duodenojejunal junction, where the jejunum begins (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the whole small intestine measures more than 6 m (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a normal duodenal biopsy during an anemia workup. How far do normal small intestinal villi project into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"About 0.5–1.5 mm","B":"About 5–15 µm","C":"About 50–150 µm","D":"About 5–15 mm","E":"About 5–15 cm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Villi extend 0.5–1.5 mm from the mucosal surface into the lumen and give it a velvety look. Microvilli are micrometer-scale, and plicae are larger folds. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Fig. 17.18). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in lactose intolerance the villi look normal unless lactase loss is secondary to injury at the villus tips (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the core of each villus is an extension of the lamina propria containing fenestrated capillaries (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has an ileal biopsy examined by electron microscopy. In goblet cells, what is the thin rim of apical cytoplasm bearing microvilli around the mucinogen granules called?","choices":{"A":"Calyx","B":"Cuticle","C":"Theca","D":"Capsule","E":"Mantle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In goblet cells, microvilli are restricted to a thin rim of cytoplasm called the theca. It surrounds the apical and lateral parts of the mucinogen mass. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet cells, Fig. 17.22). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mitochondria in goblet cells are concentrated in the basal cytoplasm (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the shape of the goblet cell, with a narrow basal stem under an apical granule mass, gives it its name (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman has a jejunal biopsy taken for research. In the transit-amplifying compartment of the crypt, how many divisions do intermediate cells usually undergo before committing to an absorptive or goblet fate?","choices":{"A":"About ten divisions","B":"No further divisions","C":"Thirty to forty divisions","D":"Hundreds of divisions","E":"One or two divisions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Intermediate cells form the amplifying compartment of the niche. They usually undergo one or two divisions before committing to the absorptive or goblet fate. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Intermediate cells; Epithelial cell renewal). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells have short, irregular microvilli with long core filaments and many desmosomal junctions (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: small mucin-like granules form a central column in the supranuclear cytoplasm of intermediate cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old man has a jejunal biopsy for evaluation of diarrhea. Roughly how much of the normal intestinal mucosa is made up of loosely organized lymphatic tissue?","choices":{"A":"About one-fourth","B":"About one-tenth","C":"About one-half","D":"About two-thirds","E":"About nine-tenths"},"correct":"A","explanation":"About one-fourth of the gut mucosa is a loosely organized layer of lymphatic nodules, lymphocytes, macrophages, plasma cells, and eosinophils in the lamina propria. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT is prominent in the lamina propria). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lymphocytes in the gut are also found between epithelial cells as well as in the lamina propria (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: activated antigen-presenting cells migrate to lamina propria nodules, where new plasma cells arise (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has upper endoscopy for anemia. How far beyond the pylorus do the plicae circulares begin to appear?","choices":{"A":"About 5–6 mm","B":"About 5–6 cm","C":"About 50–60 cm","D":"About 1–2 m","E":"About 3–4 m"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Plicae circulares begin to appear about 5–6 cm beyond the pylorus. They are most numerous in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Fig. 17.17). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: plicae circulares are also called the valves of Kerckring (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: each circular fold contains a core of submucosa, unlike a villus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true}],"24":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"How many teniae coli run along the cecum and colon?","choices":{"A":"Two opposing bands","B":"Four paired bands","C":"Five irregular bands","D":"Three equal bands","E":"Six spiral bands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"There are three narrowed, thickened, equally spaced teniae coli, formed from the outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: between the teniae, the outer longitudinal layer of the colon is an extremely thin sheet (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: omental appendices are small fat-filled peritoneal projections on the outer colonic surface (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"In which parts of the large intestine are teniae coli absent?","choices":{"A":"Cecum, ascending, and transverse colon","B":"Transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon, cecum, and appendix","D":"Descending colon, sigmoid, and rectum","E":"Rectum, anal canal, and appendix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teniae coli are seen mainly in the cecum and colon. In the rectum, anal canal, and vermiform appendix, the outer longitudinal muscle is a uniformly thick layer, as in the small intestine. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix differs from the cecum by having a uniform layer of longitudinal muscle in its muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the anal canal averages 4 cm and runs from the upper pelvic diaphragm to the anus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"What are omental appendices of the colon?","choices":{"A":"Fatty projections of the serosa","B":"Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules","C":"Submucosal outpouchings of the wall","D":"Bands of longitudinal smooth muscle","E":"Sacculations between muscle bands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Omental appendices are small fatty projections of the serosa on the outer colonic surface. Teniae are muscle bands, and haustra are sacculations between them. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustra coli are the visible sacculations between the teniae on the external surface of the cecum and colon (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the colon is subdivided into ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid parts by anatomic location (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorbing most dietary amino acids","B":"Secreting brush-border disaccharidases","C":"Reabsorbing water and electrolytes","D":"Transporting dimeric IgA to the lumen","E":"Producing antimicrobial defensins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells is reabsorbing water and electrolytes. They use the same Na+/K+-ATPase-driven system as small intestinal enterocytes. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive cells are essentially identical in morphology to small intestinal enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the principal functions of the large intestine are electrolyte and water reabsorption and elimination of undigested waste (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the ratio of columnar absorptive cells to goblet cells in most of the colon?","choices":{"A":"About 4:1","B":"About 1:1","C":"About 1:3","D":"About 20:1","E":"About 100:1"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Columnar absorptive cells predominate about 4:1 over goblet cells in most of the colon. The ratio approaches 1:1 near the rectum, where goblet cells increase. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: as absorptive cells are traced into the colonic glands they become fewer, while goblet cells increase (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the 4:1 predominance of absorptive cells is not readily apparent in histologic sections (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the turnover time of the glycocalyx secreted by colonic absorptive cells?","choices":{"A":"16–24 minutes","B":"2–3 hours","C":"4–6 days","D":"3–4 weeks","E":"16–24 hours"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic absorptive cells secrete glycocalyx rapidly, with a turnover time of 16–24 hours in humans. Unlike in the small intestine, it has not been shown to contain digestive enzymes. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colonic glycocalyx has not been shown to contain digestive enzymes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Na+/K+-ATPase is abundant in the lateral plasma membranes of colonic absorptive cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What part of the colonic intestinal gland forms the stem cell niche?","choices":{"A":"Upper third near the surface","B":"Lower third of the gland","C":"Middle third of the gland","D":"Entire length of the gland","E":"Surface cuff between glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the bottom of the intestinal gland, and the lower third of the gland is the stem cell niche. New cells then migrate up to the luminal surface. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: all mucosal epithelial cells of the large intestine come from stem cells at the bottom of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the intermediate cell types in the lower third of the colonic gland are identical to those in the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the turnover time of colonic enteroendocrine cells?","choices":{"A":"About 6 hours","B":"About 24 hours","C":"About 2 days","D":"About 1 year","E":"Up to 4 weeks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic absorptive and goblet cells turn over in about 6 days, whereas enteroendocrine cells last up to 4 weeks. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic epithelial turnover times are similar to those of the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the stomach, cells migrating downward to become parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells turn over in about 1 year (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where on the colonic surface are senile epithelial cells shed?","choices":{"A":"Base of each intestinal gland","B":"Rim of each gland opening","C":"Crests of the semilunar folds","D":"Between two adjacent glands","E":"Tips of the colonic villi"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Senile colonic epithelial cells that reach the surface undergo apoptosis and are shed at the midpoint between two adjacent intestinal glands. The colon has no villi. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on scanning EM, the colonic surface is divided by clefts into territories of gland openings (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic gland openings on the luminal surface are arranged in an orderly pattern (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer of the colon lie?","choices":{"A":"Between muscularis mucosae and submucosa","B":"Between the teniae and the circular muscle","C":"Under the epithelial basal lamina","D":"Within the core of each lymphatic nodule","E":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The collagen table is a thick layer of collagen and proteoglycans between the epithelial basal lamina and that of the fenestrated absorptive venous capillaries. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the collagen table participates in regulating water and electrolyte transport from the epithelium to the vascular compartment (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the absorptive venous capillaries under the colonic epithelium are fenestrated (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"How thick is the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer in the normal human colon?","choices":{"A":"Up to about 5 µm","B":"Up to about 0.5 µm","C":"Up to about 50 µm","D":"Up to about 0.5 mm","E":"Up to about 5 mm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The collagen table is about 5 µm thick in the normal colon and can be up to 3 times thicker in hyperplastic colonic polyps. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are generally benign, not neoplastic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: the colonic lamina propria shares the basic components of the rest of the gut but has extra specializations (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"From what may macrophages in the core of the colonic lamina propria arise?","choices":{"A":"Circulating neutrophils","B":"Pericryptal fibroblasts","C":"Exhausted goblet cells","D":"Epithelial niche cells","E":"Muscularis mucosae cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pericryptal fibroblasts divide beneath the gland base and migrate upward with the epithelium. Near the surface most take on macrophage features, and lamina propria macrophages may be their terminal stage. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pericryptal fibroblast sheath exists in both the large and small intestines (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: pericryptal fibroblasts divide adjacent to the epithelial stem cells at the bottom of the gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do colonic lymphatics from the gland bases first drain?","choices":{"A":"Lacteals in the lamina propria core","B":"Vessels reaching the luminal surface","C":"Plexus beneath the serosal surface","D":"Network within the muscularis mucosae","E":"Sinusoids of the regional lymph nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lymphatics are generally absent from the lamina propria core, but occasional small vessels at the gland bases drain into a network in the muscularis mucosae. Lymph then passes to submucosal and muscularis externa plexuses and on to regional nodes. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: no lymphatic vessels extend toward the luminal surface of the large intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: lymph leaves the colonic wall only after passing through plexuses in the submucosa and muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody marker is used to identify lymphatic endothelium in colonic lamina propria?","choices":{"A":"CD117","B":"CD34","C":"D2-40","D":"CK20","E":"S-100"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The monoclonal antibody D2-40 reacts with a 40-kDa O-linked sialoglycoprotein on lymphatic endothelium and is used to map lamina propria lymphatics. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the absence of lamina propria lymphatics was first found with light and electron microscopy of biopsy samples (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: D2-40 recognizes an O-linked sialoglycoprotein of about 40 kDa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"About what fraction of adenomatous polyps of the large intestine lie in the rectum and sigmoid colon?","choices":{"A":"About a tenth","B":"Almost half","C":"About a quarter","D":"About three-quarters","E":"About nine-tenths"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Almost 50% of adenomatous polyps of the large intestine are in the rectum and sigmoid colon, so rectosigmoidoscopy can detect them. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tubular adenomas have less malignant potential than villous adenomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: an adenomatous polyp is an intraepithelial neoplasm on a tissue mass protruding into the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl24_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a screening colonoscopy, and a transverse colon biopsy is normal. Compared with an ileal biopsy, which mucosal feature distinguishes it?","choices":{"A":"Glands lined by simple columnar cells","B":"Lamina propria rich in lymphocytes","C":"Muscularis mucosae deep to the glands","D":"Smooth surface without villi","E":"Goblet cells among absorptive cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The large intestinal mucosa has a smooth surface with neither villi nor plicae circulares, and its straight tubular glands span the full mucosa. The other features are shared with the ileum. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts of Lieberkühn with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the smooth colonic mucosa shows semilunar folds formed by contractions of the muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with long-standing ulcerative colitis has a barium study showing a lead-pipe colon. Which normal arrangement produces the sacculations she has lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares anchored in the submucosa","B":"Teniae bundles crossing circular muscle","C":"Fat lobules pushing out the serosal surface","D":"Lymphoid nodules distorting the gland rows","E":"Uniform longitudinal muscle around the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Muscle bundles from the teniae penetrate the inner circular layer at irregular intervals. These discontinuities let colonic segments contract independently and form the haustra. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa); First Aid Gastrointestinal (inflammatory bowel diseases). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is continuous colonic inflammation that involves the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: ulcerative colitis causes friable mucosa with superficial or deep ulcerations and crypt abscesses (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a flexible sigmoidoscopy. In the upper rectum, the endoscopist sees several shelf-like mucosal folds. Which structures are these?","choices":{"A":"Colonic semilunar folds","B":"Longitudinal anal columns","C":"Transverse rectal folds","D":"Plicae circulares folds","E":"Gastric rugae-type folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The upper rectum is distinguished from the rest of the large intestine by transverse rectal folds. Anal columns are longitudinal folds of the upper anal canal, and semilunar folds lie in the colon. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Rectum and anal canal). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pectinate line forms where hindgut endoderm meets ectoderm (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the depressions between the anal columns are called anal sinuses (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl24_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy. At the luminal surface, many tall, thin goblet cells hold only a few mucinogen granules in the central apical cytoplasm. What explains their appearance?","choices":{"A":"Secretion outpacing mucin synthesis","B":"Mucin loss during tissue processing","C":"Immature cells still in the niche","D":"Apoptosis of aging goblet cells","E":"Conversion into absorptive cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Goblet cells secrete mucus continuously as they migrate. At the surface the secretion rate exceeds the synthesis rate, so exhausted goblet cells appear. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: caveolated tuft cells described in the colonic epithelium may be a form of exhausted goblet cell (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic goblet cells may mature deep in the gland, even within the replicative zone (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has biopsies at several colonoscopy sites. One site has absorptive and goblet cells in a ratio close to 1:1. Where was it most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Proximal cecum","B":"Lower rectum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Transverse colon","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Absorptive cells outnumber goblet cells about 4:1 in most of the colon, but the ratio approaches 1:1 near the rectum, where goblet cells increase. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Rectum and anal canal). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectal mucosa resembles distal colonic mucosa, with straight tubular glands rich in goblet cells (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the rectum is the dilated distal portion of the alimentary canal (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with chronic constipation has a colonic biopsy showing abundant goblet cells in the intestinal glands. What is the main role of their continuous secretion?","choices":{"A":"Neutralizing gastric acid in chyme","B":"Digesting the residual disaccharides","C":"Killing bacteria with lysozyme","D":"Absorbing short-chain fatty acids","E":"Easing the passage of firm contents"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic goblet cells, more numerous than in the small intestine, secrete mucin continuously to lubricate the bowel. This eases passage of increasingly solid contents. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is associated with low-fiber diets and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticulosis). COMLEX: elimination of semisolid to solid waste is aided by the large amount of mucus in the colonic glands (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man in a research study has newly generated colonic crypt cells labeled. About how long after they begin migrating are these cells shed at the luminal surface?","choices":{"A":"About 5 hours","B":"About 5 weeks","C":"About 5 days","D":"About 5 months","E":"About 1 year"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Newly generated colonic cells undergo a few more divisions as they migrate up to the surface, where they are shed about 5 days later. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: as new colonic cells begin their upward migration, they continue dividing (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the stomach, surface and pit mucous cells turn over in 3–5 days (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy examined by electron microscopy. Deep in a gland, two cells with apical mucinogen granules are in mitosis. What does this show?","choices":{"A":"Mature goblet cells can still divide","B":"Mucinogen granules form during mitosis","C":"Exhausted surface cells reenter the cycle","D":"Paneth cells give rise to new goblets","E":"Absorptive cells convert into goblets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some intestinal cells keep dividing after they differentiate. Colonic goblet cells can mature deep in the gland, even in the replicative zone, and still divide. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dividing intestinal cells typically move away from the basal lamina toward the gland lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: chromosomes of dividing goblet cells lack a surrounding nuclear membrane on EM (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl24_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has hundreds of colonic adenomatous polyps, and his father had colectomy at age 35. Which initial epithelial event leads to small polyp formation in his colon?","choices":{"A":"Mutation of the KRAS proto-oncogene","B":"Deletion of the TP53 suppressor gene","C":"Methylation of the MLH1 repair gene","D":"Loss of the APC suppressor gene","E":"Gain of the RET tyrosine kinase gene"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the chromosomal instability pathway, colonic epithelial cells that lose the APC tumor suppressor gene develop small polyps. KRAS mutation then makes a benign adenoma, and p53 and DCC loss lead to carcinoma. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Folder 17.7, colorectal cancer); First Aid Gastrointestinal (familial adenomatous polyposis). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: familial adenomatous polyposis is an autosomal dominant APC mutation on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, familial adenomatous polyposis). COMLEX: about 98% of colorectal cancers are adenocarcinomas arising from glandular epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl24_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has a small hyperplastic colonic polyp removed. Under the surface epithelium, a layer of fibrillar protein and proteoglycans is about 15 µm thick. Which structure is thickened?","choices":{"A":"Basal lamina","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Pericryptal sheath","D":"Submucosa","E":"Collagen table"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The collagen table lies between the epithelial and capillary basal laminae and can be up to 3 times its normal thickness in hyperplastic colonic polyps. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mucosal polyps are usually small, look like normal mucosa, and are clinically insignificant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: submucosal polyps may include lipomas, leiomyomas, and fibromas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy. Large lymphatic nodules distort the regular spacing of the glands and extend into the submucosa. What best explains this extensive lymphoid development?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of dietary antigens","B":"Thin goblet-cell mucus layer","C":"Absence of epithelial renewal","D":"Abundant luminal microbes","E":"Drainage of lacteal lymph flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Colonic GALT is continuous with that of the terminal ileum and is more extensively developed. This probably reflects the large number and variety of microorganisms and noxious metabolic products in the colonic lumen. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease microscopically shows noncaseating granulomas and lymphoid aggregates (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: large colonic lymphatic nodules extend from the lamina propria into the submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has acute appendicitis without a fecalith. The resected appendix shows markedly enlarged lymphatic nodules with germinal centers. What is the most likely cause of his obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous scar tissue","B":"Lymphoid hyperplasia","C":"Carcinoid tumor mass","D":"Inspissated mucus plug","E":"Spasm of the teniae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most conspicuous feature of the appendix is its many lymphatic nodules extending into the submucosa. In children, appendicitis can be caused by lymphoid hyperplasia, whereas fecaliths are typical in adults. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix); First Aid Gastrointestinal (appendicitis). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendiceal obstruction causes early diffuse periumbilical pain through T8-T10 visceral afferents (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: the appendix is a common site of carcinoid, a tumor of enteroendocrine cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"sdl24_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a pedunculated sigmoid polyp removed. It contains carcinoma confined to the mucosa above the muscularis mucosae. Why is endoscopic removal considered adequate?","choices":{"A":"Polyp stalks contain no blood vessels","B":"Basal lamina halts carcinoma cell spread","C":"Serosa blocks spread to the regional nodes","D":"Mucosal cancer cells stop dividing early","E":"No lymphatics in the lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The colonic lamina propria normally has no lymphatic vessels, so cancer may grow within the epithelium and lamina propria before it reaches lymphatics at the muscularis mucosae. While a lesion is confined to the mucosa, endoscopic removal is adequate, pending microscopic review. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous adenomas have more malignant potential than tubular ones ('villous histology is villainous') (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: the polyp stalk is continuous with the colonic submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with ulcerative colitis has a colonic biopsy. Lymphatic vessels are numerous in the lamina propria between the glands. What does this finding indicate?","choices":{"A":"Active inflammation","B":"Healed remission phase","C":"Normal colonic mucosa","D":"Early adenoma growth","E":"Ischemic mucosal injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lamina propria lymphatics are normally absent. In ulcerative colitis, granulation tissue brings new blood and lymphatic vessels, so more of them signals active inflammation, and their disappearance tracks response to treatment. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis complications include toxic megacolon and colorectal cancer, with risk rising in pancolitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: treatment progress in ulcerative colitis can be monitored by biopsies showing lymphatics disappearing (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has biopsies showing new lymphatic vessels forming in the lamina propria. Expression of which factor is linked to this lymphangiogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Transforming growth factor alpha","B":"Hepatocyte growth factor and c-Met","C":"Vascular endothelial growth factor","D":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 signaling","E":"Insulin-like growth factor 1 axis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lymphangiogenesis in ulcerative colitis is linked to the expression of vascular endothelial growth factors (VEGFs). Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: in ulcerative colitis, granulation tissue forms with proliferating blood and lymphatic vessels (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a colonoscopy. Crescent-shaped folds project into the colonic lumen. What forms these folds?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal plicae circulares","B":"Lymphoid nodules in the mucosa","C":"Fat in the omental appendices","D":"Muscularis externa contraction","E":"Pressure from the luminal feces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The smooth colonic mucosa has no plicae or villi. Its semilunar folds are formed by contraction of the muscularis externa. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustra form because teniae bundles penetrate the circular layer, so colonic segments contract independently (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: mass peristaltic movements of the colon usually occur about once a day (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a colon biopsy. Absorptive cells show dilated intercellular spaces. Which lateral membrane pump drives their fluid uptake?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","B":"H⁺/K⁺-ATPase","C":"Ca²⁺-ATPase","D":"Proton V-ATPase","E":"Cu²⁺-ATPase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colonic absorptive cells transport water and electrolytes with the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase system in their lateral membranes, so the intercellular space is often dilated. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive cells are morphologically like small intestinal enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the dilated intercellular space reflects active fluid transport (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has a research colon biopsy. After leaving the stem cell niche, how many more divisions do new colonic cells undergo?","choices":{"A":"Five to six","B":"Ten to twelve","C":"Two to three","D":"Twenty or more","E":"Thirty to forty"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Newly generated colonic cells undergo two or three more divisions as they migrate up toward the luminal surface. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive and goblet cells turn over in about 6 days (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic stem cells reside at the bottom of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has a right hemicolectomy. With which region's lymphoid tissue is his cecal GALT continuous?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Distal duodenum","D":"Upper jejunum","E":"Anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GALT of the large intestine is continuous with that of the terminal ileum and is more extensively developed. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic lymphatic nodules can distort the regular spacing of the glands (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the appendix has many lymphatic nodules that extend into the submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a sigmoid colectomy. Lymph leaves the network in the deepest layer of the colonic mucosa. Where does it go next?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal and muscular plexuses","B":"Lacteals in the lamina propria","C":"Vessels near the luminal surface","D":"Portal vein radicles in the liver","E":"Thoracic duct directly from mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"From the network in the muscularis mucosae, lymphatics drain to plexuses in the submucosa and muscularis externa, then to regional lymph nodes. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colonic lamina propria core between glands normally lacks lymphatic vessels (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: lymphatic endothelium can be labeled with the D2-40 antibody (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 42-year-old man's colon mucosa is imaged by scanning EM. Clefts divide the surface into territories. About how many gland openings does each hold?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2","B":"3 to 5","C":"5 to 10","D":"500 to 1000","E":"25 to 100"},"correct":"E","explanation":"On scanning EM, the colonic surface is divided by clefts into territories that each contain 25 to 100 gland openings. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Fig. 17.29). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic glands are straight tubular glands that extend through the full thickness of the mucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the colonic mucosa lacks villi and plicae circulares, so its luminal surface is flat (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"}],"25":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Slow waves in intestinal smooth muscle originate in which cells?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Myenteric ganglion neurons","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons","D":"Enterochromaffin-like cells","E":"Longitudinal smooth muscle cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Costanzo describes the interstitial cells of Cajal, abundant in the myenteric plexus, as the pacemaker of gastrointestinal smooth muscle; their spontaneous depolarizations spread to smooth muscle through gap junctions. Myenteric and submucosal neurons modulate action potentials and force but do not set slow wave rhythm. Enterochromaffin-like cells are gastric histamine cells; it is mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cells that release serotonin to start the peristaltic reflex. Smooth muscle cells follow the pacemaker rather than generate it. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Slow waves, origin of slow waves); Guyton Ch. 63 (slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: interstitial cells of Cajal act like the sinoatrial node of the gut and couple to smooth muscle by gap junctions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: unitary smooth muscle lets action potentials spread rapidly from cell to cell (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the intrinsic slow wave frequency of the duodenum?","choices":{"A":"3 per minute","B":"6 per minute","C":"9 per minute","D":"12 per minute","E":"20 per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo gives slow wave frequencies from 3 to 12 per minute along the tract, with the duodenum highest at 12 per minute. Three per minute is the stomach, the lowest rate. Nine per minute is the ileum. Six per minute is not a characteristic value given, and 20 per minute exceeds the stated range. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; Small intestinal motility); Guyton Ch. 64 (segmentation maximum near 12 per minute in duodenum, 8 to 9 in terminal ileum). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow wave frequency sets the maximum contraction frequency, so segmentation in the proximal small intestine reaches at most about 12 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow wave frequency falls from duodenum to terminal ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"How does parasympathetic stimulation change the electrical activity of intestinal smooth muscle?","choices":{"A":"Higher slow wave frequency","B":"More spikes per slow wave","C":"Larger slow wave amplitude","D":"Shorter slow wave plateau","E":"Longer repolarization phase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neural and hormonal inputs do not alter the intrinsic slow wave frequency; parasympathetic stimulation increases the number of action potentials on the slow wave plateau and so strengthens contraction. A higher slow wave frequency is the classic trap because that rhythm is set by the pacemaker. Slow wave amplitude, plateau, and repolarization are set by pacemaker calcium and potassium currents and are not described as vagal targets; sympathetic input instead decreases spikes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; gastric and small intestinal motility). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the more action potentials ride on a slow wave, the larger the phasic contraction (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: acetylcholine depolarizes gut smooth muscle and norepinephrine hyperpolarizes it (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main role of segmentation contractions in the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Propelling chyme toward the cecum","B":"Clearing residue during fasting","C":"Emptying stomach into duodenum","D":"Moving feces into the rectum","E":"Mixing chyme with secretions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Segmentation splits a bolus orad and caudad and then lets it recombine, mixing chyme with pancreatic enzymes and exposing it to the mucosa with no forward propulsion. Propulsion toward the cecum is peristalsis. Fasting clearance is the migrating complex. Gastric emptying and rectal filling by mass movements are other patterns. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Segmentation contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: segmentation mixes and peristalsis propels, both coordinated by the enteric nervous system (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: segmentation movements may still travel about 1 cm anally, so the two patterns overlap (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"During small intestinal peristalsis, what happens to the segment orad to the bolus?","choices":{"A":"It narrows and lengthens","B":"It widens and shortens","C":"It narrows and shortens","D":"It widens and lengthens","E":"It keeps a fixed diameter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts while longitudinal muscle relaxes, so the segment narrows and lengthens. Widening and shortening is the caudad segment, where circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle contracts. Narrowing with shortening or widening with lengthening would need both layers to contract or relax together, which reciprocal innervation prevents. A fixed diameter does not propel the bolus. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excitatory transmitters such as ACh and substance P act behind the bolus, inhibitory VIP and NO ahead of it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: contraction of circular muscle narrows a segment and longitudinal contraction shortens it (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal mediator activates intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Somatostatin","B":"Serotonin","C":"Histamine","D":"Glucagon","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the bolus is sensed by mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cells, which release serotonin onto intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex. Somatostatin is an inhibitory paracrine. Histamine is a gastric acid secretagogue. Glucagon and secretin inhibit small intestinal motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT from mucosal cells activates IPANs that initiate peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrin, CCK, insulin, motilin, and serotonin enhance small intestinal motility, while secretin and glucagon inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"At what interval do migrating myoelectric complexes recur during fasting?","choices":{"A":"At 5 minute intervals","B":"At 30 minute intervals","C":"At 90 minute intervals","D":"At 4 hour intervals","E":"At 12 hour intervals"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo states that migrating myoelectric complexes recur about every 90 minutes during fasting to clear residual chyme from the stomach and small intestine. The shorter and longer intervals do not match the textbook value. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric motility; Small intestinal motility; motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the interdigestive complex sweeps from stomach through small intestine every 90 minutes while fasting (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: fasting complexes clear the stomach of residual chyme from the previous meal (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone initiates the interdigestive migrating complexes?","choices":{"A":"Cholecystokinin","B":"Secretin","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Motilin","E":"Peptide YY"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Motilin, secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, initiates the interdigestive myoelectric complexes. CCK and secretin are fed-state hormones that slow gastric emptying. Ghrelin is a gastric hunger hormone, not the initiator of fasting complexes. Peptide YY from ileum and colon inhibits secretion and appetite. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (candidate hormones, motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin is a 22 amino acid candidate hormone outside the gastrin-CCK and secretin families (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin receptor agonists such as erythromycin are used as prokinetics (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"How does eating a meal affect motilin secretion?","choices":{"A":"It inhibits secretion","B":"It stimulates secretion","C":"It doubles the cycle length","D":"It shifts release to the ileum","E":"It has no measurable effect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that motilin is released cyclically during fasting and that its secretion is inhibited after ingestion of food, which is why the fasting complexes stop in the fed state. Stimulation is the opposite effect. Cycle length doubling and an ileal shift are not described; motilin comes from stomach and upper duodenum. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin secreted during fasting). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: feeding replaces fasting complexes with segmentation and peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin is secreted by the stomach and upper duodenum, not the ileum (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"How often do colonic mass movements occur?","choices":{"A":"Ten to twelve times per minute","B":"Three to five times per hour","C":"Twenty to thirty times per day","D":"Once or twice per week","E":"One to three times per day"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo states that mass movements move colonic contents long distances, such as transverse to sigmoid colon, one to three times per day. Ten to twelve per minute resembles duodenal slow wave rhythm. The hourly and frequent daily options are too often, and once or twice per week too rare. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mass movements); Guyton Ch. 64 (series of mass movements lasting 10 to 30 minutes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a final mass movement propels feces into the rectum and produces the urge to defecate (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: during a mass movement the colon loses haustrations and contracts as a unit (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"The efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is mediated by which hormones?","choices":{"A":"Secretin and GIP","B":"Motilin and ghrelin","C":"CCK and gastrin","D":"Somatostatin and PYY","E":"VIP and nitric oxide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo describes gastric distention as the afferent limb, carried by the parasympathetic nervous system, and CCK and gastrin as the efferent limb that increases colonic motility and mass movements. Secretin and GIP decrease contractions. Motilin drives fasting complexes. Somatostatin and peptide YY are inhibitory. VIP and NO are inhibitory neurocrines. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Gastrocolic reflex). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastrocolic reflex explains the urge to defecate soon after a meal (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrocolic and duodenocolic reflexes nearly disappear when extrinsic autonomic nerves to the colon are removed (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combined contraction forms colonic haustrations?","choices":{"A":"Circular and teniae coli relaxation","B":"Muscularis mucosae fold contraction","C":"External anal sphincter contraction","D":"Circular and teniae coli contraction","E":"Ileocecal sphincter tonic contraction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton describes about 2.5 cm of circular muscle constricting while the teniae coli, the three longitudinal strips, also contract, so the unstimulated wall bulges into baglike haustrations. Relaxation of both layers would not form sacs. The muscularis mucosae folds the mucosa, and the anal and ileocecal sphincters are not involved. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Mixing movements, haustrations); Costanzo Ch. 8 (segmentation in the cecum and proximal colon). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustral contractions peak in about 30 seconds and fade over the next 60 seconds (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow haustral mixing takes 8 to 15 hours to move chyme from the ileocecal valve through the colon (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"What does rectal distention cause in the rectosphincteric reflex?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of the external anal sphincter","B":"Contraction of the internal anal sphincter","C":"Relaxation of the internal anal sphincter","D":"Relaxation of the ileocecal sphincter","E":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the rectum fills, its smooth muscle contracts and the internal anal sphincter relaxes in the rectosphincteric reflex. The external sphincter stays tonically contracted until it is relaxed voluntarily. Internal sphincter contraction is the opposite response. Ileocecal sphincter contraction follows entry of chyme into the cecum and is unrelated to rectal filling. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation is delayed after internal sphincter relaxation because the striated external sphincter remains contracted (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the internal anal sphincter is a tonic smooth muscle sphincter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"At what degree of rectal filling is the urge to defecate felt?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent of capacity","B":"About 25 percent of capacity","C":"About 50 percent of capacity","D":"About 75 percent of capacity","E":"About 90 percent of capacity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the urge to defecate arises once the rectum fills to 25 percent of its capacity. The other values do not match the textbook. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Valsalva maneuver raises intraabdominal pressure to aid defecation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: a weak functional sphincter at the rectosigmoid junction keeps the rectum empty most of the time (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve controls the external anal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","B":"Hypogastric nerves","C":"Vagus nerve","D":"Inferior mesenteric nerves","E":"Pudendal nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton states that the external anal sphincter, striated voluntary muscle, is controlled by the somatic pudendal nerve. Pelvic splanchnic nerves carry sacral parasympathetic fibers that reinforce the defecation reflex. Hypogastric and inferior mesenteric nerves are sympathetic. The vagus does not reach the anal canal. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (external anal sphincter is striated). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the external anal sphincter is striated voluntary muscle, unlike the smooth muscle internal sphincter (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic outflow comes from cord segments S2 to S4 (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has abdominal distention and no flatus 2 days after an open colectomy. Temperature is 37.2°C, pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. Bowel sounds are absent. Serum potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Heightened sympathetic outflow to the gut is suspected. Which change in his intestinal smooth muscle electrical activity is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Faster pacemaker slow wave rhythm","B":"Slower pacemaker slow wave rhythm","C":"Fewer spikes on each slow wave","D":"More spikes on each slow wave","E":"Loss of gap junction coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic activity and norepinephrine hyperpolarize gut smooth muscle and decrease the number of action potentials on each slow wave, weakening contractions. Slow wave frequency is intrinsic to the pacemaker and is not changed by neural input, so faster or slower rhythms are traps. More spikes is the parasympathetic effect. Gap junction coupling is not altered by sympathetic input. The normal potassium excludes hypokalemia as a cause of the ileus. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (frequency of slow waves; small intestinal motility, sympathetic activity decreases contraction); Guyton Ch. 63 (norepinephrine hyperpolarizes; peritoneointestinal reflex in Ch. 64). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal irritation inhibits excitatory enteric nerves and can cause intestinal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sympathetic fibers to the small intestine arise from the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — sympathetic and parasympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman develops constipation and dry mouth 3 weeks after starting a drug for urge incontinence that blocks muscarinic receptors. She has no abdominal pain. Abdominal examination shows mild distention with hypoactive bowel sounds. Serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). Reduced action of which transmitter on intestinal smooth muscle best explains her constipation?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Somatostatin","E":"Acetylcholine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Muscarinic blockade removes the excitatory action of acetylcholine released by parasympathetic and enteric neurons, so fewer action potentials ride on each slow wave and contractions weaken. Norepinephrine, VIP, nitric oxide, and somatostatin are inhibitory, so reducing their action would increase motility. Normal calcium excludes hypercalcemia. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic stimulation increases contraction); Guyton Ch. 63 (acetylcholine excites; norepinephrine inhibits); Guyton Ch. 64 (atropine weakens segmentation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anticholinergic adverse effects include constipation, dry mouth, urinary retention, and mydriasis (First Aid Pharmacology, muscarinic antagonists). COMLEX: postganglionic parasympathetic neurons sit in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic neurotransmitters and effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has 2 years of bloating, vomiting, and abdominal distention without mechanical obstruction on imaging. Full-thickness jejunal biopsy shows normal ganglion cells but a marked loss of c-KIT positive cells around the myenteric plexus. Serum TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–5.0 μU/mL). Loss of which function most likely explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal serotonin release","B":"Pacemaker slow wave generation","C":"Inhibitory nitrergic signaling","D":"Sympathetic ganglion output","E":"Somatic sphincter control"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The c-KIT positive cells around the myenteric plexus are interstitial cells of Cajal, which generate slow waves and pass them to smooth muscle through gap junctions; without pacemaker activity, coordinated contractions fail and pseudo-obstruction follows. Ganglion cells are normal, so nitrergic inhibition and enteric circuits are intact. Serotonin release is a mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cell function. Sympathetic and somatic outputs are extrinsic. Normal TSH excludes hypothyroidism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (origin of slow waves); Guyton Ch. 63 (slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow waves are not action potentials but oscillations in membrane potential set by pacemaker cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: slow wave depolarization is due to inward calcium current and repolarization to outward potassium current (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system regulation of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man develops profuse watery diarrhea and cramping 12 hours after eating undercooked chicken at a picnic. Temperature is 38.3°C and pulse is 104/min. Barium given earlier for another reason traveled from the jejunum to the colon within minutes. Which motility pattern best explains this rapid transit?","choices":{"A":"Peristaltic rush","B":"Segmentation contractions","C":"Migrating motor complex","D":"Haustral contractions","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intense mucosal irritation, as in severe infectious diarrhea, triggers powerful rapid peristalsis, the peristaltic rush, that sweeps contents into the colon within minutes to relieve the small intestine. Segmentation mixes without much propulsion. The migrating complex is a fasting pattern that travels slowly. Haustral contractions are colonic mixing movements. The gastroileal reflex intensifies ileal peristalsis after a meal but does not cause minute-scale transit. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Peristaltic rush; peristalsis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (peristaltic contractions). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal small intestinal peristalsis moves chyme only about 1 cm/min, taking 3 to 5 hours from pylorus to ileocecal valve (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: peristaltic rush is driven by autonomic and brainstem reflexes plus intrinsic myenteric reflexes (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman in a drug trial receives an agent that inhibits neuronal nitric oxide synthase in the enteric plexuses. Over the next week she reports cramping and slowed bowel transit. Pulse is 72/min and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Abdominal examination shows mild diffuse tenderness. Failure of which step of the peristaltic reflex best explains her slowed transit?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of circular muscle behind the bolus","B":"Contraction of longitudinal muscle ahead of the bolus","C":"Relaxation of circular muscle ahead of the bolus","D":"Release of serotonin from mucosal cells","E":"Slow wave generation in pacemaker cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In front of the bolus, inhibitory pathways that release VIP and nitric oxide relax circular muscle, so the segment widens and receives the bolus. Blocking NO synthesis leaves that segment narrow, so propulsion stalls and cramping follows. Circular contraction behind the bolus and longitudinal contraction ahead of it use excitatory transmitters. Serotonin release and pacemaker slow waves do not depend on neuronal NO. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP and NO are inhibitory enteric neurocrines that relax gut smooth muscle (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: reciprocal innervation keeps circular and longitudinal layers from contracting together (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — esophageal peristalsis and its neural control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with abdominal cramping takes a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. Jejunal manometry before the dose shows ring contractions at about 11 per minute that split and recombine chyme. After the dose, contractions at the same rhythm become very weak, and the slow wave frequency is unchanged. Serum magnesium is 2.0 mg/dL (reference range: 1.5–2.5 mg/dL). This change shows that his mixing contractions depend on which input?","choices":{"A":"Circulating motilin from the duodenum","B":"Excitation from the myenteric plexus","C":"Sympathetic outflow from the celiac ganglion","D":"Voluntary outflow from the sacral cord","E":"Stretch afferents of the colonic wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that segmentation becomes very weak when atropine blocks enteric excitation; slow waves set the rhythm but contractions are not effective without background excitation, mainly from the myenteric plexus. Motilin drives fasting complexes rather than fed mixing. Sympathetic outflow inhibits contraction. Voluntary sacral outflow controls the external anal sphincter. Colonic stretch signals affect the ileocecal sphincter, not jejunal mixing. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Mixing contractions, segmentation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (segmentation contractions). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the terminal ileum the maximum segmentation rate is about 8 to 9 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: segmentation exposes chyme to pancreatic enzymes and the mucosa for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and extrinsic modulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing type 1 diabetes has early satiety and postprandial vomiting. Gastric emptying scintigraphy shows marked delay. He is started on a low dose of a macrolide antibiotic for its prokinetic effect, and his symptoms improve. Which drug action most likely explains his improvement?","choices":{"A":"Dopamine D2 antagonism","B":"Muscarinic receptor agonism","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonism","D":"Mu opioid receptor agonism","E":"Motilin receptor agonism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Erythromycin, a macrolide, acts as a motilin receptor agonist and triggers migrating motor complex activity, promoting gastric and small intestinal emptying. Dopamine D2 antagonism is the metoclopramide mechanism. Muscarinic agonism is bethanechol. 5-HT3 antagonists are antiemetics and slow transit. Mu opioid agonists slow motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin initiates interdigestive complexes); Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI hormones, motilin receptor agonists). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist prokinetic that can cause tardive dyskinesia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, metoclopramide). COMLEX: motilin is released cyclically during fasting and suppressed by eating (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has chronic bloating and loose stools. A lactulose breath test shows an early rise in hydrogen. Fasting antroduodenal manometry shows no phase III activity over 6 hours. Serum albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Loss of which function most likely contributed to her condition?","choices":{"A":"Postprandial mixing of jejunal chyme","B":"Rectal storage of formed feces","C":"Colonic absorption of luminal water","D":"Interdigestive clearance of residue","E":"Gastrocolic reflex after meals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Absent phase III activity means the migrating motor complex is lost; these fasting contractions recur about every 90 minutes and sweep residual chyme and bacteria from the small intestine, so their loss allows bacterial overgrowth, shown by the early hydrogen rise. Postprandial mixing is segmentation. Rectal storage, colonic water absorption, and the gastrocolic reflex are colonic functions. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (migrating myoelectric complexes clear residual chyme); Guyton Ch. 63 (interdigestive complexes every 90 minutes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: small intestinal bacterial overgrowth causes bloating, diarrhea, and a positive hydrogen breath test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: the interdigestive complex moves through the stomach and small intestine in a fasting person (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — pathogenesis of malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman in a fasting motility study has a jejunal recording showing bands of intense contractions that start in the stomach, travel down the small intestine, and recur periodically throughout the fast. At the 6-hour mark she eats breakfast. Blood glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL) before the meal. Which change in her recording is most likely after the meal?","choices":{"A":"Shortening of the complex interval","B":"Spread of complexes to the colon","C":"Interruption of the complex cycle","D":"Increase in phase III force","E":"Doubling of slow wave frequency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Interdigestive complexes are a fasting pattern; food ingestion inhibits motilin secretion, so the cycle is interrupted and replaced by fed segmentation and peristalsis. Eating does not shorten the interval or strengthen phase III. Complexes do not extend into the colon. Slow wave frequency is intrinsic and does not change with feeding. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin secretion inhibited after food; interdigestive complexes); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin; small intestinal motility). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peristalsis rises after a meal through duodenal stretch and the gastroenteric reflex (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: motilin is a candidate hormone secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — hormonal control of gastric and duodenal motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman reports an urge to have a bowel movement within 20 minutes after almost every breakfast. She has no weight loss, blood in the stool, or nocturnal symptoms. Physical examination is normal. Hemoglobin is 13.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Which reflex best explains the timing of her urge?","choices":{"A":"Gastroileal reflex","B":"Rectosphincteric reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Gastrocolic reflex","E":"Peritoneointestinal reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastric distention by food increases colonic motility and mass movements through the gastrocolic reflex, which pushes feces into the rectum and produces the urge soon after a meal. The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum into the cecum. The rectosphincteric reflex follows rectal filling rather than a meal. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The peritoneointestinal reflex inhibits motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Gastrocolic reflex); Guyton Ch. 64 (gastrocolic and duodenocolic reflexes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an exaggerated gastrocolic reflex contributes to postprandial urgency in irritable bowel syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: the afferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is parasympathetic (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes and duodenal stimuli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has 8 to 10 bloody stools a day during a flare. Temperature is 37.9°C and pulse is 102/min. Hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and C-reactive protein is 48 mg/L (reference range: 0–10 mg/L). Which colonic motility pattern is most likely persistently increased in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Haustral mixing","B":"Mass movements","C":"Migrating complexes","D":"Jejunal segmentation","E":"Reverse peristalsis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that irritation of the colon, as in ulcerative colitis, initiates intense mass movements that persist almost all the time, which drives frequent stools. Haustral mixing is lost during mass movements and in active colitis. Migrating complexes and jejunal segmentation are small intestinal patterns. Retrograde peristalsis belongs to vomiting. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (mass movements, ulcerative colitis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mass movements). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and extends proximally in continuous fashion (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel disease). COMLEX: a series of mass movements lasts 10 to 30 minutes and may recur half a day later (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — lymphatic drainage and ulcerative colitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has a barium enema for mild constipation. Images show the cecum and ascending colon divided into regular saclike bulges that shift position over a few minutes, with no mass lesion. Thyroid studies and serum calcium are within normal limits. Which function do these contractions mainly serve?","choices":{"A":"Mixing contents for water absorption","B":"Propelling feces into the rectum","C":"Clearing residue during fasting","D":"Relaxing the internal anal sphincter","E":"Preventing backflow into the ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The saclike bulges are haustra from segmentation contractions of the cecum and proximal colon, which mix contents and expose them to the mucosa for water and electrolyte absorption. Propulsion into the rectum is a mass movement. Fasting clearance is the small intestinal migrating complex. Internal sphincter relaxation is the rectosphincteric reflex. The ileocecal sphincter prevents reflux into the ileum. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (large intestinal segmentation contractions, haustra); Guyton Ch. 64 (haustrations). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: teniae coli are three longitudinal muscle bands that create haustra (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: water absorption in the distal colon makes feces semisolid and harder to move (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has abdominal distention and bilious vomiting. He was born at term to a mother with an uncomplicated pregnancy. Digital rectal examination is followed by an explosive release of stool. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Absence of which cells in the distal colon most likely explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Striated sphincter muscle fibers","C":"Enterochromaffin cells of the mucosa","D":"Sacral preganglionic parasympathetic neurons","E":"Ganglion cells of the myenteric plexus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delayed meconium, distention, and explosive stool after rectal examination indicate Hirschsprung disease, a lack of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon. Without the intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex, the aganglionic segment stays contracted and the internal sphincter does not relax. Pacemaker cells, striated sphincter fibers, and mucosal endocrine cells are present. Sacral preganglionic neurons lie in the cord, not the colon wall. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Hirschsprung disease). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease results from failed neural crest migration and is associated with Down syndrome and RET mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: rectal suction biopsy is diagnostic, showing absent ganglion cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — myenteric and submucosal plexuses","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman has fecal urgency and leakage 4 months after a prolonged forceps-assisted vaginal delivery. Anal manometry shows a normal resting pressure but a markedly reduced voluntary squeeze pressure. Neurologic examination of the lower limbs is normal. Urinalysis is unremarkable. Dysfunction of which structure most likely explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"External anal sphincter","B":"Internal anal sphincter","C":"Rectal circular muscle","D":"Sigmoid colon teniae","E":"Ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Voluntary squeeze pressure reflects the striated external anal sphincter, controlled by the somatic pudendal nerve, which can be stretched during difficult delivery. Resting pressure comes mainly from the tonically contracted smooth muscle internal sphincter, which is normal here. Rectal circular muscle and sigmoid teniae propel feces. The ileocecal sphincter is not involved in continence. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation, external sphincter and pudendal nerve); Costanzo Ch. 8 (tonic internal anal sphincter; striated external sphincter). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pudendal nerve can be blocked at the ischial spine for vaginal delivery (First Aid Reproductive, pudendal nerve). COMLEX: the external sphincter is held contracted subconsciously unless conscious signals inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has worsening constipation and weak, ineffective attempts at defecation 6 weeks after a sacral fracture that damaged the S2 to S4 nerve roots. Rectal examination shows a large amount of soft stool in the vault. Serum potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Loss of which pathway most likely explains his weak rectal propulsion?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic fibers in the hypogastric nerves","B":"Vagal fibers supplying the proximal colon","C":"Intrinsic myenteric fibers in the rectum","D":"Parasympathetic fibers in the pelvic nerves","E":"Somatic fibers to the abdominal wall muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton describes the intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex as weak and dependent on reinforcement by a parasympathetic reflex through sacral cord segments; pelvic nerve fibers intensify peristalsis in the descending colon, sigmoid, and rectum and relax the internal sphincter. Damage to S2 to S4 roots removes that reinforcement. Hypogastric fibers are sympathetic and inhibitory. The vagus supplies proximal gut. The intrinsic plexus is intact. Abdominal wall nerves arise higher. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation reflexes, Fig. 64.6); Guyton Ch. 63 (sacral parasympathetics S2 to S4). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation signals entering the cord also trigger a deep breath, glottic closure, and abdominal muscle contraction (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic fibers via the pelvic nerves supply the distal colon, rectum, and anus (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerve parasympathetic distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man develops constipation after starting a drug for angina that slows atrioventricular nodal conduction and relaxes vascular smooth muscle. Which current underlying intestinal slow wave depolarization does this drug most directly reduce?","choices":{"A":"Inward sodium current","B":"Inward calcium current","C":"Outward potassium current","D":"Outward chloride current","E":"Inward magnesium current"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo attributes the depolarizing phase and plateau of the slow wave to cyclic opening of calcium channels with inward Ca2+ current; the drug described, a nondihydropyridine L-type calcium channel blocker such as verapamil, blunts that depolarization and contraction. Outward potassium current repolarizes the slow wave. Sodium, chloride, and magnesium currents are not described as the basis of the slow wave. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mechanism of slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: constipation is a common adverse effect of nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as verapamil (First Aid Cardiovascular, calcium channel blockers). COMLEX: even subthreshold slow waves produce weak basal contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old healthy volunteer eats a radiolabeled meal for a transit study. About how long does chyme normally take to travel from the pylorus to the ileocecal valve?","choices":{"A":"10 to 20 minutes","B":"30 to 45 minutes","C":"3 to 5 hours","D":"12 to 18 hours","E":"2 to 4 days"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton states that small intestinal peristaltic waves are weak and die out after 3 to 5 cm, so net movement averages about 1 cm/min and passage from pylorus to ileocecal valve takes 3 to 5 hours. Minutes describe a peristaltic rush. Twelve hours to days fit colonic transit. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Peristalsis in the small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peristaltic waves move at 0.5 to 2.0 cm/sec, faster proximally than distally (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: villous contractions driven by the submucosal plexus milk lymph from the central lacteals (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has an overnight fasting manometry study. Cyclic bands of strong contractions recur periodically throughout the fast. Over which part of the gut do these bands travel?","choices":{"A":"Stomach through small intestine","B":"Esophagus through duodenum","C":"Cecum through sigmoid colon","D":"Duodenum through rectum","E":"Sigmoid colon through anus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton describes the interdigestive myoelectric complexes as waves that move through the stomach and small intestine every 90 minutes in a fasting person, clearing residual chyme. They do not begin in the esophagus and do not run through the colon or rectum, where haustral contractions and mass movements dominate. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin, interdigestive complexes); Costanzo Ch. 8 (migrating myoelectric complexes in stomach and small intestine). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of fasting complexes predisposes to small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI hormones). COMLEX: motilin is secreted by the stomach and upper duodenum (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with an end ileostomy after colectomy notices that his stoma output rises sharply within minutes of each meal. Which reflex best explains this increase?","choices":{"A":"Gastrocolic reflex","B":"Enterogastric reflex","C":"Rectosphincteric reflex","D":"Renointestinal reflex","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton describes a gastroileal reflex in which eating intensifies ileal peristalsis and forces chyme out of the ileum; with an ileostomy that chyme appears as stoma output. The gastrocolic reflex acts on the colon, which has been removed. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The rectosphincteric reflex needs a rectum. The renointestinal reflex inhibits motility. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Peristalsis in the small intestine; ileocecal valve emptying). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileocecal sphincter normally holds chyme in the ileum, prolonging absorption (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: about 1500 to 2000 mL of chyme empties into the cecum each day (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with chronic constipation is taught to aid defecation by bearing down. Which action most directly increases his intraabdominal pressure during this effort?","choices":{"A":"Relaxing the abdominal wall","B":"Contracting the external anal sphincter","C":"Breathing rapidly through the mouth","D":"Expiring against a closed glottis","E":"Inspiring through an open glottis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo states that the intraabdominal pressure for defecation can be increased by the Valsalva maneuver, expiring against a closed glottis, and Guyton adds deep inspiration and abdominal wall contraction. Relaxing the abdominal wall lowers pressure. Contracting the external sphincter opposes defecation. Rapid breathing and inspiration through an open glottis do not build pressure. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation); Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation reflexes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: straining with a Valsalva maneuver raises intraabdominal and intrathoracic pressure and transiently lowers venous return (First Aid Cardiovascular, maneuvers). COMLEX: people who often inhibit natural defecation reflexes become constipated (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has marked cecal distention from an obstructing sigmoid tumor. Which reflex response is expected where the small intestine joins the large intestine?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter","B":"Intensified peristalsis in the ileum","C":"Faster emptying of ileal chyme","D":"Increased jejunal segmentation","E":"Relaxation of the internal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that cecal distention intensifies contraction of the ileocecal sphincter and inhibits ileal peristalsis, delaying further emptying into the cecum; these reflexes run through the myenteric plexus and extrinsic nerves. Intensified ileal peristalsis and faster emptying are the opposite responses. Jejunal segmentation and internal anal sphincter relaxation are not part of this reflex. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Feedback control of the ileocecal sphincter). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: large bowel obstruction is most often caused by colorectal cancer in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: an inflamed appendix can cause ileocecal sphincter spasm and partial ileal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"}],"26":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic α-amylase digests starch into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glucose, galactose, and free fructose units","B":"Maltose, maltotriose, α-limit dextrins","C":"Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose units","D":"Glycogen, amylopectin, and cellulose","E":"Galactose, maltose, and sucrose units"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds in starch and yields three oligosaccharides: α-limit dextrins, maltose, and maltotriose. Brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase then produce glucose. Monosaccharides are the brush-border end products, not amylase products. Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are dietary disaccharides. Glycogen and amylopectin are substrates, and cellulose is not digested. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary amylase starts starch digestion but is inactivated by low gastric pH (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: human amylase does not hydrolyze the β-1,4 bonds of cellulose (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Brush-border lactase digests lactose into which monosaccharides?","choices":{"A":"Glucose and fructose","B":"Two glucose molecules","C":"Glucose and galactose","D":"Galactose and fructose","E":"Glucose and maltose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lactase splits each lactose into one glucose and one galactose. Glucose plus fructose is the sucrase product, and two glucose molecules come from trehalose or maltose. Galactose with fructose is not a dietary disaccharide product, and maltose is a disaccharide. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose, and trehalase splits trehalose into two glucose molecules (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 80 percent of carbohydrate end product is glucose, with galactose and fructose making up the rest (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Undigested lactose in lactase deficiency causes diarrhea by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Active chloride secretion by crypts","B":"Faster colonic motility from gastrin","C":"Mucosal inflammation and blood loss","D":"Bile acid irritation of colonocytes","E":"Osmotic water retention in the lumen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nonabsorbable lactose stays in the lumen and holds an equivalent amount of water to keep contents isosmotic, which causes osmotic diarrhea. Crypt chloride secretion drives secretory diarrhea. Gastrin and motility changes are not the mechanism. Inflammation with blood loss suggests colitis. Bile acid diarrhea follows ileal disease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption, Box 8.2). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic bacteria ferment undigested lactose and produce gas, causing bloating and flatulence (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: lactose intolerance is managed by avoiding milk products or adding lactase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical transporter moves glucose and galactose into intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"GLUT4","D":"SGLT1","E":"NHE3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SGLT1, the Na⁺-glucose cotransporter, carries glucose and galactose across the apical membrane against an electrochemical gradient. GLUT5 is the apical fructose transporter. GLUT2 moves monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane. GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive carrier of muscle and fat. NHE3 is an apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT1 in intestine and SGLT2 in the proximal tubule both use the sodium gradient for glucose uptake (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: glucose and galactose absorption resembles transport in the early proximal convoluted tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"What supplies the energy for glucose uptake by SGLT1?","choices":{"A":"Direct ATP hydrolysis by the SGLT1 protein","B":"Sodium gradient set by the Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","C":"Proton gradient set by apical exchange","D":"Glucose gradient into the enterocyte","E":"Potassium gradient through apical channels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the energy for SGLT1 does not come directly from ATP but from the Na⁺ gradient across the apical membrane, which the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase creates and maintains. This is secondary active transport. Proton gradients drive peptide uptake. Glucose moves against its gradient on SGLT1. Potassium gradients do not drive it. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary active transport couples uphill solute movement to downhill Na⁺ movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Na⁺-glucose cotransport in the small intestine resembles transport in the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"How does fructose cross the apical membrane of intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport via SGLT1","B":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT4","C":"Proton cotransport via PepT1","D":"Primary active ATP pumping","E":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fructose crosses the apical membrane by facilitated diffusion on the fructose-specific transporter GLUT5 and leaves across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. No cotransporter or energy-requiring step is involved, so fructose moves down its gradient. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. PepT1 carries small peptides. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fructose absorption by facilitated diffusion does not proceed against an electrochemical gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: glucose and galactose exit the basolateral membrane on GLUT2, as fructose does (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protease begins protein digestion in the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Elastase","C":"Renin","D":"Pepsin","E":"Thrombin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Protein digestion begins with pepsin, formed when pepsinogen from chief cells meets low gastric pH. Trypsin and elastase are pancreatic proteases that act in the small intestine. Renin is a renal enzyme, and thrombin is a coagulation protease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, and parietal cells secrete H⁺ that activates it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin is an endopeptidase that digests collagen in meat (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Above what pH is pepsin denatured and inactivated?","choices":{"A":"Above pH 5","B":"Above pH 2","C":"Above pH 3","D":"Above pH 9","E":"Above pH 11"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The three pepsin isozymes have pH optima between 1 and 3, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so it stops acting once chyme is neutralized by duodenal bicarbonate. pH 2 and 3 fall in the optimum range. pH 9 and 11 overshoot the textbook threshold. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic HCO₃⁻ neutralizes duodenal chyme and inactivates pepsin (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin handles about 10 to 20 percent of total protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pancreatic proteases are exopeptidases?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin","B":"Pepsin and elastase","C":"Carboxypeptidase A and B","D":"Elastase and trypsin","E":"Enterokinase and pepsin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exopeptidases hydrolyze one amino acid at a time from the C-terminal end; carboxypeptidases A and B are the pancreatic exopeptidases. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and pepsin are endopeptidases that cleave interior bonds. Enterokinase is a brush-border activating enzyme. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brush-border aminopeptidases and dipeptidases finish oligopeptide digestion to absorbable units (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: elastase digests the elastin fibers that hold meat together (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Aminopeptidase","B":"Chymotrypsin","C":"Elastase","D":"Cathepsin B","E":"Enterokinase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brush-border enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin; trypsin then activates the remaining zymogens and more trypsinogen. Aminopeptidase is a brush-border exopeptidase that trims oligopeptides. Chymotrypsin and elastase are themselves products of trypsin activation. Cathepsin B is a lysosomal protease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins, Fig. 8.29). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enterokinase is also called enteropeptidase (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: trypsin activates trypsinogen in an autocatalytic step after the first activation (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is enterokinase located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic zymogen granules","B":"Gastric chief cells","C":"Intestinal brush border","D":"Pancreatic ductal cells","E":"Hepatic bile canaliculi"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme of the small intestinal epithelium, so trypsinogen is first activated in the intestinal lumen, not in the pancreas. Zymogen granules hold inactive precursors. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Ductal cells secrete HCO₃⁻-rich fluid. Bile canaliculi carry bile. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: separating zymogen storage from the activating enzyme protects the pancreas from autodigestion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin from the first activation step converts further trypsinogen to trypsin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme converts chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and the procarboxypeptidases to active forms?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Enterokinase","C":"Pepsin","D":"Lipase","E":"Amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Once a small amount of trypsin is made by enterokinase, trypsin catalyzes the conversion of the other inactive precursors to chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases A and B. Enterokinase acts on trypsinogen. Pepsin works in the stomach. Lipase and amylase digest fat and starch and do not activate proteases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of trypsin alone looks like loss of the pancreatic proteases because trypsin activates each precursor (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin also activates procolipase and prophospholipase A₂ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"Dipeptides and tripeptides cross the apical membrane by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport","B":"Facilitated diffusion","C":"Primary active transport","D":"Proton cotransport","E":"Receptor endocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Separate H⁺-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the cell, using an H⁺ gradient created by the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. Sodium cotransport carries free L-amino acids. Facilitated diffusion is the basolateral exit route for amino acids. Primary active transport and endocytosis are not the described routes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide carrier is PepT1 (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: peptide carriers allow protein nutrition despite single amino acid transporter defects (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"In which form is most ingested protein absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Free single amino acids","B":"Dipeptides and tripeptides","C":"Intact dietary protein molecules","D":"Large oligopeptides","E":"Long polypeptide chains"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that most ingested protein is absorbed in the dipeptide and tripeptide forms rather than as free amino acids. Intact proteins and larger oligopeptides are not absorbable and must be broken down by brush-border peptidases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteins are absorbable in larger units than carbohydrates, which must reach monosaccharides (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oligopeptides are hydrolyzed by brush-border proteases before absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to most dipeptides and tripeptides after they enter the epithelial cell?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis by cytoplasmic peptidases","B":"Export back out through apical PepT1","C":"Re-esterification in the smooth ER","D":"Packaging into nascent chylomicrons","E":"Conversion to cellular glycogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inside the cell, most dipeptides and tripeptides are hydrolyzed to amino acids by cytoplasmic peptidases, and the amino acids exit across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion; a few peptides are absorbed unchanged. PepT1 imports peptides. Re-esterification and chylomicrons belong to lipid absorption. Glycogen storage is unrelated. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal blood receives mostly free amino acids after protein absorption (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: basolateral amino acid exit uses separate carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has bloating, flatulence, and watery diarrhea within 2 hours of drinking milk. Temperature is 36.9°C, pulse is 78/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. A hydrogen breath test is positive after an oral lactose load. Stool is negative for occult blood. Which stool finding is most likely during symptoms?","choices":{"A":"High stool sodium concentration","B":"Fecal leukocytes and blood","C":"Fat globules on Sudan stain","D":"Diarrhea persisting during fasting","E":"Increased stool osmotic gap"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Undigested lactose holds water osmotically in the lumen, so unmeasured osmoles raise the stool osmotic gap, and the diarrhea stops with fasting. High stool sodium with a low gap fits secretory diarrhea. Leukocytes and blood suggest inflammatory diarrhea. Fat on Sudan stain points to fat malabsorption. Persistent diarrhea while fasting suggests secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Box 8.2 lactose intolerance; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial fermentation of lactose raises breath hydrogen and lowers stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the other brush-border enzymes are preserved in isolated lactase deficiency (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old girl has had watery diarrhea with each formula feeding for 3 weeks after an episode of rotavirus gastroenteritis. Temperature is 37.0°C, and pulse is 128/min. She is mildly dehydrated. Stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 7.0–7.5), and stool reducing substances are positive. Which change in her small intestine best explains this course?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic amylase output","B":"Loss of brush-border lactase","C":"Defective apical glucose cotransport","D":"Absent enterokinase activity","E":"Deficient bile salt secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Viral injury to villus tip cells reduces brush-border lactase, so formula lactose reaches the colon undigested and is fermented, lowering stool pH with positive reducing substances; the deficit recovers as villi regrow. Low amylase affects starch, not lactose. A glucose cotransport defect would be congenital. Enterokinase loss impairs protein digestion. Bile salt deficiency causes fat malabsorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (lactase deficiency, Box 8.2); Guyton Ch. 66 (brush-border disaccharidases). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary lactase deficiency follows mucosal injury from gastroenteritis or celiac disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: lactase sits on the villus brush border, where mucosal injury removes it first (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts acarbose with meals. Two weeks later she reports flatulence and loose stools. Temperature is 36.8°C, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.6% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Inhibition of which digestive step most directly explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border oligosaccharide hydrolysis","B":"Pancreatic triglyceride ester hydrolysis","C":"Basolateral monosaccharide exit","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransport","E":"Gastric pepsin protein hydrolysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acarbose inhibits brush-border α-glucosidases, so maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins are not split to glucose and reach the colon, where fermentation causes gas and osmotic stool. Triglyceride hydrolysis is the target of orlistat. Basolateral exit and apical cotransport would act on monosaccharides already formed. Pepsin digests protein. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates); First Aid Endocrine (α-glucosidase inhibitors). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: α-glucosidase inhibitors lower postprandial glucose by delaying carbohydrate digestion (First Aid Endocrine, diabetes drugs). COMLEX: oligosaccharides that escape digestion produce osmotic diarrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old boy has severe watery diarrhea with breast milk and with standard formula. Temperature is 36.9°C, pulse is 162/min, and weight is below birth weight. Serum urea nitrogen is 28 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL). Diarrhea stops on a formula whose sole carbohydrate is fructose. Which transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Apical fructose-specific uniporter","B":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","C":"Brush-border lactase hydrolase","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransporter","E":"Basolateral monosaccharide uniporter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose and galactose share the apical SGLT1 carrier, so a defect causes osmotic diarrhea with any glucose or galactose source, while fructose enters through GLUT5 and is tolerated. Loss of the fructose uniporter would spare glucose. A Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase defect would not be selective. Lactase deficiency would tolerate glucose formula. Loss of the basolateral uniporter would block fructose exit too. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucose-galactose malabsorption is an autosomal recessive SGLT1 defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: fructose is absorbed independently of sodium (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — solute-coupled water absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea after travel to an area with a cholera outbreak. Temperature is 37.1°C, pulse is 112/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Serum bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). An oral rehydration solution corrects his volume deficit. Why does this solution work?","choices":{"A":"Glucose blocks crypt chloride secretion","B":"Sodium enters through the apical GLUT5","C":"Glucose stimulates pancreatic fluid outflow","D":"Sodium uptake no longer requires ATP use","E":"Glucose-coupled sodium uptake is preserved"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholera toxin drives secretion, but apical SGLT1 still couples glucose entry to sodium entry, and water follows the absorbed solute. Glucose does not block chloride secretion. GLUT5 carries fructose, not sodium. Pancreatic fluid adds to losses. Sodium uptake still requires ATP indirectly, because the gradient depends on the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates; oral rehydration in cholera). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral rehydration solution uses SGLT1 cotransport of sodium and glucose (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: cholera toxin raises cAMP in crypt cells and opens CFTR chloride channels (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — cholera toxin and secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman training for a marathon tolerates more carbohydrate per hour from a gel that mixes glucose and fructose than from one with glucose alone. Pulse is 58/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Fasting glucose is 84 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which property of intestinal absorption best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Fructose activates the glucose cotransporter","B":"Fructose uses a separate apical carrier","C":"Glucose stimulates fructose cotransport","D":"Fructose enters against its gradient","E":"Glucose exits through apical GLUT5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose uptake is limited by apical SGLT1, while fructose enters through a distinct carrier, GLUT5, by facilitated diffusion, so mixing sugars adds absorptive capacity. Fructose does not activate SGLT1, and it is not cotransported. GLUT5 carries fructose down its gradient. Glucose exits across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLUT2 carries glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: fructose absorption is passive and depends on its concentration gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — monosaccharide transporters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man is seen 1 year after total gastrectomy for gastric adenocarcinoma. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and fecal nitrogen is normal. Which statement best describes his protein digestion?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal proteases complete digestion","B":"Protein digestion falls by about half","C":"Free amino acid uptake becomes defective","D":"Trypsinogen activation fails in duodenum","E":"Undigested protein reaches the colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pepsin starts protein digestion, but pancreatic and brush-border proteases can complete it, so protein digestion and absorption stay normal after gastrectomy or without gastric acid. Halved digestion overstates pepsin's role. Amino acid transport and enterokinase activation are intestinal and intact. Normal fecal nitrogen argues against colonic protein loss. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after total gastrectomy, vitamin B₁₂ deficiency follows loss of intrinsic factor (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin accounts for a minor share of protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — intrinsic factor and vitamin B12","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis from alcohol use has bulky stools and weight loss. Temperature is 36.7°C, and pulse is 88/min. Fecal elastase is 80 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Fecal nitrogen is increased. Which digestive step is most impaired for dietary protein?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of lactose to monosaccharides","B":"Hydrolysis of proteins to oligopeptides","C":"Activation of pepsinogen in the stomach","D":"Apical uptake of small peptides","E":"Basolateral exit of amino acids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic insufficiency removes trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases, so protein is not broken down to absorbable units and is lost in stool. Lactose hydrolysis is a brush-border step. Pepsinogen activation depends on gastric acid. Peptide uptake and amino acid exit are epithelial transport steps that remain intact. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low fecal elastase is a marker of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: pancreatic enzyme replacement treats both steatorrhea and creatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has taken high-dose omeprazole for 2 years for reflux esophagitis. Pulse is 70/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Intragastric pH during monitoring is 6.0 (reference range: 1.5–3.5). Which change in gastric protein digestion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin activity rises in the stomach","B":"Trypsinogen stays inactive in the duodenum","C":"Pepsinogen stays inactive in the stomach","D":"Protein absorption ceases in the jejunum","E":"Chief cells stop secreting pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen is converted to pepsin only at low pH, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so gastric proteolysis falls; protein absorption remains normal because pancreatic and brush-border proteases finish the job. Pepsin activity falls, not rises. Trypsinogen activation by enterokinase does not require acid. Chief cells keep secreting. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly block the parietal cell H⁺-K⁺ ATPase (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin isozymes have optimal activity between pH 1 and 3 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation and pharmacologic inhibition of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has failure to thrive, diarrhea, and generalized edema. Temperature is 36.8°C, and pulse is 148/min. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Sweat chloride is normal. Duodenal fluid has normal trypsinogen content, and adding a brush-border extract to the sample restores proteolysis. Which finding was most likely in the untreated duodenal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic trypsinogen secretion","B":"Low brush-border lactase activity","C":"Low pancreatic α-amylase activity","D":"Low trypsin and elastase activity","E":"Low gastric pepsin enzyme activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital enterokinase deficiency leaves trypsinogen unactivated, and because trypsin activates the other zymogens, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases stay inactive too. Trypsinogen secretion is normal here. Lactase, amylase, and pepsin do not depend on enterokinase. Normal sweat chloride argues against cystic fibrosis. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins; Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypoproteinemic edema reflects low oncotic pressure from protein malabsorption (First Aid Cardiovascular, edema). COMLEX: absence of trypsin mimics loss of the pancreatic proteases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — pancreatic proteases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back after a weekend of heavy drinking. Temperature is 38.0°C, pulse is 116/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum lipase is 1,450 U/L (reference range: 0–160 U/L). Premature activation of which zymogen inside the pancreas most directly starts the autodigestive cascade?","choices":{"A":"Chymotrypsinogen","B":"Proelastase","C":"Trypsinogen","D":"Procarboxypeptidase A","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Trypsin activates itself and the other pancreatic zymogens, so intrapancreatic trypsinogen activation triggers the cascade of autodigestion. Chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and procarboxypeptidase are downstream targets of trypsin. Pepsinogen is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, autodigestion; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alcohol and gallstones are the leading causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: lipase is more specific than amylase for acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl has had three episodes of acute pancreatitis without gallstones or alcohol use. Temperature is 37.4°C, and pulse is 96/min. Serum triglycerides are 110 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL). Genetic testing shows loss of function of a protein stored with digestive zymogens in acinar cells. Which protective mechanism is most likely lost?","choices":{"A":"Blocking of early active trypsin","B":"Enterokinase storage in acinar cells","C":"Bicarbonate secretion by duct cells","D":"Secretin release from duodenal cells","E":"Bile reflux into the pancreatic duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inhibitors of pancreatic trypsin are copackaged in secretory granules to block trypsin that forms prematurely, so their loss permits autodigestion. Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme, not an acinar protein. Duct bicarbonate and secretin regulate fluid output. Bile reflux is a trigger, not a protection. Normal triglycerides exclude a lipid cause. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, why autodigestion does not occur). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SPINK1 and PRSS1 mutations cause hereditary pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: zymogen granules are impermeable to proteins, keeping enzymes out of the cytoplasm (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has a photosensitive scaly rash and episodes of ataxia. Pulse is 84/min, and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Urine shows increased neutral amino acids, and his growth is normal. Serum albumin is 4.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which mechanism best explains his normal protein nutrition despite this defect?","choices":{"A":"Absorption on basolateral GLUT2 carriers","B":"Paracellular leak of free amino acids","C":"Pinocytosis of intact dietary protein","D":"Colonic absorption of amino acids","E":"Absorption as dipeptides and tripeptides"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hartnup disease is a defect in the Na⁺-dependent neutral amino acid transporter, but most protein is absorbed as dipeptides and tripeptides on separate H⁺-dependent carriers, so nutrition is preserved; tryptophan loss causes the niacin-deficiency rash. GLUT2 carries monosaccharides. Paracellular leak and pinocytosis are not significant routes. The colon does not absorb amino acids in useful amounts. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins); First Aid Biochemistry (Hartnup disease). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hartnup disease causes pellagra-like findings from tryptophan loss (First Aid Biochemistry). COMLEX: neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids use separate Na⁺ cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — amino acid cotransporters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"In a study of jejunal biopsies from a 41-year-old male volunteer, apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange is blocked with a selective inhibitor. The bath is kept at 37°C, and the dipeptide glycylglycine is added to the luminal side. Uptake of the dipeptide falls sharply, while brush-border peptidase activity is unchanged. What best explains the fall in uptake?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the luminal sodium gradient","B":"Inhibition of brush-border peptidases","C":"Block of basolateral amino acid exit","D":"Loss of the luminal proton gradient","E":"Reduced pancreatic protease release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dipeptide cotransporters use an H⁺ gradient that the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger creates, so blocking the exchanger dissipates the driving force. The sodium gradient drives free amino acid uptake, not peptide uptake. Peptidase activity is unchanged by design. Basolateral exit does not set apical uptake. Biopsies lack pancreatic input. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide transporter uses H⁺ cotransport (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: free amino acid uptake mirrors glucose uptake by Na⁺ cotransport (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has recurrent flank pain and passes hexagonal urinary crystals. Temperature is 36.9°C, and blood pressure is 120/78 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). His defective transporter also fails to absorb cystine, lysine, and arginine in the intestine. Which transport mechanism normally moves these amino acids into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Proton-dependent cotransport","B":"Primary active ATP pumping","C":"Sodium-dependent cotransport","D":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis","E":"Paracellular solvent drag"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cystinuria is absence of the Na⁺-amino acid cotransporter for dibasic amino acids in intestine and kidney. Free L-amino acids enter enterocytes by Na⁺-dependent cotransport, like glucose. Proton cotransport carries dipeptides and tripeptides. ATP pumping, endocytosis, and solvent drag are not the described routes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins; Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria causes hexagonal cystine stones with a positive cyanide-nitroprusside test (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: unabsorbed dibasic amino acids are excreted in feces (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption by enterocytes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has bloating and watery diarrhea after drinking juice with added cane sugar, but he tolerates milk. Deficiency of which brush-border enzyme is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Lactase","B":"Trehalase","C":"Amylase","D":"Lipase","E":"Sucrase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose; without it, sucrose stays in the lumen and causes osmotic diarrhea, while lactose is still split by lactase. Lactase deficiency would cause symptoms with milk. Trehalase splits trehalose from mushrooms. Amylase acts on starch, and lipase on fat. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency presents when sucrose enters the diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: sucrase also contributes to digestion of starch oligosaccharides to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has a normal oral glucose tolerance test. Which transporter carries absorbed glucose out of her enterocytes into the blood?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"SGLT1","D":"GLUT4","E":"SGLT2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose and galactose leave the enterocyte across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion on GLUT2, which also carries fructose. GLUT5 is the apical fructose carrier. SGLT1 is the apical sodium-coupled entry step. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. SGLT2 is a renal proximal tubule carrier. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT2 inhibitors lower plasma glucose by blocking renal glucose reabsorption (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: basolateral exit by facilitated diffusion follows the glucose gradient out of the cell (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis takes pancreatic enzyme capsules with meals. Which protease in the capsules digests the elastin fibers of meat?","choices":{"A":"Elastase","B":"Lipase","C":"Amylase","D":"Pepsin","E":"Colipase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elastase is a pancreatic endopeptidase that hydrolyzes elastin fibers, and it is activated from proelastase by trypsin. Lipase, amylase, and colipase digest fat and starch or assist lipase. Pepsin is gastric, not a pancreatic product. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins); Guyton Ch. 66 (elastase and meat fibers). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency from thick secretions in pancreatic ducts (First Aid Respiratory, cystic fibrosis). COMLEX: endopeptidases cleave interior peptide bonds, unlike exopeptidases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":3,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman asks why her pancreas does not digest itself. Why are pancreatic proteases secreted as inactive precursors?","choices":{"A":"To speed gastric protein digestion","B":"To allow absorption as intact peptides","C":"To resist bile salt denaturation","D":"To prevent pancreatic autodigestion","E":"To permit storage in pancreatic duct cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic peptidases are stored in zymogen granules as inactive precursors and are activated in the intestinal lumen, which prevents autodigestion of the pancreas. They act in the duodenum, not the stomach. Zymogen form does not change absorption or bile salt resistance. Duct cells secrete bicarbonate, and zymogens are stored in acinar cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, why autodigestion does not occur). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: premature intrapancreatic zymogen activation underlies acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: trypsin inhibitors in acinar granules add a second safeguard (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old man eats a high-protein meal. By which mechanism do free amino acids leave his enterocytes into the blood?","choices":{"A":"Facilitated diffusion","B":"Proton cotransport","C":"Sodium cotransport","D":"Primary active pumping","E":"Vesicular exocytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Free amino acids cross the basolateral membrane into portal blood by facilitated diffusion; the four separate Na⁺-coupled carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids are the apical entry step. Proton cotransport is the apical route for dipeptides and tripeptides. Sodium cotransport is the apical entry route for free amino acids. Primary active pumping and exocytosis are not described. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free amino acid entry mirrors glucose entry, with sodium cotransport at the apical membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: absorbed amino acids reach the liver through the portal vein (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman chews bread slowly before swallowing. Why does salivary amylase contribute little to her overall starch digestion?","choices":{"A":"Absence of starch substrate","B":"Rapid absorption by the mouth","C":"Inactivation by gastric acid","D":"Inhibition by bile salts","E":"Need for enterokinase activation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary amylase starts starch digestion in the mouth, but it plays little overall role because the low pH of gastric contents inactivates it; pancreatic amylase does most of the work. Bread contains starch. The oral mucosa does not absorb the enzyme. Bile salts and enterokinase do not regulate salivary amylase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic amylase secretion is stimulated by CCK and vagal input (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: salivary amylase activity continues inside the food bolus until gastric acid mixes in (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"}],"27":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes each triglyceride molecule into which products?","choices":{"A":"One monoglyceride and two fatty acids","B":"Glycerol and three free fatty acids","C":"Two monoglycerides and one fatty acid","D":"Cholesterol and two free fatty acids","E":"Lysolecithin and one free fatty acid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic lipase, secreted in active form, splits a triglyceride into one monoglyceride and two fatty acids. Pancreatic lipase does not release free glycerol, and two monoglycerides would need a second glycerol backbone. Cholesterol ester hydrolase splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids, and lysolecithin comes from phospholipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before the chyme reaches the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the optimum pH for pancreatic lipase is about 6, so duodenal acid must be neutralized by bicarbonate (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main function of colipase in lipid digestion?","choices":{"A":"Emulsifying lipid droplets in the gastric lumen","B":"Activating trypsinogen in the duodenal lumen","C":"Displacing bile salts from lipase","D":"Hydrolyzing cholesterol esters to free cholesterol","E":"Packaging lipids into chylomicrons in enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts displace pancreatic lipase from emulsified droplets. Colipase, activated from procolipase by trypsin, displaces the bile salts and binds lipase so digestion can proceed. Dietary proteins emulsify fat in the stomach, enterokinase activates trypsinogen, cholesterol ester hydrolase acts on cholesterol esters, and enterocytes assemble chylomicrons. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: CCK released by fat in the duodenum slows gastric emptying and gives pancreatic enzymes time to act (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2 hydrolyzes dietary phospholipids into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glycerol and fatty acids","B":"Cholesterol and fatty acids","C":"Monoglyceride and fatty acids","D":"Lysolecithin and fatty acids","E":"Bile acids and free glycine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phospholipase A2, secreted as a proenzyme and activated by trypsin, converts phospholipids to lysolecithin and fatty acids. Lysolecithin then joins bile salts in emulsifying fat. The other pairs come from triglyceride or cholesterol ester hydrolysis, and bile acids with glycine come from deconjugation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salts are amphipathic, so the hydrophilic side faces water and the hydrophobic side faces the lipid core (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the end products of lipid digestion, other than glycerol, are insoluble in water and must be carried in micelles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"In which organelle do enterocytes re-esterify absorbed lipid digestion products?","choices":{"A":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum","B":"Golgi secretory apparatus","C":"Apical brush-border membrane","D":"Lysosomal digestive compartment","E":"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inside the enterocyte, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and lysolecithin are re-esterified on the smooth endoplasmic reticulum to triglycerides, cholesterol esters, and phospholipids. The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles afterward. Lipids cross the brush border by diffusion, and lysosomes digest heme, not lipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chylomicrons carry dietary triglyceride and are cleared by lipoprotein lipase in peripheral capillaries (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: lipid crosses the apical membrane by diffusion, so absorption depends on mucosal surface area (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"Chylomicrons leave the intestinal villus by entering which vessels?","choices":{"A":"Portal venules","B":"Villus blood capillaries","C":"Hepatic sinusoids","D":"Lymphatic lacteals","E":"Mesenteric arterioles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chylomicrons are about 1000 Å across, too large to enter vascular capillaries, so they move between endothelial cells into lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct. Portal venules and villus capillaries carry water-soluble nutrients. Hepatic sinusoids and arterioles are not the exit route. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: injury to the thoracic duct leaks chylomicron-rich lymph, giving milky fluid high in triglyceride (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: glycerol is water soluble and does not need micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to the bile salts of a micelle when its lipids are absorbed at the brush border?","choices":{"A":"They enter the enterocyte with the lipids","B":"They are packaged into chylomicrons","C":"They remain in the intestinal lumen","D":"They are re-esterified in the cytosol","E":"They are degraded by brush-border enzymes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Micelles release lipids at the apical membrane, but the micelles themselves do not enter the cell. The bile salts stay in the lumen and are absorbed downstream in the ileum. They are not part of chylomicrons, not re-esterified, and not degraded at the brush border. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed micelles are disks about 50 Å across with bile salts lining the outside (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile salt reuse through the enterohepatic circulation keeps the pool large without heavy hepatic synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"In which intestinal segment are conjugated bile salts actively reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Conjugated bile salts are reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver in portal blood, the enterohepatic circulation. About 95 percent of secreted bile acids return this way. The duodenum and jejunum are where bile salts work on lipids. The colon and stomach do not reabsorb them. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: Crohn disease of the terminal ileum can interrupt bile salt reabsorption and cause fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does surgical removal of the intestinal segment that reabsorbs bile salts shrink the total bile salt pool?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic synthesis stops after resection","B":"Hepatic synthesis lags behind fecal loss","C":"Bile salts are deconjugated in the bile","D":"Gallbladder storage is lost to the colon","E":"Micelles enter the ileal wall intact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resecting the reabsorptive segment interrupts the enterohepatic circulation, so bile salts go out in feces. The liver increases synthesis, but synthesis falls short of the fecal loss and the pool shrinks. Synthesis rises rather than stopping. Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism. Gallbladder storage is unaffected, and micelles do not enter cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption from short bowel or Crohn disease can cause fat malabsorption and bile acid diarrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: a smaller bile salt pool limits micelle formation and causes steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"By which intestinal level are most ingested lipids absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Mid-jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal colon","E":"Rectosigmoid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most dietary lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, so the work of bile salts is done long before they reach the terminal ileum for reabsorption. The ileum recycles bile salts rather than absorbing most fat, and the stomach and colon absorb little lipid. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: short bowel syndrome follows extensive small bowel resection and causes malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, short bowel syndrome). COMLEX: bile salts left in the lumen after lipid uptake travel on to the ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells secrete intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Gastric chief cells","B":"Pancreatic acinar cells","C":"Gastric parietal cells","D":"Salivary gland cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intrinsic factor is a glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells, the same cells that secrete H+. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, acinar cells secrete pancreatic enzymes, salivary glands secrete R proteins, and S cells secrete secretin. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes chronic gastritis and pernicious anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: after gastrectomy, oral B12 is not absorbed and B12 must be given by injection (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Free vitamin B12 released in the stomach first binds to which carrier?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"R protein","C":"Transferrin","D":"Apoferritin","E":"Calbindin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Free B12 binds R proteins from salivary secretions. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins and B12 moves to intrinsic factor. Transferrin and apoferritin carry iron, and calbindin binds calcium. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 absorption as well as fat absorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: most water-soluble vitamins use sodium-dependent cotransport, unlike B12 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"What event in the duodenum allows vitamin B12 to bind intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Gastric hydrochloric acid","C":"Conjugated bile salts","D":"Brush-border lactase","E":"Salivary amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins, so B12 transfers to intrinsic factor. The B12-intrinsic factor complex resists these proteases and travels to the ileum. Acid and pepsin act earlier in the stomach, and bile salts, lactase, and amylase do not free B12. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with neurologic findings (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Active vitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption mainly by inducing which protein?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin storage protein","B":"Transferrin carrier","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Calbindin D-28K","E":"Intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the vitamin D-dependent calcium-binding protein calbindin D-28K in enterocytes, which promotes calcium absorption. Apoferritin and transferrin handle iron, colipase helps lipase, and intrinsic factor binds B12. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inadequate calcium absorption causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: 25-hydroxycholecalciferol is the main circulating but inactive form of vitamin D (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How do absorbed vitamins A, D, E, and K leave the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Bound to albumin in portal blood","B":"By sodium cotransport into blood","C":"In micelles across the basal membrane","D":"Bound to transferrin in portal blood","E":"In chylomicrons into lymph"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fat-soluble vitamins are handled like dietary lipids. They travel in micelles, diffuse into the cell, are incorporated into chylomicrons, and are extruded into lymph. Sodium cotransport handles most water-soluble vitamins, micelles do not enter the cell, and transferrin carries iron. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase and decreases absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How is heme iron converted to free iron inside the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Digestion by pancreatic proteases","B":"Digestion by lysosomal enzymes","C":"Hydrolysis by brush-border peptidases","D":"Cleavage by gastric pepsin","E":"Release by bile salt micelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heme iron crosses the apical membrane intact. Inside the enterocyte, lysosomal enzymes digest heme and release free iron, which binds apoferritin and then crosses the basolateral membrane. The other enzymes act in the lumen, and micelles carry lipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis increases intestinal iron absorption through low hepcidin (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: transferrin carries absorbed iron to the liver and bone marrow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman starts a weight-loss drug that blocks gastric and pancreatic lipase. One month later she has oily, foul-smelling stools. Temperature is 36.9°C, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Stool fat is 14 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which digestive step is most directly impaired?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of dietary triglyceride","B":"Exocytosis of chylomicrons into lymph","C":"Synthesis of apolipoprotein B in enterocytes","D":"Reabsorption of bile salts in the ileum","E":"Activation of procolipase by trypsin enzyme"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blocking lipase leaves triglycerides unhydrolyzed. Undigested triglycerides are not absorbable and appear in the stool. Chylomicron export and apoprotein synthesis follow digestion. Ileal bile salt reabsorption and procolipase activation are not drug targets here. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (orlistat). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat causes steatorrhea and decreased absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: lingual and gastric lipases normally digest only about 10 percent of triglyceride (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers, diarrhea, and greasy stools. Fasting gastrin is 1200 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL), and duodenal aspirate pH is 3 (reference range: 6–7). Exocrine enzyme output is normal. Which mechanism best explains his fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Loss of hepatic bile salt secretion","B":"Defective chylomicron assembly in enterocytes","C":"Excess colipase release from acini","D":"Reduced ileal absorptive surface","E":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A gastrin-secreting tumor drives excess H+ secretion that overwhelms pancreatic bicarbonate. Duodenal pH stays far below the lipase optimum of about 6, so pancreatic lipase is inactivated. Bile secretion, chylomicron assembly, and ileal surface are intact, and colipase helps lipase rather than causing malabsorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrin-secreting tumor, often of the pancreas or duodenum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: a proton pump inhibitor reduces the H+ load delivered to the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and gastrin","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has jaundice, dark urine, and pale bulky stools. Imaging shows a mass blocking the common bile duct. Total bilirubin is 9.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.2 mg/dL). Pancreatic enzyme secretion is normal. Which mechanism best explains his steatorrhea?","choices":{"A":"Loss of pancreatic lipase activity in the duodenal lumen","B":"Failure of trypsin to activate procolipase","C":"Failure of emulsification and micelle formation","D":"Defective apoprotein synthesis in enterocytes","E":"Obstruction of intestinal lymphatic drainage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts emulsify fat and form the mixed micelles that carry lipid products to the brush border. When bile fails to reach the duodenum, lipid digestion and micellar transport fail. Pancreatic lipase, colipase activation, apoprotein synthesis, and lymph flow are intact. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pale stools and dark urine point to obstructed bile flow with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: bile salts are amphipathic, which lets them line micelles in the aqueous lumen (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile formation and micelles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-month-old boy has failure to thrive and pale, foul-smelling stools. Blood smear shows acanthocytes. Fasting triglyceride is 8 mg/dL (reference range: 30–150 mg/dL), and duodenal biopsy shows enterocytes filled with lipid droplets. Which defect best explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Failure of micelle formation","B":"Inactive pancreatic lipase","C":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption","D":"Failure of chylomicron assembly","E":"Deficient brush-border lactase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Failure to synthesize Apo B causes abetalipoproteinemia. Chylomicrons do not form or are not exported to lymph, so lipid builds up in enterocytes and plasma lipid stays low. Lipid filling the cells shows that micelle formation, lipase digestion, and uptake into the cell are working. Bile salt uptake and lactase do not explain lipid-filled enterocytes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia presents with acanthocytes, steatorrhea, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: apoproteins cover about 20 percent of the chylomicron surface (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — abetalipoproteinemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man develops a milky right pleural effusion 5 days after esophagectomy. Pleural fluid triglyceride is 420 mg/dL (reference range: <110 mg/dL). He is placed on a diet in which medium-chain triglycerides replace long-chain fats. Why does this diet reduce the leak?","choices":{"A":"Medium-chain fats enter portal blood directly","B":"Medium-chain fats bypass bile salt emulsification","C":"Medium-chain fats increase lacteal reabsorption","D":"Medium-chain fats block chylomicron uptake by tissues","E":"Medium-chain fats seal the thoracic duct wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-chain fats are re-esterified and packaged in chylomicrons, which travel in lacteals to the thoracic duct. Small amounts of short- and medium-chain fatty acids are more water soluble and diffuse straight into portal blood, so less lipid enters the injured duct. The other options are not mechanisms of this diet. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Guyton Ch. 66 (Direct absorption of fatty acids into the portal blood). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thoracic duct carries intestinal lymph and chylomicrons into the left subclavian vein (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus lacteals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. Tissue transglutaminase IgA is positive, and duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Pancreatic function is normal. Which mechanism best explains her impaired fat absorption?","choices":{"A":"Absent pancreatic lipase secretion into the duodenum","B":"Reduced mucosal surface for lipid diffusion","C":"Deficient apoprotein B synthesis in enterocytes","D":"Loss of bile salt conjugation","E":"Blocked lacteal drainage by tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lipid products leave micelles and cross the apical membrane by diffusion, which depends on surface area. Villous loss reduces that area and impairs fat absorption. Lipase secretion is normal, apoprotein synthesis is not the lesion, bile salt conjugation is hepatic, and no lymphatic tumor is present. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum and is linked to iron deficiency anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease gives an abnormal D-xylose test, unlike pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with Crohn disease had 100 cm of terminal ileum resected. She now has steatorrhea and flank pain, and imaging shows a renal stone. Stone analysis shows a calcium stone. Serum calcium is 9.1 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains her kidney stone?","choices":{"A":"Increased colonic oxalate absorption","B":"Increased hepatic bile acid synthesis","C":"Decreased renal calcium filtration","D":"Increased intestinal calcium uptake","E":"Decreased colonic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lost bile salts shrink the pool, so fat is malabsorbed. Unabsorbed fatty acids bind luminal calcium, leaving oxalate free to be absorbed in the colon and excreted in urine as calcium oxalate stones. Increased hepatic synthesis falls short but does not form stones. Calcium uptake falls rather than rises. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fat malabsorption from ileal bile salt loss causes calcium to bind fat, so oxalate is absorbed and forms calcium oxalate stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: about 95 percent of secreted bile acids normally return to the liver (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had 30 cm of terminal ileum removed after a penetrating injury. He now has watery diarrhea several times daily without weight loss, and it improves with cholestyramine. Stool fat is 6 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which mechanism best explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Undigested lactose holding water in the lumen","B":"Gastrin-driven acid overload of the duodenum","C":"Bile acids stimulating colonic secretion","D":"Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum","E":"Loss of colonic surface for water uptake"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A short ileal resection lets bile acids spill into the colon, where they stimulate secretion and cause watery diarrhea. The liver still makes enough bile acid to keep micelles adequate, so stool fat stays near normal. A binding resin removes the irritant. Lactose, gastrin, deconjugation, and colonic loss are not the mechanism here. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption can cause bile acid diarrhea as well as fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: longer ileal resections deplete the pool faster than the liver can replace it, adding steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a surgical blind loop of small bowel has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. A hydrogen breath test is positive soon after a glucose load. Vitamin B12 is 160 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which mechanism best explains her fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase","B":"Loss of the ileal bile salt carrier","C":"Failure of chylomicron export to lymph","D":"Bacterial deconjugation of bile salts","E":"Autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bacteria remove glycine and taurine from bile salts, making nonionized bile acids that are absorbed too early, before micelles finish their work. The pool reaching the lumen falls and fat absorption fails. Acid inactivation follows gastrinoma, carrier loss follows ileal disease, chylomicron failure is abetalipoproteinemia, and intrinsic factor loss does not cause steatorrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial overgrowth can cause B12 deficiency because bacteria consume the vitamin (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: low luminal pH also promotes early bile acid absorption by converting bile acids to the nonionized form (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has fatigue and numbness in both feet. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), and mean corpuscular volume is 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Antibodies to parietal cells are positive. Which route of vitamin B12 replacement is most reliable for her?","choices":{"A":"Oral tablets taken with food","B":"Oral tablets with antacid","C":"Sublingual tablets at bedtime","D":"Oral liquid with pancreatic enzymes","E":"Intramuscular injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autoimmune loss of parietal cells removes intrinsic factor, so dietary or oral B12 is not absorbed in the ileum. Costanzo states that B12 must be given by injection in this setting. Antacids and pancreatic enzymes do not replace intrinsic factor. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia reflects autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor from parietal cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: B12 deficiency also causes neurologic findings, which folate does not correct (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with Crohn disease had 90 cm of terminal ileum resected 4 years ago. He now has fatigue and paresthesias. Mean corpuscular volume is 114 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum B12 is 110 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is lost?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food by pepsin","B":"Ileal uptake of the B12-intrinsic factor complex","C":"Binding of free B12 to R proteins","D":"Gastric secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","E":"Protease cleavage of R proteins in the duodenum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The B12-intrinsic factor complex is absorbed by a specific transport mechanism in the ileum. Removing the terminal ileum removes that site even though the stomach makes intrinsic factor normally. Pepsin release, R protein binding, intrinsic factor secretion, and protease cleavage all occur above the ileum. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: body B12 stores last years, so deficiency appears long after resection (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with chronic alcoholic pancreatitis has greasy stools and weight loss. Mean corpuscular volume is 106 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum B12 is 170 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food in the stomach","B":"Secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","C":"Uptake of the complex in the terminal ileum","D":"Degradation of R proteins in the duodenum","E":"Binding of B12 to transferrin in plasma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins in the duodenum so B12 can transfer to intrinsic factor. Without them, B12 stays on R proteins and is poorly absorbed. Pepsin release and parietal cell function are normal, the ileum is intact, and transferrin carries iron. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat, fat-soluble vitamins, and vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal D-xylose test because the mucosa is intact (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man on hemodialysis has bone pain and muscle cramps. Serum calcium is 7.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. Which mechanism best explains his low intestinal calcium absorption?","choices":{"A":"Deficient hepatic 25-hydroxylation","B":"Deficient renal 1α-hydroxylation","C":"Excess intestinal transferrin synthesis","D":"Loss of intrinsic factor secretion","E":"Impaired chylomicron formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The proximal tubule converts 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to active 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol through 1α-hydroxylase. In chronic kidney disease this step fails, calbindin D-28K induction falls, and calcium absorption drops. Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is intact because 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. The other choices involve iron, B12, and lipid. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, inadequate calcium absorption causes osteomalacia (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: active vitamin D acts on intestine, kidney, and bone (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — calcium absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has fatigue, joint pain, and darkening skin. Transferrin saturation is 78% (reference range: 20–50%), and ferritin is 1900 ng/mL (reference range: 24–336 ng/mL). Genetic testing shows a homozygous HFE mutation. Which change best explains his iron overload?","choices":{"A":"High hepcidin increasing iron absorption","B":"Loss of lysosomal heme digestion","C":"Excess intrinsic factor secretion","D":"Excess vitamin D-induced calbindin","E":"Low hepcidin increasing iron absorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Absorbed iron moves from apoferritin across the basolateral membrane and onto transferrin. HFE mutation lowers hepcidin, which normally limits that export, so absorption runs high and iron accumulates. High hepcidin would decrease absorption. Lysosomal heme digestion, intrinsic factor, and calbindin are unrelated. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron); First Aid Hematology (hemochromatosis). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis is linked to HFE mutation on chromosome 6 (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: iron enters enterocytes as Fe2+ or as heme iron (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — iron absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has stopped taking her pancreatic enzyme capsules. She now has easy bruising and greasy stools. Prothrombin time is 19 seconds (reference range: 11–13.5 seconds), and platelet count is normal. Deficiency of which vitamin best explains her bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin C","B":"Vitamin B12","C":"Vitamin K","D":"Vitamin B1","E":"Folic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Without pancreatic lipase, fat is not digested and fat-soluble vitamins, which travel with lipids in micelles and chylomicrons, are malabsorbed. Vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time. Vitamin C, B1, and folate are water-soluble vitamins absorbed by sodium-dependent cotransport. B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption syndromes). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency from cystic fibrosis causes malabsorption of vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: vitamin K is needed to make clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — malabsorption and pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-week-old infant digests some milk fat before it reaches the duodenum. Which enzymes start this digestion?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2","B":"Brush-border disaccharidases","C":"Hepatic lipoprotein lipase","D":"Lingual and gastric lipases","E":"Salivary α-amylase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before chyme reaches the small intestine. Phospholipase A2 is pancreatic, disaccharidases act on sugars, lipoprotein lipase clears plasma lipoproteins, and amylase digests starch. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the stomach, dietary proteins keep lipid droplets emulsified because bile acids are absent (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: slow gastric emptying gives pancreatic enzymes time to digest lipid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old man eats a fatty meal. Which molecules cover most of the surface of the chylomicrons his enterocytes export?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipids","B":"Triglycerides","C":"Cholesterol esters","D":"Bile salts","E":"Free fatty acids"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Phospholipids cover about 80 percent of the chylomicron surface, and apoproteins cover the rest. Triglycerides and cholesterol esters form the core. Bile salts stay in the lumen, and free fatty acids are re-esterified. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Apo B is required for chylomicron formation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: chylomicrons reach the blood through the thoracic duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman's liver must replace bile acids lost in her stool. Which enzyme limits the rate of new bile acid synthesis?","choices":{"A":"HMG-CoA reductase","B":"Lipoprotein lipase","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Phospholipase A2","E":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis from cholesterol. HMG-CoA reductase limits cholesterol synthesis, lipoprotein lipase clears plasma lipoproteins, and colipase and phospholipase A2 act in the intestinal lumen. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid loss in feces increases hepatic bile acid synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acids are conjugated with glycine or taurine before secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man eats meat as his main source of vitamin B12. What releases the vitamin from food proteins in his stomach?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"Lysosomal enzymes","C":"Pepsin digestion","D":"Bile salts","E":"Brush-border peptidases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dietary B12 is released from food by pepsin in the stomach, then binds R proteins. Intrinsic factor binds B12 later in the duodenum, lysosomal enzymes act inside cells, bile salts form micelles, and brush-border peptidases act in the small intestine. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 is found in animal-derived foods, so strict vegans risk deficiency (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: R proteins come from salivary secretions (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman takes oral iron for anemia. Which plasma protein carries the absorbed iron to her liver and marrow?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin","B":"Transferrin","C":"Albumin","D":"Calbindin","E":"Ceruloplasmin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transferrin, a β-globulin, carries iron from the intestine to liver stores and then to bone marrow. Apoferritin binds iron inside enterocytes, albumin is not the iron carrier, calbindin binds calcium, and ceruloplasmin carries copper. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low hepcidin in hemochromatosis increases iron absorption (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: heme iron is absorbed intact and freed inside the enterocyte (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man eats eggs rich in sterol esters. Which pancreatic enzyme frees the sterol for micelles?","choices":{"A":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase","B":"Phospholipase A2","C":"Pancreatic amylase","D":"Carboxypeptidase A","E":"Enteropeptidase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase is secreted in active form and splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids. Phospholipase A2 acts on phospholipids, amylase on starch, carboxypeptidase on peptides, and enteropeptidase on trypsinogen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free cholesterol is solubilized in mixed micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cholesterol ester hydrolase can also release glycerol from triglycerides (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"About how much fluid enters the gastrointestinal lumen each day from diet plus secretions?","choices":{"A":"About 2 L","B":"About 4 L","C":"About 15 L","D":"About 9 L","E":"About 0.5 L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 2 L of dietary fluid plus about 7 L of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, biliary, and intestinal secretions gives slightly more than 9 L. Most is absorbed by the small intestine and colon. 2 L is the dietary share alone. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 9 L absorbed daily is almost equal to the whole extracellular fluid volume, so diarrhea can rapidly deplete it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: crypt cells add secreted fluid that villus cells then reabsorb (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the osmolarity of the fluid absorbed by the intestine compared with plasma?","choices":{"A":"Hyposmotic to plasma","B":"Isosmotic to plasma","C":"Hyperosmotic to plasma","D":"Variable with meal tonicity","E":"Hyperosmotic in the jejunum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The absorbate is isosmotic: solute and water are absorbed in proportion, as in the renal proximal tubule. It is not hyposmotic or hyperosmotic, and it does not vary with meal tonicity or segment. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal transport mechanisms resemble those of the early renal proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the small remaining volume of 100–200 mL is lost in feces each day (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first step in intestinal fluid absorption by villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of solute","B":"Absorption of water","C":"Secretion of chloride","D":"Secretion of potassium","E":"Secretion of mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Villus cells first absorb solute, and water follows osmotically so the absorbate stays isosmotic. Water does not move first. Chloride secretion is a crypt function, potassium secretion occurs in the colon, and mucus does not drive fluid uptake. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cell type determines direction, with villus cells absorbing and crypt cells secreting fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: disease that reduces absorptive surface area, such as small intestinal infection, reduces fluid absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"The jejunum mainly produces net absorption of which salt?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride","B":"Potassium chloride","C":"Sodium bicarbonate","D":"Potassium bicarbonate","E":"Calcium chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the jejunum, H+ leaves on apical Na+-H+ exchange and HCO3- made by carbonic anhydrase is absorbed into blood, giving net NaHCO3 absorption. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange and absorbs net NaCl. The colon absorbs Na+ and secretes K+. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the jejunum is the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: jejunal Na+ also enters on Na+-glucose, Na+-galactose, and Na+-amino acid cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone induces synthesis of apical Na⁺ channels in colonic epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Antidiuretic hormone","B":"Natriuretic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Aldosterone","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Like renal principal cells, colonocytes have apical Na+ and K+ channels, and aldosterone induces Na+ channel synthesis. This increases Na+ absorption and, secondarily, K+ secretion. ADH acts on renal water channels, ANP promotes natriuresis, and CCK and secretin regulate pancreatic and biliary secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic cells resemble the principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: increased Na+ entry raises basolateral pumping, which drives K+ into the cell for secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical exchanger is present in the ileum but not the jejunum?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ileum has the jejunal mechanisms plus apical Cl--HCO3- exchange and a basolateral Cl- transporter. Combined with Na+-H+ exchange, this gives net NaCl absorption. Na+-H+ exchange and Na+-glucose cotransport are present in both segments. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is basolateral in crypt cells, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the ileum, HCO3- is secreted into the lumen rather than absorbed into blood (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the ileal basolateral membrane has a Cl- transporter instead of an HCO3- transporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal cells secrete chloride and water into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Villus tip enterocytes","B":"Goblet mucus cells","C":"Paneth defensin cells","D":"Enteroendocrine I cells","E":"Crypt epithelial cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Epithelial cells lining the crypts secrete fluid and electrolytes, while cells lining the villi absorb them. Goblet cells secrete mucus, Paneth cells secrete defensins, and I cells secrete CCK. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells that replace enterocytes and goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, digestive tract histology). COMLEX: normally villus cells reabsorb what crypt cells secrete (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which basolateral transporter brings chloride from the blood into chloride-secreting intestinal cells?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger protein","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"Basolateral K⁺ leak channel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crypt cells have a basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter like the one in the thick ascending limb. Cl- enters on it and exits through apical Cl- channels. Na+-glucose cotransport and Na+-H+ exchange are apical absorptive carriers, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the same three-ion cotransporter in the thick ascending limb is the target of loop diuretics (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: the Na+-K+ ATPase on the basolateral membrane keeps intracellular Na+ low for the cotransporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"How does sodium reach the lumen during intestinal chloride secretion?","choices":{"A":"Passively by the paracellular route","B":"Through apical Na⁺ channels into the lumen","C":"On the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","D":"By apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","E":"By vesicular transcytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cl- is secreted through apical channels, and Na+ follows passively by the paracellular route between cells. Water then follows the NaCl. Apical Na+ channels and Na+-H+ exchange move Na+ into cells, and the Na+-K+ ATPase is basolateral. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leaky small-intestinal tight junctions permit substantial paracellular movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon has high-resistance tight junctions that limit paracellular flow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which covalent change does cholera toxin make to the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine phosphorylation","B":"Glycosylation","C":"Ubiquitination","D":"Methylation","E":"ADP-ribosylation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The A subunit of cholera toxin catalyzes ADP-ribosylation of the αs subunit of the Gs protein. The other modifications are not the toxin's action. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the αs subunit so Gs stays GTP-bound and adenylyl cyclase stays active (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts on Gs at the basolateral side (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the direct effect of cholera toxin on the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Loss of its GTP binding","B":"Faster GDP rebinding","C":"Loss of its GTPase activity","D":"Faster receptor desensitizing","E":"Gain of Gi-type coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ADP-ribosylation inhibits the GTPase activity of αs, so GTP stays bound and the subunit stays active. GTP binding is kept, not lost. Faster GDP rebinding would end the signal, and the toxin does not change receptor desensitization or coupling. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: with GTP permanently bound, cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the secreted fluid volume can reach 10 to 12 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Why does cholera cause massive diarrhea despite intact villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Villus cells switch over to net fluid secretion","B":"Crypt secretion exceeds villus absorption","C":"Colonic water absorption is abolished","D":"Toxin destroys the villus epithelium","E":"Bacteria invade the submucosal layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholera stimulates crypt Cl- and fluid secretion so strongly that it overwhelms the absorptive capacity of villus cells and the colon. Villus cells keep absorbing, which is why glucose-coupled Na+ absorption still works. The toxin does not destroy the epithelium or cause invasion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vibrio cholerae and some Escherichia coli strains cause secretory diarrhea through toxins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon can reabsorb a maximum of about 6 to 8 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Which laxative class includes lactulose and polyethylene glycol?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic laxatives","B":"Stimulant laxatives","C":"Bulk-forming laxatives","D":"Emollient laxatives","E":"Opioid antidiarrheals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osmotic laxatives provide a nonabsorbable osmotic load that draws water into the lumen, the mechanism of osmotic diarrhea. Stimulant laxatives act on enteric nerves, bulk-forming agents are soluble fibers, emollients lower stool surface tension, and opioid agents slow motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy because gut bacteria acidify the colon and trap NH4+ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: bacteria degrade some nonabsorbed solutes into more osmotically active particles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major cause of secretory diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border lactase deficiency","B":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","C":"Nonabsorbable laxative ingestion","D":"Loss of absorptive mucosal area","E":"Enteropathic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names overgrowth of enteropathic bacteria such as Vibrio cholerae or Escherichia coli as the major cause of secretory diarrhea. Lactase deficiency and laxatives cause osmotic diarrhea, pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption, and reduced mucosal area is a separate category. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: enteritis increases mucosal secretion and motility to flush the infectious agent (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Diarrheal fluid is rich in which anion because intestinal secretions contain it?","choices":{"A":"Chloride ion","B":"Phosphate ion","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Sulfate ion","E":"Lactate ion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary, pancreatic, and intestinal juices have a high HCO3- content, so diarrheal fluid does too. Loss of HCO3- relative to Cl- causes hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate. Phosphate, sulfate, and lactate are not the named anion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal fluid is also high in K+ from flow-dependent colonic secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has a high-output jejunostomy after bowel surgery. He drinks about 2 L per day, yet stoma output is 3.5 L per day. Blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, and BUN is 31 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). Which best explains why output can exceed what he drinks?","choices":{"A":"Upper gut secretions add to dietary fluid","B":"Excess colonic secretion of potassium-rich fluid","C":"Renal water loss into the bowel","D":"Bacterial fermentation of dietary starch","E":"Stoma mucosa secreting mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"About 7 L of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, and biliary secretions joins dietary fluid each day. Normally the distal small intestine and colon reabsorb it, so losing them lets secreted fluid pour out. The colon is bypassed, the kidney does not empty into the bowel, and fermentation and mucus are minor. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a high-output stoma can cause dehydration and prerenal azotemia because proximal secretions are not reabsorbed (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion adds to luminal fluid","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man receives a hypertonic enteral feed directly into the jejunum and develops cramps and watery output. Temperature is 37.0°C, and serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which process best explains the early increase in luminal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping by crypt epithelial cells","B":"Closure of jejunal tight junctions","C":"Water entry until contents are isosmotic","D":"Toxin-driven chloride channel opening","E":"Aldosterone-driven colonic secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Water crosses the leaky jejunal epithelium osmotically, so hypertonic contents draw water into the lumen until they approach isosmolarity. Water does not move by active pumping. Jejunal tight junctions are leaky. No toxin is present, and aldosterone acts on colonic absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperosmotic luminal contents draw water into the lumen until they become isosmotic (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intestinal absorbate is isosmotic, much like proximal tubular fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal osmolality and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has an end ileostomy after colectomy. Her stoma effluent sodium stays close to her plasma sodium, whereas normal stool sodium is much lower. Blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Which feature of the small intestine best explains why it fails to lower effluent sodium as the colon would?","choices":{"A":"Absent basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","B":"Resistance of enterocytes to aldosterone","C":"High colonic potassium secretion","D":"Bile salt-driven crypt secretion","E":"Leaky low-resistance tight junctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Small-intestinal tight junctions are low resistance and permit paracellular movement, so Na+ leaks back and a steep gradient against the lumen is hard to hold. The colon has tight high-resistance junctions and aldosterone-induced Na+ channels. The Na+-K+ ATPase is present, and colonic K+ secretion is absent after colectomy. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). The effluent-sodium comparison is inferred from tight-junction resistance. Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aldosterone increases colonic Na+ absorption through apical Na+ channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of the colon removes the high-resistance segment that finishes salt and water absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman has 3 days of profuse watery diarrhea from norovirus. She is weak, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which mechanism most directly explains her low potassium?","choices":{"A":"Renal K⁺ wasting from loop diuretics","B":"Shift of K⁺ into cells from alkalosis","C":"Reduced dietary potassium intake","D":"Flow-dependent colonic K⁺ secretion","E":"Crypt-cell K⁺ secretion via channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As in renal principal cells, colonic K+ secretion depends on flow. High diarrheal flow increases K+ secretion and fecal K+ loss, causing hypokalemia. No diuretic is given, diarrhea causes acidosis rather than alkalosis, intake is not the main factor, and crypt cells secrete Cl-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypokalemia can cause muscle weakness and flattened T waves (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: aldosterone also increases colonic K+ secretion through Na+ channel synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man has hypertension and muscle cramps. Serum potassium is 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), plasma renin is suppressed, and an adrenal adenoma is found. Which change in his colonic epithelium is expected?","choices":{"A":"Reduced colonic K⁺ secretion","B":"Increased opening of crypt Cl⁻ channels by cAMP","C":"Increased Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchange activity","D":"Decreased basolateral K⁺ uptake","E":"Increased apical Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aldosterone induces synthesis of apical Na+ channels in colonocytes. Na+ absorption rises, and K+ secretion rises secondarily through increased pumping, adding to K+ loss. Decreased channel synthesis is the opposite. Crypt Cl- channels respond to cAMP agonists, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is ileal. Basolateral K+ uptake increases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and low renin (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: the colonic transport pattern mirrors the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — colonic electrolyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman takes a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor for glaucoma. Serum bicarbonate is 19 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which jejunal transport step is most directly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na⁺-glucose cotransport by SGLT1 carriers","B":"Supply of H⁺ for apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","C":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Apical Na⁺ channel insertion by aldosterone","E":"Paracellular water absorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the jejunum, carbonic anhydrase converts CO2 and H2O to H+ and HCO3-. H+ is secreted on the Na+-H+ exchanger and HCO3- is absorbed. Inhibiting the enzyme limits H+ supply for that exchange and HCO3- absorption. Na+-glucose cotransport is independent, the three-ion cotransporter is in crypt cells, and apical Na+ channels are colonic. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). The drug effect on gut is inferred from the mechanism. Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause metabolic acidosis by reducing proximal tubular HCO3- reabsorption (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: jejunal electrolyte transport is identical to that of the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase and H⁺/HCO₃⁻ generation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has abdominal distension and fails to pass meconium. Contrast enema shows a microcolon with inspissated meconium in the distal ileum. Sweat chloride later returns 78 mmol/L (reference range: <30 mmol/L). Which defect best explains the thick intestinal contents?","choices":{"A":"Impaired apical Cl⁻ secretion by crypt cells","B":"Excess Na⁺ absorption by colonic channels","C":"Loss of basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Absent Na⁺-glucose cotransport on villus enterocytes","E":"Overactive apical anion exchange"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crypt Cl- secretion exits through apical Cl- channels, the CFTR channel. Without it, Cl-, Na+, and water are not secreted, and luminal contents become dehydrated. The basolateral cotransporter is intact, and absorption by villus Na+-glucose cotransport is not the defect. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: Na+ follows secreted Cl- passively between cells, and water follows NaCl (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has 3 L per day of watery diarrhea that continues while she is fasting. Serum potassium is 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and gastric acid output is low. CT shows a pancreatic tail mass. Which mechanism best explains her diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Nonabsorbed solute holding water","B":"Bile acid stimulation of colonic epithelium","C":"Peptide-driven crypt-cell cAMP excess","D":"Mucosal invasion by bacteria","E":"Rapid transit from gastrin excess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A VIP-secreting tumor activates basolateral receptors on crypt cells, raising cAMP and opening apical Cl- channels. Na+ and water follow, causing large-volume secretory diarrhea that continues during fasting. Nonabsorbed solute would stop with fasting, no ileal disease is present, no invasion is suggested, and gastrin is not the hormone. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); First Aid Gastrointestinal (VIP). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (WDHA syndrome) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: octreotide is used to treat VIPoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — VIPoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old farmworker has vomiting, drooling, small pupils, and profuse watery diarrhea after spraying an organophosphate pesticide. Heart rate is 48/min. Plasma cholinesterase is 900 U/L (reference range: 3000–8000 U/L). Which mechanism best explains increased intestinal fluid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP release from a pancreatic islet tumor","B":"Excess acetylcholine at crypt cell receptors","C":"Toxin-mediated Gs activation in crypt cells","D":"Nonabsorbed pesticide holding water","E":"Loss of colonic Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo lists acetylcholine among the basolateral agonists that open apical Cl- channels in crypt cells. Cholinesterase inhibition lets acetylcholine build up, so Cl-, Na+, and water secretion increases. VIP excess is a tumor syndrome, ADP-ribosylation is the toxin mechanism, the pesticide is not an osmotic load, and aldosterone controls colonic channels. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: organophosphates irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase and cause a cholinergic toxidrome (First Aid Pharmacology). COMLEX: atropine blocks muscarinic effects and pralidoxime regenerates acetylcholinesterase (First Aid Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and autonomic input","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker has profuse rice-water stools 2 days after drinking untreated water. Blood pressure is 92/58 mm Hg, and stool culture grows Vibrio cholerae. Which intracellular messenger remains persistently high in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Toxin-modified αs keeps adenylyl cyclase active, so cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open. Cyclic GMP is the messenger of heat-stable E. coli toxin, not cholera. IP3 and diacylglycerol come from Gq signaling, and nitric oxide relaxes smooth muscle. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin causes secretory diarrhea by permanently activating Gs (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous replacement of fluid and electrolytes is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Gs/cAMP receptor signaling pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman returns from Mexico with 4 days of watery diarrhea from toxigenic Escherichia coli. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), chloride 114 mEq/L (reference range: 98–106 mEq/L), and bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is most likely?","choices":{"A":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis","B":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","C":"Acute respiratory acidosis","D":"Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","E":"Acute respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretions lost in diarrhea are rich in HCO3-, so HCO3- falls relative to Cl-. The anion gap is 138 − (114 + 15) = 9, normal, which fits hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. A high gap would need unmeasured acids. Alkalosis follows vomiting, and respiratory disorders are not primary. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea is a classic cause of normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Escherichia coli enterotoxins cause secretory diarrhea like cholera toxin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy in a flood-relief camp has had 12 watery stools in a day. He is lethargic with sunken eyes. Heart rate is 168/min, and blood pressure is 70/40 mm Hg. Which consequence of his secretory diarrhea most directly threatens his life?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemic cardiac arrhythmia","B":"Loss of extracellular fluid volume","C":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis from vomiting","D":"Bacteremic septic shock","E":"Hypernatremic seizures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diarrhea loses extracellular-type fluid, decreasing ECF and intravascular volume and arterial pressure. Baroreceptor and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone responses fail if the loss is too great or too fast, leading to circulatory collapse. K+ is lost rather than gained, the acidosis is not alkalosis, and toxin-mediated diarrhea is not invasive. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without fluid replacement, up to 50 percent of patients with cholera die (Guyton Ch. 67). COMLEX: antibiotics can shorten the duration and volume of cholera diarrhea (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man starts milk of magnesia daily for constipation and develops watery diarrhea. It stops within a day when he is kept nil by mouth for a procedure. Serum magnesium is 2.6 mg/dL (reference range: 1.7–2.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Peptide-driven crypt Cl⁻ secretion","B":"Toxin-driven Gs activation in crypt cells","C":"Inflammatory loss of villus absorptive surface","D":"Unabsorbed cations retaining luminal water","E":"Bile acid stimulation of colonocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Poorly absorbed magnesium stays in the lumen and holds water, an osmotic diarrhea that stops when the solute is stopped. Peptide-driven or toxin-driven crypt secretion persists during fasting. Villous injury and bile acid diarrhea do not fit a laxative that stopped. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic laxatives such as magnesium salts may be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: secretory diarrhea results from excess crypt-cell secretion rather than inadequate absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — lactase deficiency as osmotic diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old girl has 4 days of vomiting and watery diarrhea during a rotavirus outbreak. Biopsy in a similar case shows blunted villi. Heart rate is 150/min, and capillary refill is 3 seconds. Which category of diarrhea best describes her primary mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Toxin-driven crypt secretion","B":"Peptide hormone-driven secretion","C":"Nonabsorbable osmotic laxative load","D":"Aldosterone-driven colonic K⁺ loss","E":"Decreased absorptive surface area"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo classifies diarrhea into decreased absorptive surface area, osmotic, and secretory types. Infection and inflammation of the small intestine reduce the surface that absorbs fluid. Toxin-driven and hormone-driven crypt secretion are secretory types, and laxatives are osmotic. Aldosterone does not cause diarrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus is a leading cause of watery diarrhea in young children and is preventable by vaccine (First Aid Microbiology). COMLEX: viral injury to villus tips can cause secondary lactase deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl28_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had 5 days of profuse watery diarrhea. She feels dizzy on standing. Blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg, and urine sodium is 8 mEq/L (reference range: 40–220 mEq/L). Which compensatory response is most likely active?","choices":{"A":"Suppressed antidiuretic hormone release","B":"Increased atrial natriuretic peptide","C":"Activated renin-angiotensin-aldosterone","D":"Reduced sympathetic outflow","E":"Suppressed baroreceptor reflex activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure. Baroreceptors increase sympathetic outflow, and the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system is activated to retain Na+, giving low urine sodium. ADH increases rather than falls, ANP falls with low volume, and sympathetic and baroreceptor responses increase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low urine sodium in hypovolemia reflects avid renal sodium retention (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: compensation fails if diarrheal fluid loss is too large or too rapid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with an ileostomy asks which bowel segment normally has the tightest epithelial junctions. Which segment has high-resistance tight junctions?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Jejunum","C":"Colon","D":"Ileum","E":"Stomach"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic tight junctions are tight, with high resistance, and do not permit paracellular movement. Small-intestinal tight junctions are leaky and permit substantial paracellular flow. The stomach is not described in this comparison. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic transport resembles that of renal principal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: tight-junction permeability decides whether fluid moves by cellular or paracellular routes (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has extensive Crohn disease of the small bowel. Which small-intestinal segment is normally the major site of Na⁺ absorption?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Ileum","C":"Cecum","D":"Rectum","E":"Jejunum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names the jejunum as the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine, through Na+-coupled cotransporters and Na+-H+ exchange. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange. The cecum and rectum are colon. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease can involve any part of the GI tract, most often the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: Na+ entering jejunal cells is pumped out by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis has recurrent distal intestinal obstruction. Which protein is defective in his intestinal crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"CFTR chloride channel","B":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","C":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","D":"Epithelial Na⁺ channel","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CFTR is the apical Cl- channel through which crypt cells secrete Cl-, with Na+ and water following. The basolateral three-ion cotransporter, Na+-H+ exchange, and epithelial Na+ channels are intact, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without crypt Cl- secretion, Na+ and water do not follow into the lumen, so contents become dehydrated (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cAMP opens apical Cl- channels in crypt cells (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has severe rice-water diarrhea from Vibrio cholerae. Which enzyme stays persistently active in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Guanylyl cyclase","B":"Phospholipase C","C":"Protein kinase C","D":"Adenylyl cyclase","E":"Carbonic anhydrase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates αs, so Gs keeps adenylyl cyclase active and cAMP stays high. Guanylyl cyclase is targeted by heat-stable E. coli toxin, phospholipase C and protein kinase C belong to Gq signaling, and carbonic anhydrase supplies H+ and HCO3-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Secretory diarrhea, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates αs, keeping Gs active and cAMP high (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cholera can secrete 10 to 12 L per day into the intestine (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor causing large-volume watery diarrhea despite fasting. Which drug reduces the tumor's hormone secretion?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Lactulose","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Psyllium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide is a long-acting somatostatin analog used for VIPoma that inhibits secretion of splanchnic vasodilatory hormones. Loperamide slows motility, lactulose is an osmotic laxative, bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative, and psyllium is bulk-forming. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (octreotide); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is also used for acute variceal bleeds, acromegaly, and carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide). COMLEX: octreotide can cause steatorrhea and cholelithiasis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 22-year-old woman eats a normal diet. About how much water does she lose in feces each day?","choices":{"A":"About 100–200 mL","B":"About 1–2 L","C":"About 2–3 L","D":"About 500–800 mL","E":"About 5–10 mL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Of slightly more than 9 L that enters the lumen daily, almost all is absorbed by the small intestine and colon, leaving about 100–200 mL in feces. Losses of liters per day mean diarrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport, Fig. 8.34). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea can lose 9 L or more of fluid per day (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 2 L of daily luminal fluid comes from the diet (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"}],"29":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of mechanical intestinal obstructions are caused by the four major mechanical causes combined?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 95 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus together cause about 80% of mechanical obstructions. Tumors, infarction, and strictures such as Crohn disease make up the rest. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 29 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is most commonly caused by intraperitoneal adhesions, tumors, and hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: upright abdominal x-ray in small bowel obstruction shows air-fluid levels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide?","choices":{"A":"Adhesions","B":"Volvulus","C":"Tumors","D":"Hernias","E":"Strictures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hernias are the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide and the third most common in the United States. Inguinal hernias, with narrow orifices and large sacs, are most often involved. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: femoral hernias protrude below the inguinal ligament and carry a greater risk of strangulation than inguinal hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias). COMLEX: acquired hernias are found in up to 5% of the population (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the segment of bowel that telescopes into the distal bowel in intussusception called?","choices":{"A":"Intussusceptum","B":"Intussuscipiens","C":"Hernia sac","D":"Mesenteric root","E":"Closed loop"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In intussusception, a segment constricted by a peristaltic wave (the intussusceptum) telescopes into the immediately distal segment (the intussuscipiens). Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception most commonly occurs at the ileocecal junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: ultrasound or CT of intussusception may show a target sign (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"What causes most acute arterial obstruction of the mesenteric circulation?","choices":{"A":"Systemic vasculitis","B":"Mesenteric venous clot","C":"Tumor compression","D":"Thrombosis or embolism","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In a large majority of cases, acute mesenteric obstruction is caused by thrombosis or embolism. Severe atherosclerosis is the main risk for thrombosis, and emboli come mostly from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acute mesenteric ischemia include atrial fibrillation, peripheral arterial disease, recent MI, and CHF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: mesenteric venous thrombosis is an uncommon cause of ischemic bowel disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal component is most vulnerable to ischemic injury?","choices":{"A":"Surface epithelium","B":"Crypt stem cells","C":"Basal crypt cells","D":"Muscularis propria","E":"Myenteric neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Capillaries run from crypt to surface before a hairpin turn, so the surface epithelium is most vulnerable while crypt stem cells are protected. Surface atrophy with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the signature of ischemia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia causes postprandial epigastric pain, food aversion, and weight loss (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: chronic intestinal ischemia is accompanied by fibrous scarring of the lamina propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"In which age group is ischemic disease of the colon most common?","choices":{"A":"Younger than 2 years","B":"Between 20 and 30","C":"Older than 70 years","D":"Between 30 and 40","E":"Between 40 and 50"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic ischemia is most common in patients older than 70 years and is slightly more common in females. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia shows a thumbprint sign on imaging from mucosal edema and hemorrhage (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: mortality in the first 30 days of colonic ischemia is about 10% with appropriate management (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is angiodysplasia most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum region","B":"Cecum or right colon","C":"Proximal jejunal loops","D":"Sigmoid colon segment","E":"Lower rectum and anus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon and usually comes to attention after the sixth decade. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia presents with hematochezia from tortuous dilated vessels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia bleeding ranges from chronic and intermittent to acute and massive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which test confirms angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Barium swallow","B":"Stool culture","C":"Plain radiograph","D":"Angiography","E":"Breath testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Visceral or aortic angiography (with colonoscopy) is the diagnostic test for angiodysplasia. The lesion consists of malformed, dilated, thin-walled submucosal and mucosal vessels. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is more common in older patients (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia has a prevalence of less than 1% in adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of major lower intestinal bleeding episodes in older people is due to angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"About 1 percent","B":"About 5 percent","C":"About 20 percent","D":"About 50 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Although its prevalence is less than 1% in adults, angiodysplasia causes about 20% of major lower intestinal bleeding episodes in older populations. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lower GI bleeding causes include diverticulosis, angiodysplasia, and hemorrhoids (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI bleeding). COMLEX: angiodysplastic channels are separated from the lumen only by the thin vessel wall and attenuated epithelium (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What share of patients with cystic fibrosis eventually develop exocrine pancreatic insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Under 1 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 50 percent","E":"Over 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In cystic fibrosis, intraductal pancreatic concretions cause duct obstruction, chronic pancreatitis, and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in more than 80% of patients. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Cystic fibrosis). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and vitamins A, D, E, and K as well as vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: CF pancreatic concretions can begin forming in utero (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"IgG antigliadin antibodies","B":"IgA anti-TG2 antibodies","C":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies","D":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","E":"Perinuclear ANCA antibodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most sensitive noninvasive test for celiac disease is IgA against tissue transglutaminase 2. IgG anti-TG2 can be used in patients with IgA deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac serology also includes anti-endomysial and anti-deamidated gliadin peptide antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease shows an abnormal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cancer in people with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Gastric diffuse large B-cell lymphoma tumor","B":"Distal esophageal Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma of the liver","E":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma, an aggressive lymphoma of intraepithelial T cells. Small intestinal adenocarcinoma is also more frequent. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with dermatitis herpetiformis and decreased bone density (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: a gluten-free diet may reduce celiac complications including anemia, female infertility, and osteoporosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Mutation of which gene causes IPEX?","choices":{"A":"CFTR","B":"MYO5B","C":"HLA-DQ2","D":"RET","E":"FOXP3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IPEX (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linkage) is caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked FOXP3 gene. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvillus inclusion disease is caused by MYO5B mutations and presents with intractable diarrhea in early infancy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: autoimmune enteropathy is treated with immunosuppressants such as cyclosporine or with stem cell transplantation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which defective molecule causes abetalipoproteinemia?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis transmembrane regulator","B":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein","C":"Sodium-glucose linked transporter protein","D":"Low-density lipoprotein receptor protein","E":"Apolipoprotein E lipid carrier protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Abetalipoproteinemia is an autosomal recessive defect in microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP), which transfers lipids to nascent apolipoprotein B in the ER. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia causes acanthocytes on the peripheral smear (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia presents in infancy with failure to thrive, diarrhea, and steatorrhea (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"About how many children worldwide are estimated to be affected by the enteropathy linked to poor sanitation and chronic fecal pathogen exposure?","choices":{"A":"Over 150 million","B":"Around 1 million","C":"Around 10 million","D":"Around 25 million","E":"Around 50 million"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Environmental enteric dysfunction is estimated to affect more than 150 million children worldwide and may contribute to many childhood deaths. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tropical sprue responds to antibiotics and is associated with folate and later B12 deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, tropical sprue). COMLEX: high oral vaccine failure rates where EED is endemic suggest a mucosal immune defect (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old boy has intermittent screaming episodes with his legs drawn up and red currant-jelly stools after a recent viral illness. A sausage-shaped right-sided mass is felt. What most likely served as the leading edge?","choices":{"A":"Intraluminal adenocarcinoma","B":"Inspissated meconium plug","C":"Fibrous adhesion band","D":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches","E":"Incarcerated hernia sac"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intussusception is the most common cause of obstruction under age 2. Idiopathic cases follow viral infection or rotavirus vaccine, probably because reactive Peyer patch hyperplasia forms the leading edge. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25); First Aid Gastrointestinal (intussusception). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in idiopathic intussusception of young children, contrast or air enemas are both diagnostic and therapeutic (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum and bowel wall hematoma from IgA vasculitis are pediatric intussusception lead points (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman who had a hysterectomy years ago has abdominal pain, distention, and vomiting. She has no external hernia, and x-ray shows air-fluid levels. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Twisting of the sigmoid colon","B":"Postoperative fibrous bands","C":"Ileocecal segment telescoping","D":"Inguinal sac herniation of bowel","E":"Gallstone lodged at the valve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgery, infection, or endometriosis can produce fibrous adhesions that create closed loops. Other viscera slide through and become trapped as an internal hernia, with obstruction and strangulation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adhesions); First Aid Gastrointestinal (small bowel obstruction). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is managed with GI decompression, volume resuscitation, and bowel rest (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: adhesions can follow peritoneal inflammation such as endometriosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man in a nursing home with chronic constipation has sudden abdominal distention. Abdominal x-ray shows a coffee-bean sign. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Midgut volvulus","B":"Cecal carcinoma","C":"Sigmoid volvulus","D":"Colonic ileus","E":"Ischemic colitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volvulus most often involves large redundant sigmoid loops, followed by the cecum. Sigmoid volvulus is more common in older adults and shows a coffee-bean sign. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Volvulus); First Aid Gastrointestinal (volvulus). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric volvulus in adults is more common with paraesophageal hernia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: because it is rare, volvulus can be overlooked clinically with catastrophic results (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe abdominal pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen on exam. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Embolic occlusion of the SMA","B":"Thrombosis of a mesenteric vein","C":"Twisting of the sigmoid mesentery","D":"Vasculitis of small serosal arteries","E":"Rupture of a cecal vascular ectasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acute mesenteric ischemia is a critical blockage of intestinal flow, often embolic SMA occlusion, with pain out of proportion to exam. Emboli commonly come from cardiac mural thrombi. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease); First Aid Gastrointestinal (acute mesenteric ischemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute arterial infarction produces a sharp demarcation between normal and ischemic bowel (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: coagulative necrosis of the muscularis propria develops within 1 to 4 days of infarction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman recovering from septic shock has crampy left lower abdominal pain, then bloody diarrhea. A colonic biopsy is taken. Which finding best supports ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas scattered in lamina propria","B":"Crypt abscesses in diffuse colitis","C":"Villous blunting with lymphocytes","D":"Upper mucosa lost but crypts spared","E":"Thickened subepithelial collagen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ischemic injury progresses from the lumen downward because crypts receive blood first. Atrophy or sloughing of the upper mucosa with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the morphologic signature. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia commonly occurs at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: neutrophils are recruited into ischemic mucosa within hours of reperfusion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has ischemic colitis confined to the right colon. Why does this location carry a worse prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The right colon wall is especially thin","B":"The right colon lacks a submucosal layer","C":"The right colon has no collateral arcades","D":"Bacteria are far denser in the right colon","E":"Its artery also feeds the small intestine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mortality is doubled in right-sided colonic ischemia. The right colon is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, which also supplies much of the small intestine, so right-sided disease may signal wider compromise. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia most commonly involves the SMA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: COPD and symptoms lasting more than 2 weeks are poor prognostic signs in colonic ischemia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with aortic stenosis has recurrent painless hematochezia and iron deficiency. Colonoscopy finds small flat red mucosal lesions. What will histology most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Glands showing high-grade dysplasia","B":"Granulation tissue in an ulcer bed","C":"Ectatic nests of thin-walled vessels","D":"Mucosal outpouching through the muscle","E":"Noncaseating granulomas in the mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angiodysplasia consists of ectatic nests of tortuous veins, venules, and capillaries, separated from the lumen only by a thin wall and attenuated epithelium. Minor injury makes them bleed. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia); First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: angiodysplasia usually comes to clinical attention after the sixth decade (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has bleeding from colonic angiodysplasia. Which mechanical factor is thought to explain its typical location?","choices":{"A":"Lowest blood flow at a watershed zone","B":"Thinnest muscularis of any colonic part","C":"Highest bacterial count in the colon lumen","D":"Largest diameter gives most wall tension","E":"Most lymphoid tissue in the large bowel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because the cecum has the largest diameter of any colonic segment, it develops the greatest wall tension. This may explain the cecal and right-colon predominance of angiodysplasia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia in older patients is associated with aortic stenosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: the pathogenesis of angiodysplasia has been attributed to mechanical and congenital factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has recurrent hematochezia from colonic angiodysplasia. Which of his conditions is classically associated with this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus","B":"End-stage renal disease","C":"Primary hypothyroidism","D":"Gouty arthritis history","E":"Chronic obstructive lung"},"correct":"B","explanation":"First Aid associates angiodysplasia with end-stage renal disease, von Willebrand disease, and aortic stenosis. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is confirmed by angiography (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: normal gut distention may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has bloating, iron deficiency anemia, and an itchy blistering rash on her elbows. A biopsy of the second part of the duodenum is taken. What will it most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lymphocytosis with villous atrophy","B":"Vacuolated enterocytes filled with lipid","C":"Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina","D":"Transmural granulomas with deep fissuring","E":"Normal villi without inflammatory cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Celiac disease shows increased intraepithelial CD8+ T cells, crypt hyperplasia, and villous atrophy, best seen in the second part of the duodenum or proximal jejunum. Dermatitis herpetiformis occurs in up to 10% of patients. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease); First Aid Gastrointestinal (celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease in adults most commonly presents between ages 30 and 60 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man with chronic diarrhea has suspected gluten-sensitive enteropathy, and HLA-DQ typing is ordered. How is this genetic result most useful?","choices":{"A":"Positive result confirms the diagnosis","B":"Result predicts the lymphoma risk","C":"Result sets the diet duration needed","D":"Result replaces the need for biopsy","E":"Negative result argues against it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HLA-DQ2 is present in about 90% of patients, with DQ8 in most of the rest. Their absence has negative predictive value, but their presence does not confirm the diagnosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac concordance in monozygotic twins is about 75% (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease is associated with type 1 diabetes, thyroiditis, Sjögren syndrome, and IgA nephropathy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has bulky greasy stools and poor weight gain. Which phase of nutrient absorption is primarily impaired?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border digestion","B":"Intraluminal digestion","C":"Transepithelial transport","D":"Lymphatic lipid transport","E":"Mucosal nutrient uptake"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, which impairs the intraluminal phase of absorption. Oral enzyme supplements treat it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Cystic fibrosis); Robbins Ch. 17 (Malabsorption and diarrhea, Table 17.6). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: malabsorption can disturb intraluminal digestion, terminal digestion, transepithelial transport, or lymphatic transport (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old girl has steatorrhea and failure to thrive. Her blood smear shows acanthocytes, and plasma lacks apoB-containing lipoproteins. What will a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes","B":"Flattened villi with lymphocytes","C":"Neutrophils within the crypts","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages","E":"Absent ganglion cell clusters"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In abetalipoproteinemia, lipids are not assembled into lipoproteins and accumulate in enterocytes. The triglyceride shows as epithelial vacuolization, highlighted by oil red O after a fatty meal. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure to absorb essential fatty acids in abetalipoproteinemia causes fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia is autosomal recessive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has severe persistent diarrhea, type 1 diabetes, and eczema. His maternal uncle died in infancy with similar findings. Which cell type is defective?","choices":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells","B":"Mature B lymphocytes","C":"CD4+ regulatory T cells","D":"Intestinal goblet cells","E":"Small-bowel enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IPEX is an X-linked autoimmune enteropathy in which loss of FOXP3, a transcription factor of CD4+ regulatory T cells, impairs their development and function. Neutrophils often infiltrate the mucosa, unlike celiac disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoantibodies to enterocytes and goblet cells are common in autoimmune enteropathy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: intraepithelial lymphocytes in autoimmune enteropathy increase less than in celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl from a rural community with poor sanitation in Zambia has stunted growth and malabsorption. No single pathogen is found, and antibiotics do not help. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Classic gluten-sensitive enteropathy","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy","E":"Environmental enteric dysfunction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Environmental enteric dysfunction occurs where sanitation is poor. It likely reflects chronic fecal pathogen exposure and repeated early diarrhea, no single agent is implicated, and antibiotics and supplements do not reverse it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biopsies in environmental enteric dysfunction show more immune cell infiltration and less villous atrophy than advanced celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: stunting from environmental enteric dysfunction may be accompanied by uncorrectable cognitive deficits (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a painful, irreducible inguinal bulge. At the neck of the sac, what is compromised first?","choices":{"A":"Arterial inflow","B":"Lymph drainage","C":"Vagal supply","D":"Peritoneal fluid","E":"Venous drainage"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pressure at the neck of a hernia sac first impairs venous drainage. Stasis and edema then cause incarceration, then arterial and venous compromise (strangulation) and infarction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias, Fig. 17.26). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inguinal hernias tend to have narrow orifices and large sacs (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: complicated hernias can present with tenderness, erythema, and fever (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has her SMA blood flow restored after mesenteric thrombosis. When does most bowel damage occur?","choices":{"A":"Before the clot","B":"During reperfusion","C":"At first hypoxia","D":"After the scarring","E":"During fasting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ischemic injury occurs in two phases: initial hypoxic injury, then reperfusion, when blood supply returns and the greatest damage occurs. Bacterial products, free radicals, and neutrophils contribute. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emboli causing mesenteric occlusion most often arise from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: necrotizing enterocolitis can cause pneumatosis intestinalis and portal venous gas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, necrotizing enterocolitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has bleeding from colonic vascular ectasias. What process likely produced them?","choices":{"A":"Repeated vein occlusion","B":"Portal hypertension","C":"Inherited telangiectasia","D":"Radiation damage","E":"Embolic infarction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal distention and contraction may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria. This leads to focal dilation and tortuosity of overlying vessels. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is associated with von Willebrand disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplastic vessels are vulnerable to disruption by even minor injury (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with celiac disease has new pain and weight loss despite a strict gluten-free diet. What should be suspected?","choices":{"A":"Lactose overload","B":"Dietary relapse","C":"Bile salt excess","D":"Refractory sprue","E":"Viral enteritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pain, diarrhea, and weight loss despite a strict gluten-free diet raise concern for refractory sprue, a possible precursor of T-cell lymphoma, or for cancer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a gluten-free diet usually restores near-normal histology within 6 to 24 months (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease primarily affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old Chinese man has bloating and flatulence after milk. What will a duodenal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flattened villi","B":"Lipid vacuoles","C":"Unremarkable mucosa","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Foamy macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acquired lactose intolerance comes from age-related downregulation of gene expression. Because the defect is biochemical, histology is generally unremarkable. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Lactase deficiency). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lactose hydrogen breath test is positive if breath hydrogen rises more than 20 ppm over baseline (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: congenital lactose intolerance is a rare autosomal recessive disorder (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has intermittent obstruction from ileal intussusception. What most likely started it?","choices":{"A":"A mass lead point","B":"Bowel wall edema","C":"Recent rotavirus","D":"Midgut rotation","E":"Thin stool water"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In older children and adults, intussusception is usually caused by an intraluminal mass or tumor acting as the traction point, and surgery is needed. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intussusception). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception causes intermittent pain, vomiting, and bloody currant-jelly stools (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: up to 1% of patients with cystic fibrosis develop intussusception (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — segmentation versus peristalsis","isHighYield":true}]};
